parts:
- text: "I'll open a second slot with an architectural variant that keeps training defaults but replaces the LSTM with a spatial ConvLSTM to better preserve and update spatial memory over time. This isolates the architectural effect while maintaining the same algorithm and hyperparameters.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Residual CNN + ConvLSTM (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convlstm, rnn, cnn, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"We keep the default policy-gradient algorithm and hyperparameters but replace the temporal LSTM with a spatial ConvLSTM on top of the residual CNN. This introduces spatially-structured memory for planning without Sokoban-specific heuristics. Architecture-only change to isolate effect.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Residual CNN backbone + ConvLSTM temporal memory with default training step and optimizer.\n  Track: Architecture (algorithm unchanged).\n  \"\"\"\n\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Fixed base seed for reproducibility\n  BASE_SEED = 44\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Match defaults to isolate architecture; expose conv_lstm_features\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          # carry: (h, c), each (B, H, W, C)\n          h, c = carry\n          # Concatenate along channels and apply a single conv to produce all gates\n          gates = nn.Conv(\n              features=4 * self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n\
    \          )(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Residual CNN backbone followed by spatial ConvLSTM memory.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          # x: (B, 8, 8, 8), done: (B,)\n          B = x.shape[0]\n\n          # Residual CNN backbone (parity with default)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n\n          # Initialize ConvLSTM state if needed\n          H = W = 8\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              h = jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              c = jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h':\
    \ h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(B, 1, 1, 1).astype(x.dtype)\n              keep = 1.0 - done_mask\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * keep,\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * keep,\n              }\n\n          # Spatial ConvLSTM update\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCell(self.conv_lstm_features)( (rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x )\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Head: flatten spatial memory and apply MLP for policy/value\n          z = h_out.reshape((B, -1))  # (B, 8*8*features)\n          z = nn.Dense(features=256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n\n          # Separate small heads (similar capacity to defaults)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(features=64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits\
    \ = nn.Dense(features=4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n\n          val_h = nn.Dense(features=64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CNNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n      )\n\n  # Use default training_step and optimizer\n```\n"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating next steps for experiments**


  I need to figure out our next steps for the experiments. We have experiment ID 5 running, which involves PPO with grad clipping and GAE tuning. Submission 3 got a mean of 36.2, which is lower than the RNN baseline of 40.8. Since we can only run one more experiment concurrently, we should strategically choose how to explore potentially better algorithm improvements. Ideas could include planning inductive biases like gated residual conv networks or memory inclusion, but we have some constraints to work within.**Assessing algorithm improvements**


  I’m considering whether off-policy algorithm improvements could be feasible, even though the environment uses on-policy collection. I think implementing a clipped surrogate with multiple epochs of mini-batching might work. The training loop currently collects just one unroll per update, but I can modify the training_step function to perform additional epochs over the collected trajectory. This function handles the entire trajectory, allowing for multiple SGD epochs. I just need to be cautious about memory constraints while using JIT compilation to ensure efficiency.**Proposing multi-epoch PPO**


  I’m reflecting on our previous PPO setup, which used only one update step instead of multi-epoch. I want to consider implementing PPO with multiple epochs and mini-batch sampling. However, I need to think about how to achieve randomness inside JAX without inconsistent results. I could use a deterministic shuffle with a seeded PRNG, which might work fine. Alternatively, I might opt for a full-batch PPO approach to run multiple passes over the entire dataset, using something like fori_loop for K epochs, say four, which could enhance performance. Adding a KL penalty or early stopping could also be beneficial here.**Exploring KL and reward adjustments**


  I’m looking into whether I can compute an approximate KL divergence within JAX and possibly use clip adjustments. However, adjusting across updates is tricky, though I could consider a trust-region approach. Another idea is to add an intrinsic curiosity reward to encourage exploration, but that modifies the reward function. Given our batch setup with observations, actions, rewards, and dones, I wonder if I can augment rewards with an auxiliary prediction error. I also need to consider how to deduce the next observation from the current trajectory data, which doesn''t store next_observations directly.**Planning the next submission**


  I’m reviewing our approach and noting that ''observations'' stored represent pre-step observations, meaning the next observation at time t corresponds to t+1. For ICM, which requires the next observation’s predictions, implementing it in training_step may become complex. I’m considering adjusting the architecture to maintain stability while potentially upgrading things like the entropy coefficient schedule or learning rate. In our next submission, I’ll propose using a fixed CNN-LSTM architecture with multi-epoch mini-batch PPO, incorporating KL penalties, and prepare the third submission with a suitable title and abstract.**Implementing mini-batching and updates**


  I need to add a fori_loop within the training_step to update parameters across multiple inner steps. While creating the AdamW optimizer, I need to ensure it accommodates weight decay, but the signature currently only accepts learning rate as a float. I might have to implement L2 regularization instead. For mini-batching, I’ll flatten the batch and compute sizes accordingly, ensuring to maintain sequence continuity for RNN states. I’m planning to use environment-level mini-batches, which means sampling subsets of environments for efficient computation. This method should help optimize updates while preserving the sequential integrity necessary for RNNs.**Computing updates and losses**


  I need to gather observations, actions, and values for selected environment indices. Using the RNN approach, I''ll compute outputs and calculate the new log probabilities, policy loss, value loss, and entropy. For L2 regularization, I want to sum the squares of parameters but need to avoid overcounting, so I''ll scale it based on the number of mini-batches. I''ll also compute an approximate KL penalty since reconstructing old probabilities isn''t feasible. Each epoch should adapt the loss, maintaining stability during updates. I’ll ensure proper batching in JAX while keeping compute costs in mind to avoid timeouts.**Analyzing training step dynamics**


  The default training step involves a costly forward pass through the entire batch using sequential RNN loops. While implementing multi-epoch training across environment subsets can reduce per-iteration time, it still increases overall compute. I have to consider that earlier runs achieved around 39062 updates in about 800 seconds, meaning each update is roughly 0.02 seconds, so doing multiple passes might still be feasible within the time limit. However, increasing updates could potentially lead to fewer environment interactions, which may hurt performance. I''m also considering architecture improvements like a ConvLSTM that utilizes spatial features over time, which might enhance planning capabilities.**Proposing ConvLSTM architecture**


  I’m considering an architecture improvement by implementing a "ConvLSTM Residual CNN" flow while keeping the default policy gradient training step. Since guidelines suggest focusing on one track, I believe using this custom architecture can help manage compute limits while experimenting. I need to define hyperparameters for the ConvLSTM features. I’ll implement the ConvLSTM policy with the necessary layers and ensure the shapes align correctly. Additionally, I plan to add features for tuning the hyperparameters while maintaining some defaults for stability. This new setup should allow for both exploration and adherence to good research practices.**Crafting experiment message**


  I need to make sure I don''t exceed the concurrency limit since we have two experiments running: one with five iterations and this new one. It''s important to propose the code correctly. I also want to avoid YAML special characters that might cause issues, like colons in the title, so I’ll exclude those. I’m focusing on creating the final message, including the action command and the YAML block for clarity. It''s all about getting it just right!'
