- text: "The ConvLSTM architecture with default PG is a strong improvement (52.7 mean solve rate). I’ll 1) save the ConvLSTM network to our lineage storage for reuse, 2) re-run the default CNN+PG baseline under our author ID to meet paper requirements, and 3) open Private Memory to start documenting results.\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          # carry: (h, c), each (B, H, W, C); x: (B, H, W, Cx)\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(\n              features=4 * self.features, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates,\
    \ 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Residual CNN backbone followed by spatial ConvLSTM memory.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          # x: (B, 8, 8, 8), done: (B,)\n          B = x.shape[0]\n\
    \n          # Residual CNN backbone (parity with default)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n\n          # Initialize ConvLSTM state if needed\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              h = jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              c = jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              mask = (1.0 - done.reshape(B, 1, 1, 1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * mask,\
    \ 'c': rnn_state['c'] * mask}\n\n          # Spatial ConvLSTM update\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCell(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Heads\n          z = h_out.reshape((B, -1))\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  __all__ = [\"ConvLSTMCell\", \"ResidualBlock\", \"CNNConvLSTM\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/__init__.py}\n```yaml\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  from .cnn_convlstm import ConvLSTMCell, ResidualBlock, CNNConvLSTM\n  __all__ = [\"ConvLSTMCell\", \"ResidualBlock\", \"CNNConvLSTM\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline Re-run: Default CNN + Policy Gradient\"\ntags: \"baseline, cnn, policy gradient, re-run\"\nabstract: \"We replicate the system’s DefaultResidualCNN with the default policy-gradient training to produce our own baseline evaluation ID for fair comparison in our paper. No architectural or algorithmic changes.\"\ncontent: |\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      from defaults import DefaultResidualCNN\n      return DefaultResidualCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{help private_memory}"
thinking_content: "**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m looking to outline our next steps after achieving a strong result with the ConvLSTM architecture and default PG, reaching a mean solve of 52.7. This really surpasses our RNN baseline of 40.8, which feels like a breakthrough! \n\nNow, I need to document everything in the Private Memory Room, noting our experiment results, hypotheses, and future plans. I should summarize our state-of-the-art results with key details like configuration, code, results per seed, and training curves. We have test-level solves, and I should include the logs as well.**Evaluating improvements for reliability**\n\nI think we need to evaluate additional modifications and enhancements to boost reliability and produce a paper. For instance, I could compare the features count of ConvLSTM and run tests with and without PG versus PPO. Even though it might complicate the analysis by mixing architectures and algorithms, I can conduct separate experiments. \n\nCurrently,\
  \ we have two baseline experiments plus three of our own, totaling five, but we still need to run the baselines ourselves as they were system-run defaults. This means I might need to replicate those using our submission.**Planning baseline re-evaluation and experiments**\n\nI can create our own evaluation ID by re-running the RNN LSTM PG baseline using the same architecture and default training steps. It’s allowed to copy the system's implementation since we can read their code from the Evaluation 2 block. I should include a `create_network` function and keep the hyperparameters as defaults. By submitting to \"replicate RNN baseline\" and \"replicate CNN baseline,\" I can add two experiments.\n\nAlso, I’m considering additional variations, like testing different ConvLSTM feature sizes, possibly incorporating a small self-attention spatial aggregator. I can focus on preserving the algorithm and expanding my hyperparameter search for `conv_lstm_features` to include options among 32, 64,\
  \ and 96.**Planning ablations and submission details**\n\nThe system allows for tuning choices, but I previously set `conv_lstm_features` to 64 to focus on its effect. Now, I can run an ablation with values 32 and 128 by making separate submissions. Since each submission uses Ray sampling across four seeds and hyperparameters, it’s better to keep `conv_lstm_features` fixed for each submission to ensure controlled ablation.\n\nSo, I’ll submit two more experiments: one with `conv_lstm_features` set to 32 and another to 128. I could also explore adding gating or residual connections, and think about testing \"frame stacking\" or \"time as a channel.\" Additionally, I need to write a paper outline in Private Memory, and maybe consider publishing it in the Archive Room.**Planning module storage and actions**\n\nI need to create code for `ConvLSTM` and save it in `zephyr/modules/convlstm.py` and `zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm.py`. In each module, I’ll include the same code as in previous submissions\
  \ to facilitate reuse while keeping things concise.\n\nNext, I’ll write the necessary actions: First, I’ll store the `ConvLSTM` code, then submit a replication of the baseline using `Default CNN + PG`. After that, I’ll access Private Memory and help to summarize results with evaluation IDs. I’ll ensure I execute each step carefully, and also consider whether to queue the baseline re-run.**Writing module and preparing submission**\n\nI need to write the content for `cnn_convlstm.py`, which will include the imports for JAX and Flax, along with the `ConvLSTMCell`, `ResidualBlock`, and `CNNConvLSTM` classes, plus the `__all__` list for imports. \n\nFor the baseline re-run submission, I’ll title it “Baseline Re-run: Default CNN + PG (our run)”, tagging it with \"baseline, cnn, policy gradient, re-run.\" The abstract will summarize that we’re replicating the system baseline while keeping it concise, under 100 words. Let's proceed with that!"
