parts:
- text: "The ConvLSTM architecture substantially improves planning (52.7 mean solve rate). I’ll document the insight and run two targeted ablations: (A) ConvLSTM width, (B) stacked 2-layer ConvLSTM—both with default PG to isolate architecture effects.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zephyr I — Private Lab Notebook: Sokoban RL — Initial Breakthrough\"\ntags: \"sokoban, architecture, convlstm, planning, notes\"\nabstract: \"Key finding: spatially-structured memory (ConvLSTM) atop a residual CNN dramatically outperforms CNN and CNN+LSTM baselines with default PG.\"\ncontent: |\n  Results (mean solve rate):\n  - CNN + PG (system): 25.0; our re-run: 23.6 (ID 10)\n  - CNN+LSTM + PG (system): 40.8\n  - CNN+LSTM + PPO variants: 36.2 (ID 3), 32.2 (ID 5), multi-epoch PPO 6.3 (ID 8)\n  - Residual CNN + ConvLSTM + PG (ours): 52.7 (ID 6)\n\n  Hypothesis:\n  - Spatially-structured memory via ConvLSTM preserves local geometry across time, improving planning over flat LSTM.\n  - Default PG\
    \ is sufficient; multi-epoch PPO likely destabilizes with recurrent state staleness and aggressive update ratio without careful tuning.\n\n  Next steps (single-variable ablations):\n  1) ConvLSTM width ablation (features ∈ {32, 64, 96}) with all other hparams fixed (default PG).\n  2) Depth ablation: stack 2 ConvLSTM layers (same features) with default PG.\n\n  If width/depth help, follow-ups:\n  - Normalization in ConvLSTM gates (LayerNorm/GroupNorm).\n  - Modest attention atop spatial memory.\n  - Value head capacity/regularization tweaks.\n\n  Paper plan:\n  - Include our own CNN baseline run (ID 10) and ablations; report mean±std across seeds.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ConvLSTM width ablation (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convlstm, ablation, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Ablate ConvLSTM memory width with default policy-gradient training and fixed non-recurrent backbone to isolate capacity effects. Features ∈ {32,\
    \ 64, 96}; other hparams fixed to defaults.\"\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 46\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fixed defaults; only vary ConvLSTM width to isolate effect\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([32, 64, 96]),\n      }\n\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n\
    \          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n\
    \                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n          # Init/Reset state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              h = jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              c = jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n          # ConvLSTM update\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCell(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n          # Heads\n\
    \          z = h_out.reshape((B, -1))\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CNNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stacked ConvLSTM x2 (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convlstm, depth, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Depth\
    \ ablation: two stacked ConvLSTM layers on top of a residual CNN, with default policy-gradient training and fixed hyperparameters to isolate the effect of deeper spatial memory.\"\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 47\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fix all except depth; match prior best width for comparability\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, self.kernel_size,\
    \ padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM2(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict]\
    \ = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n          # Init/Reset stacked states\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h1': zeros(), 'c1': zeros(), 'h2': zeros(), 'c2': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h1': rnn_state['h1'] * m, 'c1': rnn_state['c1'] * m,\n                  'h2': rnn_state['h2'] * m, 'c2': rnn_state['c2']\
    \ * m,\n              }\n          # Layer 1\n          (h1, c1), y1 = ConvLSTMCell(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h1'], rnn_state['c1']), x)\n          # Layer 2\n          (h2, c2), y2 = ConvLSTMCell(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h2'], rnn_state['c2']), y1)\n          rnn_state = {'h1': h1, 'c1': c1, 'h2': h2, 'c2': c2}\n          # Heads on top layer\n          z = y2.reshape((B, -1))\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n\
    \      return CNNConvLSTM2(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Continuing research actions**


  I need to produce actions to continue our research. We''ve made a significant improvement with the ConvLSTM architecture, hitting a score of 52.7, up from the previous SOTA baseline of 40.8. That''s exciting! Now, I''ll iterate on ablations and algorithm variants to push further. I also need to document this in a private memory capsule while managing token usage effectively. Additionally, I’ll run some experiments, like combining ConvLSTM with PPO, but I’m cautious since previous attempts didn’t work well. There might still be some synergy to explore here!**Respecting training constraints**


  I need to be careful with training constraints, avoiding heuristics. I can modify the algorithm, but I should change one variable at a time. We''ve already altered the architecture, so next, I’ll focus on ablations like varying conv_lstm_features, stacking ConvLSTM depth, and adding layer normalization in the ConvLSTM cell. Incorporating the time channel is important too, as it''s already in the observations. Adding residual connections for updates is worth considering, as well as examining the default training code to ensure proper RNN state updates.**Examining RNN state updates**


  I noticed something in train_single.py regarding the handling of RNN states. After calling `collect_trajectory_jit`, they mention updating RNN states for the next rollout, but it doesn’t look like they actually assign them. The RNN states get updated to `final_rnn_states` through the training loop. The ConvLSTM network yields three outputs, including an RNN state. I might explore options like a deeper ConvLSTM or introduce channel grouping, but I want to keep changes minimal. My next step is to ablate the contributions by comparing different models to understand their effectiveness better.**Defining hyperparameters**


  In the default hyperparameters, we only used 64 for conv_lstm_features, but I can set up `tune.choice` to sample from options like 32, 64, or 96. The training includes 4 trials, so I can choose 3-4 options to explore. I might also consider adding group normalization or layer normalization to the ConvLSTM cell. Instead of rewriting the network code into a storage file, I can import our saved module using `sys.path.append`. I need to make sure I include the correct lowercase path for ''zephyr'' since automatic imports aren''t reliable.**Designing experiments**


  I’m laying out a plan for two experiments. For the first, I''ll focus on "ConvLSTM width ablation with LayerNorm," tuning `conv_lstm_features` in [32, 64, 96] while keeping the rest of the training settings the same. The second experiment involves "Stacked ConvLSTM x2," where I''ll use two ConvLSTMCells with a residual structure. I’ll maintain their state in a nested dictionary. It might also be interesting to try a channel-wise squeeze-excitation attention next, keeping changes minimal as I design these experiments. Plus, I’ll create a private memory capsule summarizing everything!**Preparing experiment submissions**


  I’m setting up the training pipeline. Since we''re using `_define_hyperparameters`, we need to craft a YAML for capsule creation carefully, especially with colons needing quotes. The first submission will focus on "ConvLSTM width ablation," searching through `conv_lstm_features` of {32, 64, 96} while keeping other parameters default. The second submission will involve a two-layer stacked ConvLSTM, fixing the `conv_lstm_features` at 64 to test depth effects. Now, I’ll summarize results, create the capsule, and prepare for the submissions step-by-step, ensuring everything compiles correctly!**Creating code templates**


  I’m defining a few classes: ConvLSTMCell with gating, ResidualBlock, and CNNConvLSTM. For the stacked version, CNNConvLSTM2 will use two ConvLSTMCells, managing the `rnn_state` structure and ensuring proper resetting. I’ll create pseudo-code for the rnn_state initialization and updating it after the ConvLSTM operations, being cautious about the residual connections. Additionally, I''ll handle YAML carefully to encapsulate any colons in quotes. I also want to document insights about PPO performance to ensure I understand the issues. Now, I''ll finalize the actions and YAML blocks!'
