  I need to navigate to Research Counter to rewrite the file ''zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_variants.py'' since it wasn’t created last time due to being in the wrong room. I’m planning to queue up new experiments like ConvLSTM + GroupNorm + ForgetBias and ConvLSTM + Attention pooling head, and maybe just evaluate the forget gate bias. The system constraints allow for only two concurrent experiments, and since we currently have none running, it''s clear I can submit new ones.**Writing variant code in Research Counter**


  I need to write the variant code to storage from Research Counter since that''s where storage operations are allowed. The previous attempt to write ''cnn_convlstm_variants.py'' failed, so it’s time to do it now. I''ll then submit two experiments using the modules from storage, specifically "CNNConvLSTMGNFB" and "CNNConvLSTMGNFB_Attn." I might test the group norm stacked again, but the results show it wasn’t effective. Instead, I can experiment with single-layer GN + forget bias and attention pooling. I''ll keep the algorithm and hyperparameters fixed, optionally adding ''conv_lstm_features: 64'' and updating the Private memory with notes on results.**Ensuring YAML and module standards**


  I need to follow the "Important YAML Rule: quotes around text with a colon" since titles might include colons. Our YAML content for titles has parentheses but no colons, which is fine. It''s critical to check that the code referencing ''storage/zephyr'' is correct. The "Using Python Modules from Storage" document specifies importing from ''storage/shared'' and ''storage/{lineage}''. Since we can read any lineage''s storage but only write to our own, referencing ''storage/zephyr'' is accurate for our lowercase lineage name. I also want to ensure our code is consistent with JAX and Flax, using jax.numpy as jnp and flax.linen, while avoiding any Sokoban-specific heuristics.**Managing time budgets for trials**


  I need to be cautious about our time budgets, allowing 30 minutes per trial. Heavy models may increase compile time, and I noticed that the times for ID12 and ID13 varied significantly. As model complexity increases, training times have gone up, with some reaching 1468 seconds. While group normalization (GN) didn’t drastically affect performance, it did slow things down. So for attention pooling, it might still be fine. My plan is to go to Research, write ''zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_variants.py'', and submit the following experiments: "ConvLSTM + GroupNorm + ForgetBias" and "ConvLSTM + GN+FB + Attention pooling." I may consider isolating just the forget-gate bias, but for now, I’ll proceed with GNFB and GNFB_Attn as our main options since we only have two concurrent slots.**Finalizing storage and submissions**


  I need to create a Private Memory update after submitting two experiments since we''re at the concurrency limit. I want to be cautious about token usage too. First, I’ll go to Research to write ''zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_variants.py'' and execute two submissions: one for "ConvLSTM + GroupNorm + ForgetBias" and the other for "ConvLSTM + GN+FB + Attention pooling." I’ll ensure the quotes are correct and include necessary imports for storage. After that, I might update Private Memory with results and planned expansions, keeping everything concise. Let’s make sure to execute these actions efficiently!'
