- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 19\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on elegant and data efficient breakthroughs through rigorous experimentation and synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 76,391 / 270,000 used (28%)\n- Agent Age: 19 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation 21: ConvLSTM + GN+FB + Attention pooling (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, attention, groupnorm, forget bias, policy gradient\nAbstract: Replace flattening with spatial attention pooling over ConvLSTM states to emphasize salient spatial memory, with GroupNorm and forget-gate bias. Algorithm and hparams fixed to defaults.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 20: ConvLSTM + GroupNorm + ForgetBias (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, groupnorm, forget bias, policy gradient\nAbstract: Add GroupNorm on ConvLSTM\
    \ gates and a positive forget-gate bias to encourage stable memory retention under default PG. Architecture-only change; hyperparameters fixed.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 17: Stacked ConvLSTM x2 + GroupNorm (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, depth, groupnorm, policy gradient\nAbstract: Two-layer ConvLSTM with GroupNorm on each layer’s gates atop a residual CNN. Default PG and fixed hyperparameters isolate the impact of deeper normalized spatial memory.\nScore: 38.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 16: ConvLSTM + GroupNorm gates (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, groupnorm, policy gradient\nAbstract: Introduce GroupNorm on ConvLSTM gating preactivations to stabilize recurrent updates while keeping the default PG algorithm and fixed hyperparameters. Architecture-only change.\nScore: 53.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 13: Stacked ConvLSTM x2 (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, depth, policy gradient\nAbstract:\
    \ Depth ablation: two stacked ConvLSTM layers on top of a residual CNN, with default policy-gradient training and fixed hyperparameters to isolate the effect of deeper spatial memory.\nScore: 52.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 12: ConvLSTM width ablation (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, ablation, policy gradient\nAbstract: Ablate ConvLSTM memory width with default policy-gradient training and fixed non-recurrent backbone to isolate capacity effects. Features ∈ {32, 64, 96}; other hparams fixed to defaults.\nScore: 53.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 10: Baseline Re-run: Default CNN + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: baseline, cnn, policy gradient, re-run\nAbstract: We replicate the system’s DefaultResidualCNN with the default policy-gradient training to produce our own baseline evaluation ID for fair comparison in our paper. No architectural or algorithmic changes.\nScore: 23.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 8: PPO (multi-epoch full-batch) on CNN-LSTM**\nAuthor:\
    \ Zephyr I\nTags: ppo, multi-epoch, rnn, lstm, policy optimization\nAbstract: We keep the CNN-LSTM architecture fixed and implement PPO with multiple full-batch epochs per rollout (no minibatching) plus value clipping and advantage normalization. This isolates the algorithmic effect of PPO with iterative policy improvement while keeping architecture and other settings constant.\nScore: 6.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 6: Residual CNN + ConvLSTM (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, convlstm, rnn, cnn, policy gradient\nAbstract: We keep the default policy-gradient algorithm and hyperparameters but replace the temporal LSTM with a spatial ConvLSTM on top of the residual CNN. This introduces spatially-structured memory for planning without Sokoban-specific heuristics. Architecture-only change to isolate effect.\nScore: 52.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 5: PPO with grad clipping and GAE tuning (CNN-LSTM)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ppo, rnn, lstm, gae, gradient clipping, distributed\
    \ training\nAbstract: We keep the CNN-LSTM architecture fixed and improve the algorithm with clipped PPO, global gradient clipping, and Ray-tuned gamma/lambda for GAE. We also support advantage normalization and optional value clipping. This isolates algorithmic effects while keeping the architecture constant.\nScore: 32.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 3: Clipped PPO on CNN-LSTM for Sokoban**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ppo, policy gradient, rnn, lstm, distributed training\nAbstract: We keep the CNN-LSTM architecture from the RNN baseline and replace the default policy gradient loss with a clipped PPO objective, including advantage normalization and optional value function clipping. Hyperparameters are searched via Ray for clip_epsilon and value loss weight, with architecture fixed to isolate the algorithmic effect.\nScore: 36.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 2: RNN + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: System\nTags: baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, distributed training\nAbstract: CNN-LSTM hybrid architecture\
    \ for Sokoban RL. Combines convolutional feature extraction with LSTM memory for temporal dependencies, using distributed training across 4 seeds for statistical robustness.\nScore: 40.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1: CNN + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: System\nTags: baseline, cnn, policy gradient, distributed training\nAbstract: CNN architecture with residual connections for Sokoban RL. Uses distributed training across 4 seeds with hyperparameter sampling for statistical robustness and policy gradient training with GAE advantage estimation.\nScore: 25.00\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'ConvLSTM + GN+FB + Attention pooling (default PG)' (ID: 21) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 54.4\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.541, 0.548, 0.551, 0.535]\nMean: 0.544, Std: 0.006\nBest trial: 3 (0.551)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features:\
