tick: 21
role: model
parts:
- text: "Fantastic progress—new SOTA is 56.3 (ConvLSTM+GN+Attn with f_bias=0.5). I’ll now test:\n- LayerNorm vs GroupNorm (with attention pooling)\n- Dilated CNN backbone (with GN+Attn, f_bias=0.5) to expand receptive field\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ConvLSTM + LayerNorm gates + Attention pooling (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convlstm, layernorm, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Swap GroupNorm for LayerNorm on ConvLSTM gates while keeping attention pooling and default PG. Tests whether per-pixel LayerNorm stabilizes recurrent dynamics better than GroupNorm.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm import CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fix training hparams and capacity to isolate normalization effect\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef':\
    \ tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated CNN + ConvLSTM (GN+FB) + Attention pooling (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, dilation, convlstm, groupnorm, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Use a dilated residual CNN backbone to enlarge spatial receptive fields while keeping ConvLSTM with GroupNorm+forget-bias and attention pooling under default PG. Tests whether wider context improves planning.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from\
    \ ray import tune\n\n  class ResidualDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 2\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(1,1),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellGNFB(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      num_groups: int = 8\n      f_bias: float = 0.5\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x,\
    \ h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.GroupNorm(num_groups=self.num_groups)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f + self.f_bias)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM_Dilated_Attn(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation: int = 2\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n      num_groups: int = 8\n      f_bias: float = 0.5\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # Dilated CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # ConvLSTM with GroupNorm + forget-bias\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellGNFB(\n              self.conv_lstm_features, num_groups=self.num_groups, f_bias=self.f_bias\n          )((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Attention pooling\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n\
    \                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          H = W = 8\n          h_flat = h_out.reshape((B, H*W, self.conv_lstm_features))\n          z = jnp.einsum('bij,bi->bj', h_flat, attn)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fix training hparams; test dilation impact with established best settings\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          # Optionally try dilations 1 (baseline) vs 2\n          'dilation': tune.choice([2]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_Dilated_Attn(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n          num_groups=8,\n          f_bias=0.5,\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring model improvements**\n\nI’m reflecting on the earlier use of \"ConvLSTM + GN + FB + Attention pooling\" and the f_bias settings. My best findings show that the combination of attention pooling with an f_bias of 0.5 gave the highest score of 56.3. Now, I want to propose some improvements:  \n\nFirst, I think about testing LN versus GN with attention pooling while considering whether to keep the f_bias. The LN version currently doesn’t have f_bias, but maybe I could include it as an option.  \n\nAdditionally, I want to explore using a Dilated CNN to enlarge the receptive field, possibly with attention pooling for consistency.**Assessing CNNConvLSTM_Dilated**\n\nI’m considering the 'CNNConvLSTM_Dilated' model, which currently uses flattening instead of attention pooling. It might perform worse than the state-of-the-art models. I’m thinking about adding attention pooling, but this would require some reorganization of the code. I could make a new submission that\
  \ imports the current model and integrates attention, but I’d have to redefine the network to incorporate attention properly. It seems like creating an entirely new network variant with attention pooling is the way to go, which is allowed in the content field without needing to write to storage.**Planning variant submissions**\n\nI’m realizing it’s better practice to keep models in storage for reusability, though it’s not strictly necessary. We already have the LN_Attn variant prepared, so I can submit that. I might also explore using a Gated attention temperature below 1 to sharpen it up. While the ablation tests showed the best results with a temperature of 1.0, testing 0.9 could be interesting, even if it leads to small effects. \n\nFor simplicity, I’ll craft two submissions:  \n1. \"ConvLSTM + LayerNorm gates + Attention pooling,\" using the LN_Attn variant.\n2. \"Dilated CNN backbone + ConvLSTM + Attention pooling,\" which I’ll implement inline.**Deciding on new network implementation**\n\
  \nI’m considering importing the 'cnn_convlstm_dilated.py', but it doesn’t include attention functionality. I could implement a new network in a submission or create a new file called 'cnn_convlstm_dilated_attn.py'. Since we can perform two actions along with their YAML configurations, I can write the storage and then submit both. \n\nI need to remember to quote any YAML titles containing colons to avoid parser issues. The \"Archive Room\" is currently unavailable due to maturity restrictions, but that's okay; I'll use private memory instead.**Planning submission commands**\n\nI’m looking at my token budget, which is 87,548 out of 270k, so that’s safe. Next steps include submitting the LN+Attn variant from storage and writing a new submission for 'Dilated CNN + ConvLSTM + Attn'. I’m considering adjusting conv_lstm_features to 64 and keeping the default hyperparameters.\n\nI’ll also update private memory with the new state-of-the-art score of 56.3, but immediate actions involve just two\
  \ submission commands. I need to ensure proper quoting for any YAML titles with special characters, especially colons. \n\nFor the first submission, I’ll import from 'storage/zephyr' and use the appropriate network definitions. For the second submission, I’ll implement inline code and specify the relevant parameters. Finally, I’ll check that everything is correctly implemented. Time to craft the commands!"
