tick: 29
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Zephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 29)\n  SOTA: 63.5 (LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention, attn_temp=1.0). CoordConv: equal 63.5. LN+FB: 61.7. LN+MHA(4): 58.4. GN trails LN by 7–10 pts.\n  Policy: Keep default PG; change one variable at a time; 2 concurrent jobs max; prefer architectural parsimony; lock attn_temp=1.0 unless ablation.\n  Pending: Width ablation (IDs 47:48ch, 48:80ch). Decision rule—if 80ch > 64ch, adopt 80; else keep 64.\n  Next queue (after 47/48): \n    - If 80ch wins: confirm with replicate run; else replicate 64ch.\n    - Optional: small forget-bias sweep for LN+Attn (f_bias in {0.25, 0.5}—0.5 regressed, try 0.25).\n    - Only if signals suggest: head-count ablation (1 vs 2) since 4 heads underperformed.\n  Paper prep: maintain table of evals (IDs, mean±std). Focus on: effect of gate normalization (LN>GN), attention pooling, width sensitivity.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering capacity and strategy**\n\nI’m thinking about the balance between parsimony and capacity. Adding capacity might enhance results. Dropout and entropy balancing is another interesting idea, but it could change the algorithm and hyperparameters. I want to maintain comparability by keeping defaults, but I can still vary specifics one at a time.\n\nSince we've improved to 63.5, I'm considering using LN gating with 80 channels for the next step. After returning the results, we can compare them and prepare a summary for the paper later.**Planning research storage and submissions**\n\nI’m considering checking the research storage for code, but it might not be necessary. Here’s my proposed plan:\n\nFirst, I’ll set a meta prompt that includes the SOTA method specification, the ablation plan, and submission queue rules. While waiting for ablation results, I can prep the next submission code, possibly using LN+Attn with a width of 72 channels. The code for width is simple\
  \ and doesn’t need storage.\n\nI might also test value head gating improvements like separating BN/LN, but this could change the architecture. For now, we can create a meta prompt and consider a Private Memory update.**Implementing action steps**\n\nI need to be careful here: each action has to be on its own line, and I'll have YAML blocks after the commands. So, here’s my implementation plan:\n\nFirst, I’ll execute an action with a 'content' block summarizing SOTA, protocol, and next planned experiments. \n\nI might consider revisiting the Research Counter to rank the score again, although it’s optional. Crafting a succinct meta prompt will be helpful too. \n\nFor the SOTA, that's at 63.5 with LN+Attn, and we have a protocol that includes keeping the algorithm default PG and using LN gating. Lastly, we’re awaiting width ablation results for 47 and 48, plus considering the next experiments on head count ablation.**Planning experiments and evaluations**\n\nI see that MHA lost, which makes\
  \ me think about testing head=2 versus head=1, although we generally assume a single head performs best.\n\nI need to evaluate if 80 channels outperforms 64 channels; if it does, I’ll establish a new baseline with 80. After the width ablation, I may propose testing LN+Attn with a small forgetting bias of 0.25 instead of 0.5, since the latter reduced performance. \n\nDocumenting everything for an archive draft after 5-10 more experiments sounds good too. I'll plan to produce commands in a structured format, ensuring the YAML content is safe and correctly formatted."
