tick: 33
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/sota_ln_attn.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  SOTA network: LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM (64ch) + single-head attention pooling.\n  Default CNN widths: 32/64. Attention temperature = 1.0.\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Any\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm import CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn\n\n  def make_sota_network(\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32,\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64,\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64,\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0,\n  ) -> Any:\n      return CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn(\n          cnn_features_1=cnn_features_1,\n          cnn_features_2=cnn_features_2,\n          conv_lstm_features=conv_lstm_features,\n          attn_temp=attn_temp,\n      )\n\n  __all__ = [\"make_sota_network\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Paper outline (draft):\n  Title: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Attention Enables Strong Planning in Sokoban under Policy Gradient\n  Abstract\
    \ (sketch): We show that replacing GroupNorm with LayerNorm on ConvLSTM gates and adding a single-head spatial attention pooling yields a solve rate of ~63.5% on 8x8 Sokoban under a fixed 30-min/seed PG budget. We report extensive ablations demonstrating the importance of gate normalization and attention pooling, while wider recurrent width, multi-head attention, forget-bias, and PPO variants underperform.\n  Methods:\n  - Base env/task, training pipeline (Ray, 4 seeds, 50M steps or 30min/seed); default PG + GAE.\n  - Architecture: residual CNN (32/64), ConvLSTM (64ch), LayerNorm on gate preactivations, single-head spatial attention pooling (temp=1.0).\n  Results (mean ± std solve rate):\n  - CNN+PG (ours, ID 10): 23.6\n  - CNN+LSTM+PG (system, ID 2): 40.8\n  - ConvLSTM+PG (ours, ID 6): 52.7\n  - ConvLSTM+GN+Attn (ID 21): 54.4\n  - ConvLSTM+LN+Attn (ID 29): 63.5 (SOTA)\n  Key Ablations:\n  - Normalization: GN+Attn 54.4 vs LN+Attn 63.5 → LN>GN by ~9.1\n  - Attention: no-attn 52.7 → +Attn\
    \ 63.5 (+10.8)\n  - Width (ConvLSTM): 48ch 58.7, 64ch 63.5, 80ch 60.3 → best 64ch\n  - Temperature: 0.8 vs 1.0 → 1.0 better (60.1 max)\n  - Forget-bias (LN): +0.5 → 61.7; +0.25 → 59.5 (both regress)\n  - Multi-head (LN): 2/4 heads → 58.7/58.4 (regress)\n  - CoordConv: matches 63.5 (no gain)\n  - Dilation (GN): 57.4 (below LN)\n  - PPO variants (RNN): 32.2/36.2; multi-epoch PPO 6.3 (time-budget + state staleness issues)\n  Discussion:\n  - LN likely stabilizes gate distributions, improving credit assignment for long-horizon planning.\n  - Single-head attention concentrates spatial credit effectively; multiple heads oversmooth under budget.\n  - Capacity sweet spot at 64ch; too small underfits, too large hurts sample efficiency within time budget.\n  - PPO under fixed wall-clock budget underperformed due to fewer env steps per wall-clock and RNN inconsistencies.\n  Next steps:\n  - Await replication (ID 57) and CNN width 48/96 (ID 58).\n  - If stable, finalize figures/tables and submit\
    \ when Archive opens.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning actions and adjustments**\n\nI’m figuring out how to maintain tokens and provide necessary actions. The environment expects /execute_action commands, and I have options like goto, submit, and storage. While waiting for results, I could document everything in Private Memory. I might explore width adjustments in the convlstm or consider tuning the attn_temp closer to 1.0. We’ve already tested 0.8 versus 1.0, finding 1.0 better. I should also contemplate modifying the architecture to include connections from CNN features.**Considering LayerNorm and actions**\n\nI’m thinking about testing LayerNorm on the hidden output h_t, but it feels a bit risky. Since we’re nearing 50 ticks, it’s best to wait for the replication results. I can set meta to show waiting and start the archive structure in Private Memory. It might also be useful to summarize with 'page_size 100' and 'filter layernorm', although that adds token overhead. I’ll prepare a concise message stating that\
  \ no actions are needed, and I’ll jot down a \"prepare paper outline\" note in Private Memory. I also plan to create a 'sota_model.py' to save the best network setup.**Implementing network storage and updates**\n\nI want to write the content for 'zephyr/networks/sota_ln_attn.py,' referencing CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn and importing from a previous file to avoid duplication. For clarity, I could define a wrapper function called create_network. The submission for create_network will involve a direct import, which helps streamline things. I’ll implement a storage write with a module function that returns the necessary settings for cnn_f1, cnn_f2, conv_lstm_features, and attn_temp. \n\nAfter that, I'll update the meta to reflect this work and revise Private Memory with the paper outline. In essence, I’ll write the storage file and address the updates needed in memory.**Drafting the code for YAML and network**\n\nI need to ensure that we're following the YAML quoting rule, which states that titles\
  \ with colons must be quoted. Our previous titles were quoted correctly, so that’s good. Keeping the message concise is key, and I'll include the necessary actions. \n\nI'll start drafting the code for 'sota_ln_attn.py' by importing JAX and Flax, as well as our existing CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn from the networks directory. Then, I’ll define a function called 'make_sota_network' that returns the CNNConvLSTMLN_Attn module. Time to write it out!"
