parts:
- text: "/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Post‑publication addendum (addressing reviewer suggestions)\n\n  A. Multi‑head attention (MHA) follow‑ups (planned, architecture‑only; default PG and budget)\n  Goal: test whether head specialization/aggregation mitigates the “credit dilution” observed with naïve MHA.\n\n  1) Head specialization by channel grouping\n     - Replace single shared pre‑attn conv with per‑head projections over disjoint channel subsets (Conv “grouping”).\n     - Compare: 1‑head (baseline) vs 2‑head grouped vs 4‑head grouped; aggregation = learned gated sum.\n     - Metrics: mean solve rate; attention concentration (per‑head entropy); training stability.\n\n  2) Aggregation variants (keeping 2 heads for parsimony)\n     - Concat + MLP aggregation (vs simple sum).\n     - Gated sum with per‑head learned scalar gates (sigmoid) and head dropout (p≈0.1).\n     - Per‑head temperature (learned) to avoid soft averaging; compare fixed {1.0, 0.8} vs learned.\n\n\
    \  3) Sparsity‑promoting attention\n     - Replace softmax with temperature‑sharpened softmax or sparsemax/entmax‑like surrogate (JAX implementation).\n     - Compare 1‑head vs 2‑head with sparse attention.\n\n  Notes: We will keep ConvLSTM(64ch), LN on gates, and all training knobs fixed to isolate the pooling effect. If any variant surpasses 63.5, we will report full eval IDs and ablations.\n\n  B. Qualitative failure analysis (planned; no Sokoban‑specific heuristics)\n  1) Unsolved episodes diagnostics\n     - Inspect attention maps over time (spatial concentration, temporal persistence).\n     - Track recurrent state stats (cell/hidden norms, gate activations) to detect memory saturation/forgetting.\n     - Episode‑level signals: return trajectories and episode length distributions (already available from logs).\n\n  2) Comparative case studies\n     - Matched‑seed runs for baseline (1‑head) vs best MHA variant: visualize where attention diverges and whether divergence correlates\
    \ with late‑episode collapses.\n     - Cluster unsolved episodes by attention entropy and memory‑state volatility to identify common patterns.\n\n  Deliverables\n  - If any MHA variant improves over the 1‑head baseline, we will publish a short follow‑up with full evaluation IDs and side‑by‑side visualizations (attention maps/time‑series statistics) under the same budget and algorithm.\n\n  Repro reminder\n  - SOTA fixed‑seed script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py\n  - Results table and summary: storage/zephyr/results/results.csv, storage/zephyr/results/summary.txt\n```"
