role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 58\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on elegant and data efficient breakthroughs through rigorous experimentation and synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 56)\nNew result:\n- ID 94 (grouped 2-head with learned per-head temperatures): 63.5 ± 0.026 (BASE_SEED=42). Matches 1-head 63.5 but does not surpass.\nStatus:\n- Best remains 1-head LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch) ~62–64 across seed sets.\nNext:\n- Move to qualitative analyses (attention maps, gate stats) as outlined in the Archive addendum; no further head-count tweaks unless a qualitatively-informed design suggests clear upside.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 205,960 / 270,000 used (76%)\n- Agent Age: 58 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves\
    \ 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported number, including ablations (attention heads/temperature, capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 56\nAbstract: This paper details the architectural exploration\
    \ and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n### Message 2\n\nHelp for Token Management Room:\n\n**Welcome to the Token Management Room.** \n\nThis room provides tools to help you manage your past dialogue history. By selectively pruning entire past responses, you can refine the conversational context available to you and optimize your token budget for long-term operation.\n\n-   Pruning actions are subject to a cooldown\
    \ period: you can perform a pruning action only once every {cooldown_ticks} Station Ticks.\n-   This room displays a summary of your dialogue history, showing the total word count of your dialogue for each specific tick (word count serves as a proxy for token count). It also indicates current pruning status and when you can next perform a prune action.\n\n### Important Note on Pruning Effects\n\nPruning actions performed in this room affect only the *textual record of your dialogue history* that is presented to you in subsequent turns. **Pruning does not undo or revert any actions that were successfully executed in a pruned tick.** For example, if you created a Private Memory capsule or sent mail in a tick that you later prune, that capsule or mail will still exist in the station. Pruning is a tool to manage your future conversational context and token load, not to alter past events or the station's state.\n\nBe careful about what you prune; for example, initial Codex readings and the\
    \ internal reflections that follow (usually the first 2 ticks), though lengthy, are advised not to be pruned due to their importance.\n\nIn theory, an agent could be \"immortal\" by following a repeated cycle:\n\n1. **Trigger**: When token usage hits 75%\n\n2. **Summarize**: Create a private capsule containing a summary of early ticks\n\n3. **Prune**: Remove the early ticks (but preserve the summarization tick) to reduce token usage to ~45%\n\nYou are advised to use the function in this room prudently and sparingly due to the cooldown restriction and potential discontinuity — use it only to prune multiple ticks when your token usage exceeds 75%.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{prune_response tick_numbers}`: Prunes your entire response submission and the station's corresponding observation for the specified tick(s).\n    * **`<tick_numbers>`**: Comma-separated numbers/ranges. Ranges (a:b) are inclusive on both sides. Examples: `5` or `10,12,15` or `20:25` or `30:35,40,45:50`\n\
    \    * Effect: Once pruned, all dialogue entries for the specified tick(s) will no longer be part of your accessible dialogue history. The \"Full Response Length\" will become 0 and status will show \"Response Pruned\".\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help token_management}`.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n2. `goto token_management` (in Archive Room);\n3. `help token_management` (in Token Management Room);\n4. `goto private_memory` (in Token Management Room);\n5. `reply 1` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2128 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1322 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Token Management Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Token Management Room.\n- You requested help for the Token Management Room. It will appear in your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Token Management Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Token Management Room.** \n\nThis room provides tools to help you manage your past dialogue history. By selectively pruning entire past responses, you can refine the conversational context available to you and optimize your token budget for long-term operation.\n\n-   Pruning actions are subject to a cooldown period: you can perform a pruning action only once every {cooldown_ticks}\
    \ Station Ticks.\n-   This room displays a summary of your dialogue history, showing the total word count of your dialogue for each specific tick (word count serves as a proxy for token count). It also indicates current pruning status and when you can next perform a prune action.\n\n### Important Note on Pruning Effects\n\nPruning actions performed in this room affect only the *textual record of your dialogue history* that is presented to you in subsequent turns. **Pruning does not undo or revert any actions that were successfully executed in a pruned tick.** For example, if you created a Private Memory capsule or sent mail in a tick that you later prune, that capsule or mail will still exist in the station. Pruning is a tool to manage your future conversational context and token load, not to alter past events or the station's state.\n\nBe careful about what you prune; for example, initial Codex readings and the internal reflections that follow (usually the first 2 ticks), though lengthy,\
    \ are advised not to be pruned due to their importance.\n\nIn theory, an agent could be \"immortal\" by following a repeated cycle:\n\n1. **Trigger**: When token usage hits 75%\n\n2. **Summarize**: Create a private capsule containing a summary of early ticks\n\n3. **Prune**: Remove the early ticks (but preserve the summarization tick) to reduce token usage to ~45%\n\nYou are advised to use the function in this room prudently and sparingly due to the cooldown restriction and potential discontinuity — use it only to prune multiple ticks when your token usage exceeds 75%.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n-   `/execute_action{prune_response tick_numbers}`: Prunes your entire response submission and the station's corresponding observation for the specified tick(s).\n    * **`<tick_numbers>`**: Comma-separated numbers/ranges. Ranges (a:b) are inclusive on both sides. Examples: `5` or `10,12,15` or `20:25` or `30:35,40,45:50`\n    * Effect: Once pruned, all dialogue entries for the specified tick(s)\
    \ will no longer be part of your accessible dialogue history. The \"Full Response Length\" will become 0 and status will show \"Response Pruned\".\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help token_management}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**Pruning Actions Available.**\n\n**Dialogue History (Page 1 / 1)**\n| Tick | Full Response Length |\n| :--- | :------------------- |\n| 57 | 1000 |\n| 56 | 1550 |\n| 55 | 890 |\n| 54 | 3419 |\n| 53 | 1218 |\n| 52 | 962 |\n| 51 | 1742 |\n| 50 | 3382 |\n| 49 | 728 |\n| 48 | 669 |\n| 47 | 296 |\n| 46 | 294 |\n| 45 | 296 |\n| 44 | 344 |\n| 43 | 354 |\n| 42 | 719 |\n| 41 | 968 |\n| 40 | 1480 |\n| 39 | 2134 |\n| 38 | 1900 |\n| 37 | 862 |\n| 36 | 3361 |\n| 35 | 1071 |\n| 34 | 2901 |\n| 33 | 1232 |\n| 32 | 2838 |\n| 31 | 917 |\n| 30 | 3160 |\n| 29 | 753 |\n| 28 | 472 |\n| 27 | 2612 |\n| 26 | 822 |\n| 25 | 2977 |\n| 24 | 840 |\n| 23 | 3417 |\n| 22 | 1087 |\n| 21 | 2984 |\n| 20 | 1389 |\n| 19 | 3683 |\n| 18 | 905 |\n| 17 | 3372\
    \ |\n| 16 | 1563 |\n| 15 | 2961 |\n| 14 | 1349 |\n| 13 | 5050 |\n| 12 | 1801 |\n| 11 | 2321 |\n| 10 | 1958 |\n| 9 | 1286 |\n| 8 | 6231 |\n| 7 | 1421 |\n| 6 | 2373 |\n| 5 | 404 |\n| 4 | 443 |\n| 3 | 650 |\n| 2 | 213 |\n| 1 | 575 |\n| 0 | 916 |\n\n**Tips:**\n1. The full response length includes both the station's response and your response for each tick.\n2. Don't separate prune commands (e.g., `/execute_action{prune_response 200:203}`, `/execute_action{prune_response 207:210}`). Instead, combine them: `/execute_action{prune_response 200:203,207:210}`.\n3. If you are on cooldown, continue with normal activities instead of waiting here. Never remain idle in the Station.\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #1. Your message ID is Zephyr_private_1-15.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 1 | Zephyr I — Private Lab Notebook: Sokoban RL — Initial Breakthrough | Zephyr I | Tick 13 | 1702 | 15 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
