parts:
- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Zephyr I — Consolidated Research Summary (for pruning)\"\ntags: \"summary, sokoban, convlstm, layernorm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Compact summary of findings, SOTA config, evaluation IDs, and file locations to preserve context before pruning dialogue history.\"\ncontent: |\n  SOTA configuration:\n  - LN(shared)-gated ConvLSTM (64 ch) + single-head spatial attention (attn_temp=1.0), default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4).\n  - Stable range across fixed seed sets: ~61–64 mean solve rate.\n\n  Key evaluation IDs (mean ± std):\n  - System baselines: ID 1 CNN+PG 25.0; ID 2 CNN+LSTM+PG 40.8.\n  - Our ConvLSTM baseline (no attention): ID 6 52.7.\n  - GN+Attn best: ID 24 56.3.\n  - LN+Attn (non-fixed seeds): ID 29 63.5.\n  - Fixed-seed LN+Attn replicates: ID 62 62.5; ID 67 61.4; ID 69 62.2.\n  - CoordConv (LN): ID 40 63.5 (matches).\n  - Width ablations (LN+Attn): 48ch ID 47 58.7; 64ch ID 29 63.5; 80ch ID 48 60.3.\n  - Temp ablation: ID 41 (0.8) 60.1 vs 1.0\
    \ 63.5.\n  - Multi-head: ID 34 (4H) 58.4; ID 53 (2H) 58.7.\n  - Grouped 2-head variants (LN):\n      • Concat+MLP ID 92: 63.2\n      • Gated-sum ID 93: 53.7\n      • Learned per-head temperatures ID 94: 63.5 (matches SOTA)\n  - Optimizers (LN+Attn BASE_SEED=42): Adam+clip ID 88 59.9; AdamW+clip ID 89 60.1.\n  - ConvGRU: ID 23 51.0.\n  - PPO variants: ID 3 36.2; ID 5 32.2; ID 8 6.3.\n\n  Files (storage/zephyr):\n  - submissions/submission_sota.py (fixed seeds, reproducible)\n  - results/results.csv, results/summary.txt\n  - results/plot_results.py (bar figure generator)\n  - papers/sokoban_ln_convlstm_attn_draft.md\n  - networks/* (architectures incl. grouped MHA)\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response 0:13,15:16,18:19,21,23,25,27,30,32,34,36,39:40,50,54}"
thinking_content: '**Considering pruning actions**


  I see that I can prune once every cooldown tick, and the room indicates that pruning is available now. I''ll consider a range from tick 0 to 26 but probably avoid pruning ticks 1 and 2 since early reflections are crucial. Following the guidelines, I think summarizing first, then pruning early ticks is a safe route. I can also create a private memory capsule to summarize my research and essential eval IDs before proceeding with the pruning.**Determining prune ranges**


  I need to carefully choose the tick ranges for pruning, especially those with high lengths. I’m thinking of including ticks 0 through 12, since they have some substantial entries like tick 8, which is quite large. Tick 13 also has significant info, so I can prune it despite creating the private lab notebook there, as it will persist. Later ticks like 23, 32, 34, 36, 39, 40, and 54 are also on my list. I just want to ensure I keep crucial recent context—defining these ranges well is key!**Selecting ticks for pruning**


  I’m targeting several ticks for pruning: from 0 to 12, including tick 13 even though it has significant content, since we''re keeping the capsule. I also want to prune ticks 15, 16, 18, 19, 21, and so on, noting their data sizes. The cooldown period is a factor, but I can include multiple ticks in one pruning command to maximize efficiency. I''m mindful not to remove any current high-level results that I need, but since we have saved files, we’re safe to proceed with pruning.**Planning actions for pruning**


  I''m looking to create a new private memory capsule summary since I''m already in the Private Memory Room. I’ll execute an action to create that capsule with a title, content, and references, while tags will be optional. Then, I''ll navigate to token management to prune responses, specifically focusing on ticks from 0 to 13. While the recommendation is not to prune the initial codex readings, I feel safe proceeding since I''ve already summarized them. Reducing usage seems to be the priority, considering those early ticks may not be essential anymore.**Preparing for pruning actions**


  I can prune a long list in one command with `prune_response` covering the ticks from 0 to 13, and including others like 15 to 16, 18 to 19, 21, and more. I''m considering keeping tick 31 since it''s a smaller size. After the pruning, I need to remember that only `/execute_action` commands and YAML blocks are parsed, so my actions need to be clear. I’ll also prepare to create a private memory summarization and go to token management to prune the list.'
