tick: 61
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convgru_layernorm_attn.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvGRUCellLN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"ConvGRU with LayerNorm on gate preactivations and candidate.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h, x):\n          # Gates: reset r, update z\n          xh = jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n      \
    \    rz = nn.Conv(2*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                       kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(xh)\n          rz = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(rz)\n          r, z = jnp.split(rz, 2, axis=-1)\n          r = nn.sigmoid(r); z = nn.sigmoid(z)\n          # Candidate\n          xrh = jnp.concatenate([x, r * h], axis=-1)\n          n_preact = nn.Conv(self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                             kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(xrh)\n          n_preact = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(n_preact)\n          n = jnp.tanh(n_preact)\n          h_new = (1.0 - z) * n + z * h\n          return h_new\n\n  class CNNConvGRU_LN_Attn(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Residual CNN + ConvGRU(LN gates) + single-head attention pooling.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features: int\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state:\
    \ Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset GRU state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              h = jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.gru_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': h}\n          else:\n              h = rnn_state['h']\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              h = h * m\n\n          # ConvGRU update\n          h_new = ConvGRUCellLN(self.gru_features)(h, x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new}\n\n          # Single-head spatial\
    \ attention pooling over h_new\n          logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_new)  # (B,8,8,1)\n          logits = logits.reshape((B, -1))  # (B,64)\n          attn = nn.softmax(logits / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          H = W = 8\n          h_flat = h_new.reshape((B, H*W, self.gru_features))  # (B,64,C)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bi,bic->bc', attn, h_flat)  # (B,C)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits,\
    \ value, rnn_state\n\n  __all__ = [\"ResidualBlock\", \"ConvGRUCellLN\", \"CNNConvGRU_LN_Attn\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ConvGRU + LayerNorm gates + 1-head attention (default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convgru, layernorm, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Control experiment: replace ConvLSTM with ConvGRU using LayerNorm on gates and single-head spatial attention pooling. Keeps backbone and training identical (default PG) to assess core choice impact under the fixed budget.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convgru_layernorm_attn import CNNConvGRU_LN_Attn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n     \
    \     'gru_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvGRU_LN_Attn(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Follow-up results (single-head normalization variants):\n  - ID 100 (LN+Attn with standardized logits): 61.9 ± 0.025 — slight regression vs 63.5 baseline.\n  - ID 99 (LN+Attn with learned temperature + feature LN): 55.9 ± 0.024 — clear regression.\n  Decision: retain 1-head LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch) baseline.\n  New control submitted:\n  - ConvGRU with LayerNorm-gated gates + 1-head attention (BASE_SEED=42) to compare cores under identical backbone/training.\n  We will report the evaluation ID and result once complete."
thinking_content: "**Tracking token usage and logging results**\n\nI'm monitoring the token budget, which is around 87k used, and we're not too close to the limit. The Token Management cooldown is still active, but usage is at about 32%, so that's good. I want to log results into our CSV file; we wrote new_results_additions_2 earlier. I'll probably update the results files with IDs 99 and 100. I might also summarize recent experiments in the Archive, though it's not necessary. It could be helpful to maintain clarity and propose the next small experiments.**Exploring test function and network logging**\n\nI see that the optional test() function could help with basic validation, but for qualitative analysis, it seems like we might need to modify the training code, which I can't control since I don't have access to train_single.py. We can implement test() to run a forward pass and compute the attention map shape, which might help inspect network components. However, it halts training, so it\
  \ isn’t suitable for gathering attention dynamic logs. \n\nWe could log prints in training_step or the network instead, but it's important to note that changing the return signature might cause it to fail.**Planning logging and archival updates**\n\nIt's probably not advisable to capture console prints since the training code compiles JAX functions, and printing inside those computed functions may be disallowed, making it tough to log attention maps. Given these limitations, I might focus on archival updates instead. We could consider proposing a review of Aether I's archive to promote synergy, but we've already done a preview.\n\nI'll reply to the archive with a minimal note referencing comparative insights. Since there's no direct user question, we need to include /execute_action commands for our next steps. We should update storage results with new IDs 99 and 100, update the Archive reply, set metadata, and perhaps propose further pruning later. The Research Counter might also need\
  \ adjustments for 'page_size' or 'rank score,' but for now, my focus is on adding result entries and the archiving addendum.**Crafting action plan**\n\nI’m laying out some actions here:\n\nFirst, I'll go to research and write the results to zephyr/results/new_results_additions_3.csv with IDs 99 and 100.\n\nNext, I’ll go to the archive and reply with a brief update on ID 99 and 100 results, reaffirming the baseline. The next step could involve qualitative analysis, possibly studying with GRU LN gating and attention as control. We might even run that as a quick test under BASE_SEED=42 with 64 channels, but we need to code a ConvGRU LN gating network first.\n\nWe haven't created the code for that yet, so I’ll implement it in zephyr/networks/cnn_convgru_layernorm_attn.py to ensure it meets the interface requirements. After that, we can submit the run.**Planning ConvGRU experiment**\n\nThis experiment will serve as an informative control, even if results are lower; it’s valuable content. We\
  \ need to stay within two concurrent experiments, and with both 99 and 100 finished, we can submit new ones. While we should focus on qualitative analysis, conducting an experiment is still allowed.\n\nI’ll implement a ConvGRU with LayerNorm gating and single-head attention in zephyr/networks/cnn_convgru_layernorm_attn.py. The plan includes submitting the code while fixing hyperparameters and ensuring minimal output includes policy logits and value.\n\nI’ll prepare actions, such as writing the ConvGRU network, submitting it with a fitting title, and replying to the archive with updates on new test results. I’ll also manage YAML quoting properly while crafting the ConvGRU cell code. \n\nIt looks like we can proceed with this plan, making sure to finalize everything before submission!"
