thinking_content: "**Considering feature engineering**\n\nI’m exploring \"feature engineering,\" which might include aspects like CoordConv or time channels. Our baseline already has a time channel in observation's channel 7. I’m thinking about potential normalization of inputs and reward shaping too, but reward shaping likely isn't allowed due to fixed rewards in the environment. Maybe engineering could involve encoding extra channels from the input with operations like depthwise transformations.\n\nI have to ensure we're compliant with restrictions around specific heuristics. On another note, SOTA 69.7 might stem from improvements in algorithms or training techniques, within the fixed constraints we have.**Exploring improvements for 69.7**\n\nTo achieve a 69.7 score, let's consider a few aspects. \n\nI’m thinking about memory depth with stacked recurrent blocks, as we observed that ConvLSTM regression yielded 38 with Group Normalization, but we didn’t test Layer Normalization stacked.\
  \ I’m curious if that would help. \n\nWe also can’t use transformers due to coding restrictions, but we can implement temporal planning with attention in ConvLSTM. \n\nAdditionally, I wonder about using residual connections, the key role of Layer Normalization gating, and re-evaluating our input normalization methods. Lastly, a World Model isn’t permissible due to model-based reinforcement learning restrictions.**Considering planning modules and features**\n\nWe can explore designing \"planning modules\" like dilated convolutions or convolutional residuals with self-attention, but previous attempts with multi-head and grouped didn’t yield results. \n\nI’m wondering if advanced feature engineering could be the key. This could involve creating a temporal difference channel by incorporating differences from previous frames. It seems that this might be permissible since it doesn’t use Sokoban-specific knowledge. \n\nStill, I need to ensure that any new dimensions we add won’t disrupt the network\
  \ shape. I also considered position encodings and edge detectors, although the latter might tread into Sokoban-specific territory.**Evaluating feature engineering options**\n\nI'm thinking about adding morphological operations, which might be considered domain agnostic but still part of feature engineering. I can propose creating a \"temporal difference/change map channel\" along with a \"cumulative visited map\" that requires memory. \n\nHowever, I worry about using a visited map derived from the agent's position since it references domain-specific Sokoban knowledge. While we aren’t directly extracting those locations, if the channel isolates them explicitly, that may count as extraction. I should focus on channels that compute differences across all observations instead.**Analyzing training and evaluation strategies**\n\nI’m exploring several potential improvements like multi-step value bootstrapping, but I’m uncertain about changing the fixed training loop defaults. Although we had\
  \ poor results with PPO, could advantage normalization or an exponential moving baseline help? It’s possible to add auxiliary losses like predictive loss, though that risks introducing additional training tasks.\n\nI also want to close the loop by logging the results for IDs 99 and 100, confirming our baseline. We have a plan to run the GRU control as well and prepare qualitative analysis assets to visualize attention maps. Plus, I think reviewing evaluation code from other agents might provide insights. Using the “review evaluation_id” feature could be beneficial.**Planning for code review and improvements**\n\nI need to be mindful that while I can build on general ideas, I can’t copy code directly, as collaboration rules prohibit sharing raw code among agents. However, the system allows for reviewing submitted solutions and logs, which might let me read evaluation code without violating rules. \n\nSo, my plan is to review evaluations 102 and 103 to gather high-level insights. After that,\
  \ I can consider proposing ideas like observation normalization and Squeeze-and-Excitation gating. First, let's execute the review commands and prepare for potential next steps based on what I find. \n\nI'll go ahead and send the review actions!"
