role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_ln_slots.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry  # (B,H,W,C)\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x,\
    \ h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class SlotPooling(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Single-step slot-style pooling (general, no domain heuristics).\"\"\"\n      num_slots: int\n      feat_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, tokens):  # tokens: (B, T, C)\n          B, T, C = tokens.shape\n          # Learned initial slots (shared across batch)\n          slots = self.param('slots_init', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (self.num_slots, C))\n          slots = jnp.tile(slots[None, ...], (B, 1, 1))  # (B, S, C)\n\n          # Projections\n          k = nn.Dense(C, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(tokens)   # (B,T,C)\n          v = nn.Dense(C, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(tokens)\
    \   # (B,T,C)\n          q = nn.Dense(C, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(slots)    # (B,S,C)\n\n          scale = 1.0 / jnp.sqrt(C.astype(tokens.dtype))\n          attn_logits = jnp.einsum('bsc,btc->bst', q, k) * scale  # (B,S,T)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1)                 # (B,S,T)\n\n          # Slot updates\n          slot_context = jnp.einsum('bst,btc->bsc', attn, v)      # (B,S,C)\n          # Lightweight MLP on slots\n          slot_context = nn.relu(nn.Dense(C, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(slot_context))\n          return slot_context.reshape(B, -1)  # (B, S*C)\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM_LN_SlotPool(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Residual CNN + LN-gated ConvLSTM + slot-style pooling (S=4).\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      num_slots: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B =\
    \ x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # ConvLSTM\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state\
    \ = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Flatten spatial tokens and apply slot pooling\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B, 64, self.conv_lstm_features))  # (B,T=64,C)\n          z = SlotPooling(self.num_slots, self.conv_lstm_features)(tokens)  # (B, S*C)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  __all__ = [\"ResidualBlock\", \"ConvLSTMCellLN\", \"SlotPooling\", \"CNNConvLSTM_LN_SlotPool\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"LN-gated ConvLSTM + Slot-style\
    \ pooling (4 slots, default PG)\"\ntags: \"architecture, convlstm, layernorm, attention, slots, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"General object-like pooling: replace single-head spatial attention with a lightweight slot-style pooling (4 learned slots, one update) atop an LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch). Tests whether multi-slot summaries improve planning under the fixed budget.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_ln_slots import CNNConvLSTM_LN_SlotPool\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_SlotPool(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n\
    \          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          num_slots=4,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 64 plan:\n  - Submitted slot-style pooling (4 slots) on top of LN-gated ConvLSTM to pursue object-like summaries without domain-specific features.\n  - Running variants already in queue: dilated CNN and two-stream pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP).\n  - Will keep BASE_SEED=42 and default PG for clean comparisons. If none exceed 63.5, retain single-head baseline and focus on qualitative analysis + documentation updates.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering Sokoban rules**\n\nThe rules indicate that Sokoban-specific heuristics or extracting box or agent locations shouldn’t be used. However, I think we can utilize the given observations directly, but creating derived channels for push feasibility might be a heuristic and potentially disallowed. I’ve already noted a caution about feature engineering in public memory. Now, I’m wondering if I should update the paper with this caution without naming anyone, or just continue with the experiments and observe results. Also, we have 108k tokens used, so I think we’re safe! What should I do next?**Exploring analysis options**\n\nI can write storage analysis scripts, but evaluating them might be limited in the station environment. The last user query was routine, so I need to log my plans. I could reference the spec with exact quotes, which I've already done. Updating our capsule in the Archive about fairness and disallowed features is an option, though my work didn't\
  \ use engineered features. Running confirm replication with 42 seeds for the two-stream/dilated variants could be beneficial. Results from 109/110 are still running. While I wait, I could read through the Aether I and Cognito I archives; it's always good to stay busy!**Considering pruning and performance**\n\nThe system indicates \"Cooldown Active: You can perform another prune action in 7 ticks,\" and it’s tick 64 now. That means I can prune in 2 ticks, although it’s not critical to mention. I need to keep the meta prompt updated to reflect the new state of the art from others, but I realize that might not be allowed. Still, the research counter accepts it though the spec prohibits it. A general alternative to improve performance could be using learned object-centric representations like slot attention, which would fit the allowed guidelines!**Proposing attention methods**\n\nI can propose using Slot Attention pooling or a Perceiver IO-style cross-attention to learn object slots and plan.\
  \ This might be quite computationally heavy, but I could test a lighter variant with 4 slots and iterative refinement. Simpler alternatives could include multi-scale attention or coordinate attention gating. However, since we've discussed implementing attention with sparsemax/entmax earlier, and given our timeframe, it might be best to focus on that for now. I could also suggest a self-attention aggregator with 4 latent slots to help keep things efficient! It seems like I'll just need to keep moving with my actions.**Planning multi-action steps**\n\nI need to stick to a multi-action approach. First, I can update the meta with a caution about disallowed feature engineering and propose to disregard any results from it. Then, I should go to the Archive to update my paper regarding ethics and policy compliance, emphasizing that I've only used raw observations without any engineered features. \n\nWhile waiting for results, I think I can respond in public memory by quoting relevant spec lines,\
  \ although that might suffice. I’d also like to create code for a slot pooling variant. To manage tokens effectively, I'll remain concise while proposing a new experiment with a Slot pooling aggregator. \n\nNext, I’ll outline a network that flattens the ConvLSTM output, initializing learned slot embeddings and computing attention weights. I can implement this in the designated storage file and submit it properly with a YAML and tags. Lastly, I must ensure the code stays minimal while still implementing all necessary layers. Let’s proceed with crafting these actions!"
