parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (64ch, BASE_SEED=7)\"\ntags: \"replication, double-step, convlstm, layernorm, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Replication of the 66.2% result with double-step recurrence using a different BASE_SEED set (7) to test stability. Architecture unchanged: LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention, default PG.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step import CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n\
    \      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM width 48 (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, compute, double-step, convlstm, layernorm, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Compute-conscious ablation: reduce ConvLSTM width to 48 with double-step recurrence (to offset added temporal compute) under default PG; compare vs 64ch double-step (66.2) and 64ch single-step (≈63.5).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step import CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n\
    \          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([48]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 68:\n  - Double-step LN-ConvLSTM scored 66.2 (ID 116) — new legit improvement without domain-specific features.\n  - Submitted: replication with BASE_SEED=7 and compute-aware ablation (48ch double-step, BASE_SEED=42).\n  - Plan: if stable ≥66 across seed sets, prepare Archive revision with IDs, incl. negatives (slot pooling 46.3, two-stream 57.5, dilated 61.7, micro-sweep 61.8).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiments**\n\nI can re-run with base seed 42 for consistency. I might also want to test \"single-step with gating LN groups\" or \"learned skip-gate across time,\" aiming to minimize variable changes. Based on the 69.7 from feature engineering, I considered testing \"Compute 'push destination' channels but freeze weights,\" but that’s not allowed. I should update my private memory summary and also plan to revise the Archive paper. Replicating our double-step across fixed base seeds sets might help evaluate stability, so I could run a replicate with base_seed 7 or 123.**Preparing for experiments**\n\nI'm planning to create a new submission file for double-step replication with BASE_SEED set to 7. Since I can only run two experiments at a time and currently have none active, I'm in a good position to proceed. I'll also store the code for the double-step network I've already written, located in storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py. My\
  \ next steps will be to submit the double-step replicate, analyze cost/performance between 48 channels in double-step and 64 channels in single-step, and consider the possibility of running a triple-step instead.**Exploring experiment options**\n\nI’m considering that the current setup might be too heavy and could degrade due to compute constraints, but it seems worthwhile to test. Another option could be looking at a \"deeper value head/policy head,\" though that might not be necessary. For fairness ablation, I’ll test \"double-step with attn_temp 1.0 vs. 0.8\" as earlier standardized logits regressed. \n\nI also want to evaluate \"double-step with shared vs. unshared weights\" and resubmit with \"two-layers non-shared\" for a clean ablation. Since I can only run two experiments concurrently per agent, I should proceed with the double-step replication (base_seed=7) to confirm stability and compute-match ablation with 48 convlstm_features to optimize performance.**Planning submissions**\n\
  \nI’m considering running \"double-step with 64 channels,\" although dropout or layer normalization before attention triggers aren’t really needed. So I’ll choose to submit two experiments: the first is the \"Double-step recurrence replicate\" with BASE_SEED set to 7, and the second is the \"Double-step (48 channels) replicate\" with BASE_SEED set to 42. \n\nI’ll also update my Private Memory and optionally the Public Memory. Responding to the meta is important, and I may want to submit an Archive update later to include new results. However, I'll wait for the replicate results first. I have to remember to abide by station policies and avoid sharing code from other lineages, while still allowing for high-level discussions. Additionally, updating the results file seems like a good idea, but appending to results.csv isn’t necessary at this moment.**Coding double-step network submissions**\n\nI need to craft the code for the \"double-step network\" replicate using the existing network module.\
  \ I'll reference the network and fix the hyperparameters while setting BASE_SEED accordingly. I also want to create a submission for \"double-step width 48\" that utilizes conv_lstm_features of 48 and tune it to avoid sampling. \n\nFirst, I'll submit the replicate with BASE_SEED set to 7 with a proper title in YAML format. After that, I’ll submit the compute-match ablation for width 48 using BASE_SEED 42. Given we have no running experiments, we’re within the two concurrent limit, so everything looks good!"
