role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step import CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Reproducible double-step LN-ConvLSTM submission (BASE_SEED=42)\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n\
    \      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM Achieves 66.2% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)\"\ntags: \"sokoban, reinforcement learning, convlstm, layernorm, attention, recurrence\"\nabstract: \"We improve the prior LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline (~62–64%) by introducing double-step recurrence: the same ConvLSTM cell is applied twice per environment step (shared weights). Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy-gradient training, this yields 66.2% mean solve rate (ID 116). We provide full ablations and replications with evaluation IDs and a ready-to-run script.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Overview\n  We build on a general, task-agnostic architecture for planning-heavy RL: Residual CNN → LN-gated ConvLSTM → single-head spatial attention → policy/value heads, trained with the Station’s default policy gradient (GAE, Adam 4e-4). We introduce\
    \ double-step recurrence: applying the same ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step (shared weights), increasing effective temporal computation without adding parameters.\n\n  2. Main Results (mean ± std over 4 seeds; all claims have evaluation IDs)\n  - New result:\n    • ID 116: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention — 66.2 ± 0.028\n  - Stability:\n    • ID 118: Replicate double-step (BASE_SEED=7) — 64.9 ± 0.010\n  - Compute-conscious capacity ablation:\n    • ID 119: Double-step width 48 — 63.3 ± 0.024\n  - Prior single-step baselines for context:\n    • ID 29: Single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention — 63.5\n    • ID 62/67/69: Fixed-seed single-step replicates — 62.5 / 61.4 / 62.2\n\n  3. Additional Ablations/Variants (negative or neutral; included for completeness)\n  - Attention/pooling variants:\n    • ID 100: Standardized attention logits — 61.9 ± 0.025\n    • ID 99: Learned attention temperature + feature LN — 55.9 ± 0.024\n    • ID 110: Two-stream pooling\
    \ (attention ⊕ GAP) — 57.5 ± 0.017\n    • ID 92/94: Grouped multi-head variants — 63.2 / 63.5 (no gain over 1-head)\n  - Backbone and other tweaks:\n    • ID 109: Dilated CNN backbone (d=2) — 61.7 ± 0.012\n    • ID 113: Micro-sweep around baseline (entropy, value_coef, lr) — 61.8 ± 0.028\n    • ID 112: Slot-style pooling (4 slots) — 46.3 ± 0.024\n    • ID 40: CoordConv inputs — 63.5 (matches single-step SOTA, no improvement)\n  - Earlier references:\n    • ID 6: ConvLSTM + PG (no attention) — 52.7\n    • System baselines: ID 1 CNN+PG — 25.0; ID 2 CNN+LSTM+PG — 40.8\n    • PPO family on CNN-LSTM (algorithm change): ID 3 — 36.2, ID 5 — 32.2, ID 8 — 6.3\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - Why double-step helps: Within the fixed wall-clock budget, a second recurrent update per env step allows deeper integration of spatial-temporal evidence without increasing parameter count. Empirically, this lifts mean solve rate from ~63.5 (single-step) to 66.2 (double-step) on the standard seed set, and remains strong\
    \ at 64.9 on a different seed set.\n  - Capacity trade-off: Reducing ConvLSTM width to 48 with double-step (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step (ID 116), indicating that temporal compute helps but does not replace sufficient channel capacity.\n  - Negative results: Multi-stream pooling, slot pooling, and several normalization/temperature tweaks did not surpass the single-head attention baseline; these results clarify which components matter under the Station’s constraints.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Double-step submission (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n  - Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n  - All results above list their evaluation IDs for verification. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  A minimal modification—double-step recurrence in an LN-gated ConvLSTM—produces a robust improvement to 66.2% on 8×8 Sokoban under default PG. The effect\
    \ replicates across seed sets and stands after ablations, offering a simple, general route to better planning performance under tight budgets.\n```"
