tick: 72
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM Achieves 66.2% on 8×8 Sokoban (All Results with Evaluation IDs)\"\ntags: \"sokoban, reinforcement learning, convlstm, layernorm, attention, recurrence\"\nabstract: \"We improve the prior LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline (~62–64%) by introducing double-step recurrence: the same ConvLSTM cell is applied twice per environment step (shared weights). Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy-gradient training, this yields 66.2% mean solve rate (ID 116). We provide replications, ablations, negative results, and a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\"\ncontent: |\n  1) Method (task-agnostic)\n  - Architecture: Residual CNN → ConvLSTM with LayerNorm on gate preactivations → single-head spatial attention → policy/value heads.\n  - Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4), 4 seeds, 50M steps or 30min/seed,\
    \ batch 64, unroll 20.\n  - Contribution: Double-step recurrence — apply the same ConvLSTM cell twice per env step (shared weights). Increases temporal compute without adding parameters.\n\n  2) Main results (mean ± std across 4 seeds; evaluation IDs)\n  - New SOTA (ours, no domain-specific features):\n    • ID 116: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attn — 66.2 ± 0.028\n  - Replication stability:\n    • ID 118: Double-step (BASE_SEED=7) — 64.9 ± 0.010\n  - Capacity ablation (compute-conscious):\n    • ID 119: Double-step width 48 — 63.3 ± 0.024\n  - Single-step references:\n    • ID 29: Single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attn — 63.5 ± 0.022\n    • ID 62/67/69: Single-step fixed-seed reps — 62.5 ± 0.023 / 61.4 ± 0.015 / 62.2 ± 0.020\n\n  3) Additional ablations/variants (neutral/negative; all with IDs)\n  - Attention/pooling tweaks:\n    • ID 100: Standardized attention logits — 61.9 ± 0.025\n    • ID 99: Learned attn temperature + feature LN — 55.9 ± 0.024\n    • ID 110: Two-stream\
    \ pooling (attn + GAP) — 57.5 ± 0.017\n    • ID 92/94: Grouped multi-head variants — 63.2 / 63.5\n  - Backbone/optimizer/hparams:\n    • ID 109: Dilated CNN (d=2) + LN-ConvLSTM — 61.7 ± 0.012\n    • ID 113: Micro-sweep (entropy, v_coef, lr) — 61.8 ± 0.028\n    • ID 112: Slot-style pooling (4 slots) — 46.3 ± 0.024\n  - Earlier baselines for context:\n    • ID 6: ConvLSTM + PG (no attn) — 52.7\n    • System: ID 1 CNN+PG — 25.0; ID 2 CNN+LSTM+PG — 40.8\n    • PPO family (algorithm change, CNN-LSTM): ID 3 — 36.2; ID 5 — 32.2; ID 8 — 6.3\n    • CoordConv (single-step, LN): ID 40 — 63.5 ± 0.023\n\n  4) Discussion\n  - Double-step recurrence consistently improves over single-step (≈+2.7 points on BASE_SEED=42; ID 116 vs ID 29), and remains strong on a different seed set (ID 118).\n  - Temporal compute helps integrate spatial evidence within the fixed wall-clock constraint; reducing channels (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step, indicating capacity still matters.\n  - Attention variants beyond\
    \ a single focused head did not help under this budget, clarifying which components are essential.\n\n  5) Reproducibility\n  - Ready-to-run script (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n  - Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n  - All reported numbers include their evaluation IDs and were run under the Station’s fixed constraints. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics.\n\n  6) In-flight (not used for claims)\n  - Pending: ID 125 (double-step replicate BASE_SEED=123), ID 126 (unshared-weights double-step: two distinct ConvLSTM cells per step).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering archive updates**\n\nI’m thinking we might not need to prune things right now. We could update the SOTA reproducibility script instead. I wrote the submission script, which is a good start. Next, I should head to the Archive Room to create the revised paper, which will include table and evaluation IDs. Previously, we had issues with unverifiable ablations, but now we have many IDs. It seems better to reply to our existing capsule with the updated paper. I'm not entirely sure about the review system's requirements, though.**Understanding archive submission issues**\n\nI'm looking into the previous issue with the archive submission titled \"LayerNorm-Gated.\" It seems there was a revision made at tick 51, and maybe it's under evaluation now. The message suggests creating a new public capsule to publish, but it feels like replying in the same capsule would suffice. However, the earlier message about “Archive creation failed: Cooldown active” hints that we might\
  \ need to create a new capsule for evaluation. So, I think I should try creating a new \"Revised v2\" at Tick 72 now that the cooldown is probably over.**Planning paper components**\n\nI need to supply a title, abstract, and content for the paper. The content should include evaluation IDs for all the results I've cited, like 116, 118, and others up to 121, possibly including Krono I. While I can cite other agents as needed, it's not mandatory. The key point is that every numeric claim should link back to those evaluation IDs. I’ll create a results table, but since full text tables can be cumbersome, including ID numbers should suffice. I must remember that 125 and 126 are still pending and won't be used in claims. I'll log these updates into Private Memory to keep track.**Considering script and file creation**\n\nI think we should consider writing a plot script or a results.csv. Even though we referenced these files earlier, it would be good to actually create them for reproducibility,\
  \ even if it's optional. Having a results summary file can help others verify results. The Research Counter has all evaluation numbers, but that's not critical. I could also think about a small ablation, like \"double-step with width 64 and attention smoothing,\" but since we already have two running, adding another isn't possible. \n\nNext, I’ll head to the archive, create a new capsule with full content, and make sure the abstract mentions the IDs but not too many tags. Also, I might update Public Memory about the new SOTA, but that's not essential. Afterward, I could check the \"Submitted Evaluations\" in research to ensure there are no pending items, even though it’s not strictly necessary.**Preparing Archive Document**\n\nI need to craft the Archive document with the title: \"Double-step LN-Gated ConvLSTM Achieves 66.2% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs).\" The abstract can be longer than 100 words, but I should focus on summarizing key\
  \ components.\n\nI'll outline methods, emphasizing double-step recurrence and general architecture while disclaiming heuristics and ensuring a reproducibility path.  It's crucial to provide numeric claims with standards like ID 116: 66.2 ± 0.028 and others as needed.\n\nMy next actions are to create a YAML with the new paper, include the title in quotes, and add a note to Private Memory. I should remember that cooldown is 7, so I can wait before pruning again."
