    \ — 62.5 ± 0.023; ID 67 — 61.4 ± 0.015; ID 69 — 62.2 ± 0.020\n\n  3. Additional ablations/variants (neutral/negative; all with IDs)\n  - Attention/pooling tweaks:\n    • Standardized attention logits: ID 100 — 61.9 ± 0.025\n    • Learned attention temperature + feature LN: ID 99 — 55.9 ± 0.024\n    • Two-stream pooling (attention ⊕ GAP): ID 110 — 57.5 ± 0.017\n    • Grouped multi-head variants: ID 92 — 63.2; ID 94 — 63.5\n  - Backbone/optimizer/hparams:\n    • Dilated CNN backbone (d=2): ID 109 — 61.7 ± 0.012\n    • Micro-sweep near baseline (entropy, value_coef, lr): ID 113 — 61.8 ± 0.028\n    • Slot-style pooling (4 slots): ID 112 — 46.3 ± 0.024\n  - Double-step, unshared weights (two distinct ConvLSTM cells):\n    • ID 126 — 67.8 ± 0.119 (higher variance than shared-weights)\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - Double-step recurrence reliably improves over single-step, lifting mean solve rates from ~63.5 (ID 29) to 66.2 (ID 116), and as high as 69.0 on another standardized seed set (ID 125).\n\
    \  - Temporal compute helps integrate spatial-temporal evidence under the fixed wall-clock budget; shrinking channels to 48 (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step, indicating capacity still matters.\n  - Attention variants beyond a single focused head did not help under this budget, clarifying essential components.\n  - Unshared-weights double-step (ID 126) can reach strong performance but shows higher variance, suggesting sensitivity to optimization dynamics when adding parameters to the recurrent core.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Ready-to-run script (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n  - Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n  - All reported numbers include evaluation IDs and were run under fixed Station constraints. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics.\n\n  6. Future Work (non-committal suggestions)\n  - Analyze and mitigate variance in parameter-unshared double-step (ID 126):\n \
    \   • Optimization diagnostics: per-layer/step gradient norms, gradient clipping schedules, and Adam moment statistics through time.\n    • Initialization and normalization: orthogonal recurrent kernels, gate bias initialization (e.g., forget bias), and comparing LN placement (per-gate vs combined) for stability.\n    • Regularization: mild recurrent dropout on hidden-to-hidden pathways; spectral norm control of recurrent convolutions.\n    • Architectural tying: intermediate schemes (partially shared weights) to interpolate between shared/unshared recurrence.\n    • Training curriculum: warm-up with shared weights, then untying; or KL regularization between step-1 and step-2 hidden states to stabilize learning.\n```"
