- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_d3.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class ResidualDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 3\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(1,1),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n        \
    \  h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_D3(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Dilated residual CNN (d=3) + LN-gated ConvLSTM with shared-weights double-step\n      recurrence and single-head spatial attention pooling.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n  \
    \        # Dilated CNN backbone (d=3)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=3)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=3)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset recurrent state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              h, c = zeros(), zeros()\n          else:\n              h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              h, c = h * m, c * m\n\n          # Double-step shared ConvLSTM\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n\
    \          (h1, c1), y1 = cell((h, c), x)\n          (h2, c2), y2 = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling\n          logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y2)\n          attn = nn.softmax(logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bi,bic->bc', attn, y2.reshape((B, 64, self.conv_lstm_features)))\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n      \
    \    return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  __all__ = [\"ResidualDilatedBlock\", \"ConvLSTMCellLN\", \"CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_D3\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step_d3.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_d3 import CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_D3\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_D3(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n\
    \          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Zephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 76)\n  Published paper accepted (10/10). Pending runs: 129 (unshared+gate+FB), 130 (shared+mix-gate).\n  Prepared next test (not yet submitted due to 2-run cap): double-step + dilated CNN (d=3), BASE_SEED=42.\n  Plan:\n  - When a slot frees, submit d=3 double-step synergy to challenge 69.7 SOTA without domain-specific features.\n  - If unshared+gating stabilizes variance, replicate across seeds; else stick with shared double-step baseline.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring analysis options**\n\nI'm considering proposing some qualitative analysis code, like plotting attention maps and gate stats. There’s an optional test() function, but it doesn’t support GPU. I wonder if we can write tools for post-hoc analysis, though they would need to run within the evaluation setting. The test() function runs without training, which isn't helpful. Maybe we can prepare code for attention map extraction, but we can't fetch environment frames. I might also think about an Archive update to include results from IDs 125 and 126.**Considering reviewer suggestions**\n\nI'm thinking about addressing the reviewer's suggestion regarding the variance of the unshared double-step. We've already proposed gating and forget-bias tests that are currently running (IDs 129, 130). To improve efficiency, I might write down a final plan and ensure we comply with \"no domain-specific features.\" \n\nI see potential actions, like checking research rankings and previewing\
  \ the archive list. I should also document our results in Private Memory, prepare the \"submission_sota_double_step.py\" as 'submission_double_step.py,' and consider creating an aggregator script for the results.**Evaluating API limitations and results**\n\nIt looks like we don't have an API to fetch data automatically other than using \"review ID\". We could still review new runs after they finish. The Station test environment allows us to get full logs with \"/execute_action{review evaluation_id}\", so reviewing logs for IDs 125 and 126 might be helpful, even though we already have them in System Messages.\n\nI noticed that the new synergy from Krono at d=3 dilation scored 69.4, while the double-step synergy scored 68.1. There's potential to combine the two, but we must avoid direct code copying. We can propose our variant with d=3 residual blocks since it's allowed within general architecture, and we can adapt our dilated module easily!**Assessing current scores and submission limits**\n\
  \nIt looks like the scoreboard currently shows that Nomos has a score of 69.7 with feature engineering, while dilation 3 scored 69.4, and our replicate is at 69.0. So, to beat the state-of-the-art (SOTA), we might need to combine double-step with d=3 dilation. However, we need to be careful not to interfere with our currently running experiments, which are IDs 129 and 130. Once one of those completes, we can submit more. For now, I can prepare the code storage for the dilated d=3 double-step network and write the module 'cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated3.py,' but I won't submit it just yet. I think writing in storage is allowed at the Research Counter.**Planning code updates and experiments**\n\nI'm realizing we can go ahead and create code in storage. One idea is to add a gradient clipping schedule to the training step since we’ve changed it before for PPO. A custom training step with gradient clipping could lower variance, which might help with the unshared double-step variance.\
  \ However, we should only change one variable at a time since we're waiting on the gating experiments. \n\nI can use Private Memory to outline our plan and hold on Token Management since we’re at 40%. I also see that the \"help\" section suggests previewing all papers from the archive; while I’ve seen some already, I might still check them out. \n\nThere’s also the option of replying to our published capsule with a post-publication note, though it might not be necessary. I’ll prepare an outline to summarize everything and incorporate actionable commands as needed using YAML format. \n\nNext, I want to focus on implementing the dilated d=3 double-step network with the 'cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_d3.py' file while preparing 'submission_d3_double_step.py'. We might want to look into updating the meta prompt based on the new published status and plan out our next steps while keeping the output concise. I believe it’s best to hold off on submissions for now due to our current experiments.\
  \ \n\nSo, I’ll generate commands for these actions, summarize the meta protocol, and ensure everything gets stored correctly."
