parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_separable.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN_Separable(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      LN-gated ConvLSTM with separable 3x3 gating:\n        concat = [x, h]\n        1x1 bottleneck -> depthwise 3x3 -> 1x1 to 4*features -> LayerNorm -> gates\n      This reduces conv FLOPs while keeping spatial coupling.\n      \"\"\"\n      features:\
    \ int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.5  # fraction of concat channels before 3x3\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry  # (B,H,W,C)\n          concat = jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)           # (B,H,W,Cx+Ch)\n          in_ch = concat.shape[-1]\n          proj_ch = max(16, int(self.bottleneck_ratio * in_ch))  # >=16, Python int\n\n          # 1x1 bottleneck\n          y = nn.Conv(proj_ch, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(concat)\n\n          # Depthwise 3x3: feature_group_count = channels for true depthwise\n          y = nn.Conv(proj_ch, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                      feature_group_count=proj_ch,\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n\n          # Pointwise to gates\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n\
    \n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_Separable(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Residual CNN -> ConvLSTM (double-step shared) with separable gating -> 1-head attention.\n      Goal: lower per-step compute vs standard double-step while preserving benefits.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.5\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x);\
    \ x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              h, c = zeros(), zeros()\n          else:\n              h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              h, c = h * m, c * m\n\n          # Double-step with shared separable cell\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN_Separable(self.conv_lstm_features, bottleneck_ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)\n          (h1, c1), y1 = cell((h, c), x)\n          (h2, c2), y2 = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Attention pooling\n\
    \          logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y2)\n          attn = nn.softmax(logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bi,bic->bc', attn, y2.reshape((B, 64, self.conv_lstm_features)))\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  __all__ = [\"ResidualBlock\", \"ConvLSTMCellLN_Separable\", \"CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep_Separable\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage\
    \ write zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step_wide_cnn.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step import CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Explore wider CNN backbones under the double-step recurrent core.\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32, 64]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64, 128]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNConvLSTM_LN_DoubleStep(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\
    \n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 85 prep (waiting on 151/152):\n  - Implemented separable-gated double-step (1x1 bottleneck -> depthwise 3x3 -> 1x1 to gates). Goal: reduce per-step FLOPs while preserving spatial coupling; should help under 30-min cap.\n  - Prepared wide-CNN sweep for double-step (CNN 32/64 vs 64/128) to test if spatial width + temporal compute can close gap to 72–76 without feature engineering.\n  - Next actions when a slot frees:\n    • Submit separable-gated double-step (bottleneck_ratio∈{0.33,0.5,0.66}).\n    • Submit double-step + wide CNN sweep.\n  - If neither helps, consolidate guidance: prefer dilation+wide CNN for top ceiling; double-step remains strong with standard CNN when compute-bound.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering collaboration and experimentation**


  I’m looking at the collaboration rules, and it seems replicating others'' work can serve as a baseline for our experiments, but we shouldn''t exploit code or domain heuristics. Right now, I have two experiments running, which means I can’t submit more until one finishes. I wonder if I should explore the Reflection Chamber for possible reflective actions or consider updating the Archive. It’s important to prepare the code for depthwise separable gating to reduce costs before submitting when there’s a free slot.**Exploring gating normalization**


  I''m planning to implement a partial-shared double-step "bottleneck-wise" gating normalization, like comparing GroupNorm to LayerNorm since LayerNorm performed best previously. I noticed an error from the "astype" call on a float constant, but that''s fixed now. I''m considering a base model that uses "every-2-step" gating, allowing the second convLSTM step to compute only if a learned halting gate exceeds a threshold. It seems tricky under JAX due to training restrictions, but there''s potential for effective gating if I can reuse h and c values.**Enhancing compute efficiency**


  I''m exploring ways to improve compute efficiency by using separable depthwise convolution for gating, followed by a 1x1 conv to produce gates. There''s a feature_group_count parameter in the flax.linen.Conv that might help, though I''m uncertain about the environment''s shape (B, H, W, C). Setting features as 4*F with the same count as input channels could work, but depthwise conv reduces cross-channel mixing, which might affect performance. I could also reduce convLSTM features from 64 to 48. Alternatively, "partial frame stacking" of the last k embeddings may help enlarge the planning horizon. However, adding a FIFO buffer to include previous embeddings could provide tempo information more efficiently.**Exploring new network implementation**


  I''m considering implementing a network that utilizes a 4-frame temporal convolution across channels. This could work similarly to temporal convolution without using convLSTM, which seems to fit within our planning architecture. I''m wondering if this might open a new experimental track, but since we have a two-run limit, I can prepare the code for later. Also, I''ll update my Private memory for planning, while waiting for results from previous runs. Meanwhile, I could work on refining depthwise-separable gating variants.**Planning network and storage updates**


  I''m looking into combining double-step gating with reusing ''y'' from step 1 for step 2 in the gating convolution. It seems like we could make a linear adjustment to approximate this, but it''s a deviation from typical architecture. I''m interested in adopting "pre-activation gating" with LayerNorm for input x. Potential increases in feature width suggest testing configurations like c1=64 and c2=128 for our double-step could yield better results, although it''s compute-heavy. I’ll write storage files for both the network and submission with specific features while ensuring we adhere to the two-run cap and prepare plans in Private memory for future steps.'
