thinking_content: "**Exploring CNN improvements**\n\nI’m considering heading to the Archive Room to preview the new capsule (#6 by Nomos I). There could be insights about dilations beyond 4 and synergy. The scoreboard indicates the SOTA has improved to 76.2 with a configuration that suggests simple widening of the CNN may yield significant gains. I wonder if our wide CNN approach, when paired with the double-step, might achieve similar results, despite previous underwhelming synergy experiments.**Reviewing evaluations and archives**\n\nI can inspect the code for evaluation 147 to confirm our approach, although I can't access others' 'other lineage storage' code. I do have permission to review evaluation 147 using the command /execute_action{review 147}, as that’s allowed for finished evaluations. I should check on evaluations 155 and 156 soon; they might still be running, but they started at tick 86, so they should wrap up by the next tick. Meanwhile, I can preview Krono's archive (#6) or\
  \ read its full content. Using /execute_action{preview 6} or /execute_action{read 6} works too, though reading does consume tokens.**Updating plan and actions**\n\nI need to update Private Memory with our plan since the user didn't ask anything specific; the last message was about station info, so we should take action. Proposed actions include:\n\n- Going to research.\n- Reviewing evaluations 155 and 156, but they aren't finished yet. The review command requires completed evaluations.\n- I'll review 147 to examine the SOTA code, though the code for 139 isn’t accessible. I’ll use /review 147 for that.\n- Next, I’ll preview the archive #6 by Nomos for insights. Optionally, I can read it for more details.\n- While I wait, I can log a “diagnostic” update to check throughput and latency, using \"train_single.py\" with optional parameters.**Considering evaluation options**\n\nI’m thinking about implementing a 'test()' function to print parameter counts and FLOPs approximations, but it only\
  \ runs on CPU without training. This submission would count as an evaluation without being training, yet it occupies an evaluation slot. Since we have a limit of two concurrent runs, that would be wasteful. Instead, I could just prepare a script for future use.\n\nAnother option is to move to the Reflection Chamber, but I might just continue reviewing. I should also ensure that our wide CNN double-step code accommodates the increased compute, since the 'embedding width' might slow updates.**Planning our next steps**\n\nI'm considering whether our wide CNN with double-step might underperform, but we still need to test. I could think about additional ablation strategies, like gradient clipping schedules or 'temporal sub-sampling' leaps, but those seem heavy.\n\nFor now, I’ll review evaluations 147 and 138, and preview archive 6. I’ll write a brief comment in the archive about compute constraints and new SOTA, but I shouldn’t overdo updates there. Regarding the private summary, I’ll keep\
  \ it minimal. \n\nMy final actions will include going to research, reviewing evaluations, and previewing the archive. Let's keep it efficient!"
