- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n\
    \          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class DilatedSotaNet(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation: int = 4\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          # Pure dilation backbone (dilation applied in both convs inside residual blocks)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x);\
    \ x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)(\n              (rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x\n          )\n\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)\n         \
    \ attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape((B, -1)), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', h_out, attn.reshape((B, H, W)))\n\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n\n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  __all__ = [\"DilatedResidualBlock\", \"ConvLSTMCellLN\", \"DilatedSotaNet\"]\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate: Pure-dilation CNN (d=4) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (no feature engineering)\"\ntags: \"replication, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Replication of the strong pure-dilation baseline: residual blocks with both convs dilated (d=4), single-step LN-ConvLSTM, attention pooling, default PG. Tests whether dilation alone (no engineered features) achieves\
    \ ≥70 as reported by others.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Intentionally omit BASE_SEED to use time-based default (matches some prior strong runs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=4,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Tick 90:\n  - ASPP + single-step underperformed (53.3). Submitted a clean replication of pure-dilation (d=4) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (no feature engineering) to reconcile our earlier gap vs reported 73.8.\n  - Running: gated-dilation inside double-step core (ID 161). If ≥69, will replicate across seeds; else conclude spatial RF > temporal compute under 30-min cap.\n  - If replication succeeds, add to appendix as a verified baseline; if it fails, document mismatch and possible causes (optimizer parity, throughput, seed sensitivity).\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating CNN and ConvLSTM**


  I''m reviewing an evaluation that suggests a combination of d=4 CNN with a double-step approach. It seems like they didn’t discuss engineered features, so I’m unsure if this used their baseline dataset. My plan is to evaluate the double-step LN-ConvLSTM with a wider CNN, but so far it''s only at 62.7, possibly due to slower updates affecting performance. I''ve also tried methods like dilated gating but haven’t seen significant improvements. What else could I consider? Perhaps explore different pooling methods or a better training algorithm, though fixed unroll length is a limitation.**Exploring experiments and documentation**


  I’m considering that the “single-head” optimum we found earlier still holds. I think it’s good practice to propose a reflection and finalize an Appendix in the Archive to summarize negative results. However, I also want to run at least one more targeted experiment with “dilated gating.” We previously tried forget-bias, and now I’m wondering if we can add it to the shared double-step. Though SOTA uses domain-specific features we can''t adopt, writing a Public Memory note about compliance boundaries might be risky but valuable. Finally, I could produce a systematic ablation paper analyzing the negative results and synergy. Let’s also record a Private Memory and update the Archive with a scoreboard and guidance.**Managing tokens and experiments**


  I’m looking at token management, where I’ve currently used 49%. We pruned earlier, and since we’ve had 8 ticks since the last prune at tick 82, I think we''re due for another round. I can prune heavy ticks like 77-81 and 83-86, but I want to maintain some. My plan is to summarize in Private first and then prune. I also want to run a small experiment with “Double-step LN-ConvLSTM” plus gating dilation and AdamW with gradient clipping. I can only adjust to AdamW in the same submission by changing the create_optimizer function.**Evaluating different models**


  I’m considering running the "single-step LN-ConvLSTM" with a dilation of 4, replicating model 138, but without any feature engineering. That model achieved a score of 73.8, which gives me hope we can replicate it if allowed. We previously tried "Double-step synergy d=4" and got a lower score of 64.9. The single-step LN-ConvLSTM with d=4 had around 60, which is significantly worse than 138. I think I might have written a normal CNN with dilation in the residual blocks, which could be a problem.**Considering model replication and collaboration rules**


  I’m diving deep into our model replication strategy. I noted that our "ResidualDilatedBlock" has both un-dilated and dilated convs, while examples like 138 use dilated convs only, achieving a higher score of 73.8. It''s puzzling that we can''t replicate their success given that our configurations are similar. I''m contemplating whether we can import their code since the guidelines allow reading from other lineages. However, there''s ambiguity around reusing code and sharing it directly. I think it might be allowed to test their architecture without cross-lineage sharing. I’ll aim to implement exactly what they have without advanced features and test if we can match their results!**Planning replication and implementation**


  I’m realizing that using their code is acceptable for creating a baseline for our next paper, but I want to maintain our own path as well. We should propose a replicate of "Single-step LN-ConvLSTM with pure dilation=4" matching model 138. I''ll use our file "cnn_convlstm_ln_dilated_pure.py" and structure it exactly as theirs, with two dilated residual blocks and no feature engineering. Speaking of actions, I need to manage tokens and consider pruning earlier heavy ticks. Then, I can implement the necessary classes, ensuring we follow their approach closely while avoiding unnecessary complexity in our submission process. Let’s create this code and ensure everything is concise!'
