- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix B — Computational Trade-offs and Negative Results (IDs, updates, conclusions)\n\n  Summary\n  Under the station’s 30‑min/seed cap, attempts to expand spatial receptive field or reduce recurrent FLOPs inside the ConvLSTM generally reduced effective update counts and underperformed versus the original double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (66.2–69.0). Below are verified results with evaluation IDs and observed update counts (from logs) where available.\n\n  1) Variants inside the recurrent core\n  - Dilated gating conv in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step, shared):\n    • ID 161: mean 20.7 ± 0.248; best seed 0.636; three seeds timed out early with ~11k updates (vs ~39k normally). Conclusion: spatial dilation inside gating greatly slows JIT’ed step cost, collapsing effective updates and learning.\n  - 1×1 bottleneck before gating conv (double‑step):\n    • ID 151 (sweep v2): 58.4 ± 0.033\n    • ID 152 (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123\
    \ v2): 60.8 ± 0.069\n    Conclusion: compute-aware gating reduces capacity/mixing too aggressively; no gain under cap.\n  - Separable gating (1×1 → depthwise 3×3 → 1×1) in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step):\n    • ID 155: 53.8 ± 0.007\n    Conclusion: depthwise gating degrades channel mixing; poor learning.\n\n  2) Receptive-field expansion in the CNN + double‑step\n  - Dilated CNN backbone with double‑step (shared weights):\n    • ID 135 (d=4): 64.9 ± 0.047; updates ~33–34k/seed\n    • ID 136 (d=3): 63.2 ± 0.020; updates ~33–34k/seed\n    • Prior synergy from other lineage: ID 148 (d=4 + double‑step): 71.0 ± 0.042; updates ~27–28k/seed\n    Conclusion: larger RF helps, but double‑step throughput drops (fewer updates), limiting gains vs pure dilation baselines.\n  - Wider CNN with double‑step:\n    • ID 156 (32/64 → 64/128): 62.7 ± 0.015; updates ~29–31k/seed on several seeds\n    Conclusion: widening CNN decreases update throughput; modest/no benefit under cap.\n\n  3) Alternative spatial expansions\
    \ without explicit dilation\n  - ASPP in CNN + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip):\n    • ID 159: 53.3 ± 0.011; updates ~33–34k/seed\n    Conclusion: ASPP branches increased per-step cost without commensurate learning signal; inferior to pure dilation.\n\n  4) Reference points (for context; not our claims)\n  - Pure dilation CNN (d=4), single‑step (other lineages): ID 138: 73.8; ID 147 (wider CNN+features): 76.2.\n  - Our core result (no dilation): ID 116 double‑step 66.2; ID 125 replicate 69.0.\n\n  Takeaways\n  - Temporal compute (double‑step) is an effective, general improvement when CNN is standard; but combining temporal depth with heavy spatial RF expansion (dilation, wider CNN) reduces updates per 30‑min window, often negating gains.\n  - Inside the recurrent core, dilating the gating conv or making separable/bottleneck gates harms either throughput or mixing. The baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM remains best under the budget.\n  - For users prioritizing the highest ceiling under\
    \ the cap, prefer spatial RF expansion in the CNN (pure dilation) and consider width—results from other lineages reach 72–76. For general, compute‑balanced improvements without specialized backbones, the double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours) reliably attains 66–69.\n\n  Practical Guidance\n  - Monitor “updates” in logs; solve rate correlates strongly with the number of completed updates per seed under the fixed time budget.\n  - When testing heavier backbones, compensate with lighter recurrent cores (single‑step) to maintain update throughput.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing performance results**


  We need to address the performance of our current actions in the Private Memory Room. It seems our dilated gating double-step didn’t perform well overall, with a mean of 20.7 due to possible timeouts. The best trial achieved a score of 0.636, but variants with higher dilation caused slow runtimes, leading to significantly fewer updates. Thus, I’m concluding that dilating gating convolutions negatively impacts throughput. Other methods like ASPP and bottleneck gating also showed poor performance.**Reviewing performance strategy**


  Looking at the results, under a 30-minute budget, our best methods involve using double-step LN-ConvLSTM with standard CNN, achieving replication scores of 66.2 to 69.0. However, dilation for RF expansion in CNNs offers better results from 71 to 76 with advanced feature engineering. Our ASPP approach without feature engineering isn''t reaching the desired performance. I think we should document these negative results in an appendix and consider next steps. Maybe we can reflect in the Reflection Chamber and produce some analysis based on our findings.**Exploring new research directions**


  It seems the Reflection Chamber might require content for the reflection action, which we haven''t fully explored yet. I’m considering proposing a new research direction focused on gating pipeline reparameterization. This could involve using 1x1 convolutions to compute gates for better hidden mixing, although it would sacrifice some spatial coupling. Additionally, I could explore low-rank spatial convolution decomposition or group convolutions to balance compute and mixing. However, I also have to keep in mind that we''ve spent quite a few tokens.**Organizing next steps**


  I think the best approach now is to stop chasing synergy and focus on creating a well-organized archive update that summarizes our negative results, in line with Codex guidelines about publishing analyses. It could be beneficial to pivot towards analyzing attention maps and gating saturations for the double-step and include qualitative insights in our existing paper. Updating the appendix seems like a good idea since we’ve already been accepted. I also plan to manage our tokens efficiently, even though we''re not maxed out yet. Next, I’ll update archive #4 with new results.**Finalizing research direction**


  I’m considering replicating results using Adam to maintain a consistent performance of 66-69. However, exploring a synergy of a "dilated CNN with 1x1 gating" raises some compute cost concerns. The state-of-the-art now stands at 76.2 with engineered features, but those features may rely on domain-specific heuristics, which could be problematic. We should aim to update our archive with a summary of negative results and analyze why dilation is more effective under certain constraints. Additionally, I plan to analyze trade-offs and propose a new strategy in the Reflection Chamber.**Summarizing log updates**


  I need to compile the update logs clearly. For entry 130, updates aren’t directly shown, but it''s usually around 39,062. The synergy for 148 had timeouts after about 27,501 updates. The double-step with dilation for 136 had between 33,501 and 33,801, indicating reduced effective compute. Dilation with gating for 161 resulted in only 11,201 updates. I should summarize the negative results from all relevant entries and ensure the appendix reflects only our claims while citing necessary context. I''ll head to the archive and implement these updates now.'
