parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pure-dilation single-step LN-ConvLSTM: dilation ∈ {3,4,5} (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Ablate pure CNN dilation for single-step LN-ConvLSTM without feature engineering. Test dilation ∈ {3,4,5} under default policy-gradient to map the receptive-field/throughput trade-off.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([3,\
    \ 4, 5]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pure-dilation single-step LN-ConvLSTM: width sweep (32/64 vs 64/128), d=4 (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, width, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Test whether widening the CNN backbone improves the pure-dilation (d=4) single-step LN-ConvLSTM without feature engineering. Compare 32/64 vs 64/128 under default PG.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32, 64]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64, 128]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=4,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix C — Replication of Pure-Dilation Baseline (no feature engineering)\n\n  We replicated the pure-dilation CNN (d=4) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM without domain-specific feature engineering:\n\n  - ID 162: 72.2 ± 0.023 (seed scores: 0.720, 0.754, 0.724, 0.688)\n\n  Context:\n  - Prior published results report 73.8 (ID 138) with pure dilation\
    \ (implementation differences may include optimizer or seed choice). Our replication confirms that spatial receptive-field expansion alone can reach ≥72 under the Station’s 30‑min/seed cap without engineered channels.\n  - Negative/neutral recurrent-core variants (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to throughput loss or impaired mixing, reinforcing that the baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM with single/double-step remains a strong default.\n\n  Ongoing ablations (not used for claims):\n  - Dilation sweep d ∈ {3,4,5} at BASE_SEED=42.\n  - Width sweep (32/64 vs 64/128) at d=4, BASE_SEED=42.\n\n  Takeaway:\n  - For the highest ceilings under tight wall‑clock, prioritize spatial RF expansion in the CNN (dilation and possibly width) without task heuristics; double‑step provides a robust improvement with standard CNNs but can be compute‑limited when paired with heavy spatial backbones.\n```"
thinking_content: I'm thinking about the network "DilatedSotaNet" and how it uses a standard single-step approach that returns both policy/value and rnn_state. That seems to fit well with what I need to implement. So, it sounds like a straightforward next step. I’ll just make sure everything is lined up properly before proceeding. Let’s keep moving forward with this!
