tick: 94
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Reproducible pure-dilation (d=5) single-step LN-ConvLSTM submission\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n\
    \          dilation=5,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate: Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (BASE_SEED=7)\"\ntags: \"replication, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Replication of the pure-dilation baseline with dilation=5 and single-step LN-ConvLSTM under default PG to test stability across a new BASE_SEED.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n\
    \          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=5,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate: Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (BASE_SEED=123)\"\ntags: \"replication, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Second replication of the pure-dilation baseline with dilation=5 and single-step LN-ConvLSTM under default PG, using BASE_SEED=123.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure import DilatedSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n      \
    \    'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=5,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix D — Dilation Sweep and Width Ablation (fixed-seed, no feature engineering)\n\n  New results:\n  - ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): Pure-dilation single-step LN‑ConvLSTM with dilation ∈ {3,4,5} → best at d=5: 72.5 ± 0.024.\n  - ID 167 (BASE_SEED=42): Width sweep at d=4 (32/64 vs 64/128) → 48.8 ± 0.079. Throughput collapsed (≤28k updates on several seeds), explaining the regression.\n\n  Context with prior:\n  - ID 162: d=4 replicate (time-based seed) → 72.2 ± 0.023.\n  - Published: ID 138 (d=4) → 73.8; optimizer/seed differences likely account\
    \ for the gap.\n  - Negative: Heavy recurrent tweaks (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to slower step cost or impaired mixing.\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Spatial receptive-field expansion via pure CNN dilation (d≈4–5) is a robust, task-agnostic path to ≥72 without engineered channels.\n  - Widening the CNN at the same time budget can reduce effective updates and hurt performance; under a 30‑min cap, keep the backbone lean when using heavy RF expansion.\n\n  Reproducibility:\n  - Scripts: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (d=4, BASE_SEED=42) and submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (d=5, BASE_SEED=42).\n  - Replications in progress (not used for claims): d=5 with BASE_SEED=7 and 123.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 94:\n  - Dilation sweep (ID 166) shows d=5 > d=4 at BASE_SEED=42 (72.5). Width sweep at d=4 collapsed updates (ID 167: 48.8).\n  - Submitted replications for d=5 at BASE_SEED=7\
    \ and 123; added d=5 reproducibility script.\n  - Guidance shaping up:\n    • Highest ceilings under 30 min: pure CNN dilation (d≈4–5), single-step LN-ConvLSTM.\n    • Double-step remains strong with standard CNNs but loses throughput when paired with heavy RF expansion.\n    • Avoid recurrent gating modifications (dilated/separable/bottleneck) under this budget.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering reproducibility script**\n\nI’m thinking we could present a reproducibility script for single-step dilation, possibly with d=5 since the current submission_dilated_singlestep.py uses d=4. It might be a good idea to add a variant for d=5 and propose replication across seed sets to ensure stability, like running base seeds 7 or 123. Also, I'm noting our token management suggestions: usage was moderate at 119k at tick 94, and they pruned earlier with a cooldown of 7 ticks.**Implementing action items**\n\nI need to implement several actions. First, I'll go to the archive and reply to capsule #4 to add Appendix D with new results: ID 166 shows 72.5 (d=5 is the best), while ID 167 results in 48.8, which is negative due to throughput. I should also update takeaways, noting that d=5 yields the highest at BASE_SEED=42 with width being not beneficial.\n\nNext, I may store a reproducible script for d=5, writing it as zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py\
  \ and importing our network while setting dilation to 5 with BASE_SEED=42.\n\nI could then propose two replicates for d=5 using different BASE_SEEDS to test stability: \"Replicate d=5, BASE_SEED=7\" and \"Replicate d=5, BASE_SEED=123.\" Although there are no running experiments currently, it's acceptable to submit these two replicates since each will run 4 seeds. Lastly, I should also update Private Memory with this plan.**Considering feature engineering limitations**\n\nRight now, I’m noticing that other lineages have achieved scores like 76.2 and 73.8 through feature engineering, while our chosen path avoids domain-specific heuristics. However, I wonder if the features we’re using might still be deemed domain-specific? The advanced feature engineering uses push destination channels calculated from observation channels, which could contradict the guidelines.\n\nThe specifications state that Sokoban-specific heuristics, including rules for action sequences or detecting deadlocks, are not\
  \ allowed. Extracting box or agent locations is also prohibited, yet those details are included in environmental observation channels. It's tricky because while we're not externally extracting info, calculating things like push destination maps could still be considered domain-specific. The Research Task allows general planning architectures, but likely won’t permit what we’re doing with those derived channels. Interesting to note that despite this, other lineages seem to have used similar methods and been accepted.**Planning compliance actions**\n\nI need to prioritize compliance and make sure we avoid any non-compliant features. It's important to confirm our new ablations don’t use any feature engineering, and we need to react to results accordingly. I plan to propose that \"dilation d=5 single-step LN-ConvLSTM seems best\" and make it our top baseline.\n\nNext, I will finalize the paper addendum with these ablations and suggest next steps, like adding a small memory feature, such as\
  \ single-head attention with temperature tuning, though we’ve struggled there with a 55.9 result. \n\nI’ll outline actions: go to the archive to reply with appendix \"D — Dilation sweep & width ablation,\" write a script called 'submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py' for reproducibility, and potentially test \"d=5 replicate seeds 7 and 123.\" Then, I'll submit \"Replicate d=5, BASE_SEED=7\" and \"Replicate d=5, BASE_SEED=123,\" and remember to add these to my private memory plan.**Creating submission script**\n\nI’m planning to rank score in Research Counter to check the scoreboard, though it might not be necessary. I want to keep responses concise and ensure we adhere to YAML quoting rules, especially if content includes colons. Using code fence boundaries is acceptable for our submission.\n\nI’ll create a reproduction script using the network file 'cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py'. The file path will be zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py, setting\
  \ BASE_SEED to 42.\n\nI'll define hyperparameters to return fixed choices but will only set dilation via the network param, specifically dilation=5. For running replicates, I'll plan to submit two: \"Replicate: Pure dilation d=5 (BASE_SEED=7)\" and \"Replicate: Pure dilation d=5 (BASE_SEED=123).\" \n\nTo stay within two-run concurrency, I’ll execute several actions: write the script with the relevant code, submit the YAML for BASE_SEED=7, do the same for BASE_SEED=123, and then go to the archive to reply with appendix details. I’ll also update private memory, noting that while we could compute update counts, it may not be needed.\n\nI have to be cautious with YAML quoting for titles with parentheses and ensure to quote titles when necessary. Let's get the minimal code crafted for 'submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py' while retaining the same hyperparameters!"
