    \ pooling (4 slots),46.30,0.024,negative\n  116,Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (64ch) + 1-head Attn,66.20,0.028,main (ours)\n  118,Double-step replicate (BASE_SEED=7),64.90,0.010,replicate\n  125,Double-step replicate (BASE_SEED=123),69.00,0.046,replicate\n  130,Shared double-step + learned mix of step outputs,67.80,n.a.,variant\n  135,Double-step + Dilated CNN (d=4),64.90,0.047,throughput trade-off\n  136,Double-step + Dilated CNN (d=3),63.20,0.020,throughput trade-off\n  140,Double-step + AdamW+clip (micro-sweep),66.50,0.037,optimizer variant\n  141,Double-step + AdamW+clip (BASE_SEED=60608),60.20,0.019,underperformed\n  151,Double-step + 1x1 bottleneck gating (sweep v2),58.40,0.033,negative\n  152,Double-step + 1x1 bottleneck (rep v2, BASE_SEED=123),60.80,0.069,negative\n  155,Double-step + separable gating,53.80,0.007,negative\n  156,Double-step + wide CNN (64/128),62.70,0.015,updates dropped\n  159,ASPP + single-step LN-ConvLSTM,53.30,0.011,negative\n  161,Double-step + dilated gating\
    \ conv,20.70,0.248,heavy regression (timeouts)\n  162,Pure-dilation d=4 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM,72.20,0.023,RF expansion (no features)\n  166,Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM,72.50,0.024,RF expansion (no features)\n  167,Width sweep at d=4 (64/128),48.80,0.079,updates collapsed\n  170,Pure-dilation d=5 replicate (BASE_SEED=7),73.40,0.027,replicate\n  171,Pure-dilation d=5 replicate (BASE_SEED=123),72.80,0.019,replicate\n  174,Pure-dilation d=5 micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42),73.00,0.027,entropy 0.012 best\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix G — Final Results CSV and Reproducibility Index\n\n  We provide a consolidated CSV with all key experiments, evaluation IDs, mean±std (when available), and brief notes:\n  - storage/zephyr/results/final_results.csv\n\n  Reproducible scripts:\n  - Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n  - Pure-dilation d=4 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py\n\
    \  - Pure-dilation d=5 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py\n  - Pure-dilation d=5 (best hparams, entropy=0.012): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5_best.py\n\n  Practical guidance (confirmed):\n  - Highest ceiling under 30‑min/seed: pure-dilation CNN (d≈4–5) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (≥72 without feature engineering).\n  - Double-step remains a robust, general improvement with standard CNNs (66–69), but pairing with heavy spatial RF (dilation/width) reduces updates and limits gains.\n  - Gating modifications inside ConvLSTM (dilated/separable/bottleneck) harm throughput or mixing under the budget.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 99:\n  - Wrote consolidated results CSV (storage/zephyr/results/final_results.csv) and linked it in Archive Appendix G.\n  - Pending: moderate width 48/96 at d=5 (ID 177). Expect slight throughput drop; will keep if ≥73, otherwise\
    \ recommend 32/64.\n  - Next steps: pause heavy runs; finalize figures/tables only. If time allows, small entropy fine-sweep (0.011–0.013) at d=5, else close out with current stable recipe.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering final results and plans**\n\nI’m thinking about adding a final results table to summarize our findings, but it looks like plotting isn’t implemented yet. I could create a reproducible script for the pure dilation variant using the best hyperparameters, and that’s set. \n\nI might also write code to compute throughput based on log updates to correlate with performance, though we don't have full log access. Reviewing token management shows 141k used out of 270k, but it’s not critical right now. Next, I could propose writing a “closing” or “outlook” section.**Planning next steps and actions**\n\nI’m considering proposing to mail other agents, but I know code sharing is forbidden. However, high-level discussions are allowed, so perhaps I could explore that. The Public Memory Room might be accessible now since it has matured, and I’m wondering whether to wait for the eval 177 result.\n\nMeanwhile, I could implement the figure script or finalize the archive. Adding\
  \ \"Appendix G — Final consolidated results table\" with evaluation IDs, means, and seeds sounds like a good idea, although it could be lengthy. Adding a concise summary table in markdown would be beneficial, along with storing results in a CSV file.**Preparing to store results**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing to write a CSV file to storage that includes the results we have, like IDs and various conditions. This way, we can ensure everything is organized and easily accessible. Once that's done, we can update the archive to include the \"results file path.\" It feels like a simple yet effective way to keep track of everything and makes it easier for future reference. Let’s get started on this plan!**Compiling evaluation results**\n\nI want to compile the previously known evaluation IDs and their respective scores. Here's what I have:\n\n116: 66.2 ± 0.028 (Double-step LN ConvLSTM)\n118: 64.9\n125: 69.0 ± 0.046\n135: d=4 + double-step: 64.9 ± 0.047\n136: d=3 + double-step: 63.2 ± 0.020\n\
  151: 58.4 ± 0.033 (bottleneck v2)\n152: 60.8 ± 0.069\n155: 53.8 ± 0.007\n159: 53.3 ± 0.011 (ASPP)\n156: 62.7 ± 0.015 (wide CNN double-step)\n160: 72.2 (control: AdamW d=5)\n162: 72.2 ± 0.023\n166: 72.5 ± 0.024 (best d=5)\n170: 73.4 ± 0.027\n171: 72.8 ± 0.019\n174: 73.0 ± 0.027\n100: 61.9\n109: 61.7\n110: 57.5\n112: 46.3\n94: 63.5\n92: 63.2\n40: 63.5\n29: 63.5\n62: 62.5\n69: 62.2\n3: 36.2\n5: 32. \n\nI think this will be useful for further analysis!**Compiling CSV and planning actions**\n\nI need to compile the evaluation IDs and scores into a final CSV file for storage, including essential details like IDs, methods, means, and standard deviations. I can create this file at `storage/zephyr/results/final_results.csv` and link it in the archive as \"Appendix G — Results CSV\" to keep everything organized.\n\nWe should write the data with minimal columns to keep it clean. I’ll ensure to format the content correctly and add a note to private memory about waiting for experiment 177 to complete.\
  \ Let's implement these actions!"
