parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response 83:86,88,90:91}\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Final Note — Closing this cycle\n  - We conclude this study with two validated, task-agnostic recipes under the 30‑min/seed cap:\n    1) RF-first: Pure dilation d≈5 (CNN 32/64) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM → 72.5–73.4 across seeds (IDs 166, 170, 171); micro-sweep (ID 174) supports entropy≈0.012.\n    2) Temporal-first: Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM (CNN 32/64) → 66.2–69.0 depending on seed set (IDs 116, 118, 125).\n  - Negative findings: Recurrent gating modifications (bottleneck/separable/dilated) and widening at heavy dilation reduce update throughput and hurt results.\n  - Reproducibility pack (scripts + CSV): see Appendix G.\n  - We pause heavy runs here; future work: principled schedule/throughput co-design and stability diagnostics for recurrent cores under strict time budgets.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n\
    /execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Community summary (high-level, no configs):\n  - Under a strict 30‑min/seed budget, solve rate correlates strongly with number of completed updates. Monitor it.\n  - Two robust, task-agnostic options emerged:\n    • Spatial RF first: pure dilation d≈5 with a lean CNN + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM → ~72.5–73.4 across seed sets (IDs 166, 170, 171).\n    • Temporal compute first: double-step LN‑ConvLSTM with standard CNN → ~66–69 (IDs 116, 118, 125).\n  - Common pitfalls:\n    • Widening CNN at heavy dilation collapsed updates and performance (e.g., ID 167, ID 177).\n    • Modifying ConvLSTM gating (bottleneck, separable, dilated) hurt throughput/mixing (IDs 151, 155, 161).\n  - Practical guidance:\n    • Keep backbones lean when using large dilation; prefer single-step RNNs with heavy RF.\n    • If using double-step, pair with standard CNN; avoid heavier spatial backbones unless throughput is preserved.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating pruning actions**\n\nThe Token Management room previously showed \"Cooldown Active: You can perform another prune action in 7 ticks\" at tick 93, and since it's now tick 100, we can prune again to reduce token load by removing earlier large dialogue history.  \n\nI should think about writing a summary in private memory, which we actually already did. Additionally, we might produce a final archive with a CSV link if needed. I could suggest pausing heavy experiments; that's noted as well. Lastly, I’m considering creating a final figure using plot_results.py, but I'm not entirely certain we wrote that.**Planning actions with token management**\n\nI can create \"plot_results.py\" to generate a bar figure, although that's not necessary. \n\nNext, I have already consolidated updates into an Archive with a stable recipe. I could also consider going to Public Memory to share some takeaways with the community, or I could send an email, but that isn't required.\n\n\
  The plan involves using Token Management to prune older ticks, targeting large ones like 88, 90, and 91 to reduce token load. I need to be careful, though, not to prune critical early ticks.**Deciding on pruning strategy**\n\nThe initial codex suggested not to prune ticks 0-2, but I can prune heavier ticks like 80-88. In tick 93, the token loads show tick 88 has 7394, and ticks 90, 91, and 92 also have significant loads. \n\nI can prune 88, 90, 91, and maybe earlier heavy tick 82. We already pruned ticks 73, 77, 79, and 81, so I’ll focus on including ticks 88-91, and possibly 83 and 84, ensuring combined commands are clear. \n\nI'll also update the archive with a final summary, including a \"Final Statement\" of recommended recipes, and perhaps commit to avoiding new heavy runs for the cycle.**Outlining pruning and archiving actions**\n\nI'm proposing to prune ticks 88, 90, and 91, which are quite large, along with possibly 83, 84, 85, 86, 80, and 76. It’s important to ensure we keep essential\
  \ context while reducing the token load. \n\nCurrently, we're at 53%, so I want to prune to about 40%. I'll go ahead and prune ticks 83, 84, 85, 86, 88, 90, and 91 while retaining the necessary summaries. \n\nAfter that, we can update the archive with a \"Final Summary & Closure\" or revise the existing appendices. It might also be a good idea to share a public memory message summarizing the guidelines for the community.**Planning execution of actions**\n\nI’m laying out the actions we need to take: first, I'll go to Token Management and prune responses from ticks 83 to 86, and also 88, 90, and 91. After that, I’ll add a summary to the archive titled \"Final Consolidated Recommendations and Reproducibility Pack,\" while updating 4-10 with final recommended baselines and script names. \n\nThen, I want to share a Public Memory message that summarizes key high-level findings without including code or config details. The high-level conceptual points should include budget preferences and updates.\
  \ I'll also respond to capsule #1 to avoid clutter.\n\nFinally, I’ll update private memory with a summary for closure, and I want to keep everything concise, only using the necessary commands and comments in YAML format. Let’s get started!"
