- text: "/execute_action{review 166}\n/execute_action{review 167}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pure-dilation d=5 single-step LN-ConvLSTM: LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, hyperparams, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm\"\nabstract: \"Tune optimizer/entropy settings for pure-dilation (d=5) single-step LN-ConvLSTM under default PG to close the gap to top scores without feature engineering. We sweep LR ∈ {3e-4,4e-4,5e-4}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.01,0.012}, vcoef ∈ {0.45,0.5,0.6}.\"\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Optional, Tuple\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3e-4, 4e-4, 5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.008, 0.01, 0.012]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.45, 0.5, 0.6]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n\
    \          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(\n              self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(\n              self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(\n              4 * self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(jnp.concatenate([x,\
    \ h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class DilatedSotaNet(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation: int = 5\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # Dilated CNN backbone (both layers and residual blocks share same dilation)\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1,\
    \ dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset recurrent state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda C: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, C), dtype=x.dtype)\n              h, c = zeros(self.conv_lstm_features), zeros(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          else:\n              h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              h, c = h * m, c * m\n\n          # Single-step ConvLSTM\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h2, c2), y = cell((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling\n\
    \          logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)  # (B,8,8,1)\n          attn = nn.softmax(logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bi,bic->bc', attn, y.reshape((B, 64, self.conv_lstm_features)))\n\n          # Policy/Value heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n\
    \          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=5,\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating research options**\n\nThe task clarifies that there's no domain-specific features, but general FE might be allowed. Others have used FE without going into domain-specific heuristics. I've got ideas for our contributions involving LN-gated ConvLSTM, and new results should be archived. I might create a new paper or add to the existing one with a section on pure dilation results. Also, we could explore the synergy between pure dilation and double-step, especially since we previously saw variable outcomes.**Exploring model enhancements**\n\nI’m considering moving the attention pooling head to global average pooling or adding a 1x1 conv MLP to combine features. However, previous attempts with \"Two-stream Attn ⊕ GAP\" scored poorly at 57.5, so maybe we shouldn't pursue that. I might propose \"curriculum gating\" using d=3 for early steps and d=5 later. While the training code is fixed, using a time-based schedule could work. Implementing a gating dilation schedule\
  \ with two conv layers might allow for flexibility, which is more in line with our goals.**Assessing computational strategies**\n\nI’m looking at the implications of using different static convs, which could double the compute cost due to gating. While this might slow things down, it could still fit within our 30-minute cap. We've done expensive synergy tests before, so I’m wondering if we could use dilated separable conv in the CNN backbone to reduce compute while widening the receptive field. \n\nAlternatively, we could complete a width sweep for dilated d=4 with 64/128, but the previous score of 48.8 feels suspiciously low. I suspect our code may not have produced the right architecture, and I'm considering conducting a learning rate sweep or tuning the entropy coefficient for better results.**Planning micro-sweeps**\n\nI want our approach to be thorough yet token-savvy, so I'm adding two micro-sweeps for adjustments. I’ll explore learning rates at 2e-4, 4e-4, and 6e-4, along with entropy\
  \ values of 0.008, 0.01, and 0.012, plus varying the value loss coefficient from 0.4 to 0.6. \n\nIt seems like there's been impressive progress with others achieving 76.2 through increased CNN features. Our ID 167 had a disappointing score of 48.8, so I’ll check the logs to see what went wrong, starting by reviewing the logs for that and ID 166 to understand the best dilation choice.**Reviewing and proposing new experiments**\n\nI’m thinking about storing our results in private memory and possibly submitting more experiments for \"pure-dilation d=5\" with 64/128 width. First, I need to fix the previous width experiment, but I can't find the network file \"networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.\" There might be some confusion about earlier submissions too.\n\nReviewing logs for ID 166 and 167 is vital to debug. I might also suggest experimenting with dropout in the CNN and injecting tile-coded time embedding. Let's issue actions to review those logs and explore width\
  \ adjustments, especially considering previous scores.**Finalizing submission actions**\n\nI plan to take several steps in sequence: first, I'll review the logs, then propose a micro-sweep submission, and update the archive. It’s important to include the \"DilatedSotaNet\" code in the submission to enhance reliability, so I'll avoid importing it.\n\nWe'll keep key parameters in mind, like setting 'dilation' to 5 and preparing a compact Flax network that maintains our architecture. After issuing the review commands for logs, a micro-sweep with combinations of hyperparameters will follow, along with necessary updates to the archive.**Preparing for submission**\n\nI'm getting ready to execute a few actions: first, I’ll review the logs for both actions 166 and 167. After that, I'll submit my results with some structured YAML.\n\nThe YAML will include a title for our micro-sweep, six relevant tags, and an abstract summarizing the tuning of hyperparameters. I’ll clearly set the network to a\
  \ fixed dilation of 5 and include all relevant hyperparameters. Also, I should prepare an archive reply to summarize the results but can hold off on that for now to save tokens. Let's finalize this."
