parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pure-dilation d=5 single-step LN-ConvLSTM: attention temperature sweep (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm, attention\"\nabstract: \"Ablate attention temperature for the strong pure-dilation (d=5) single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Sweep attn_temp ∈ {0.8, 1.0, 1.2} with default PG to test stability vs. peak solve rate.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Optional, Dict, Any\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'attn_temp': tune.choice([0.8, 1.0, 1.2]),\n      }\n\n  class\
    \ ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 5\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4,\
    \ axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class DilatedSotaNet(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n      dilation: int = 5\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x);\
    \ x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda C: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, C), dtype=x.dtype)\n              h = zeros(self.conv_lstm_features); c = zeros(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          else:\n              h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              h, c = h*m, c*m\n          (h2, c2), y = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n          logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                           kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          attn = nn.softmax(logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bi,bic->bc', attn, y.reshape((B, 64, self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n\
    \          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          attn_temp=float(hparams['attn_temp']),\n          dilation=5,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 105:\n  - d=5 single-step baseline is solid (IDs 166/170/171/174 ≈72–73). LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep (ID\
    \ 182) underperformed (62.5).\n  - Submitted targeted ablation: attention temperature sweep {0.8,1.0,1.2} on d=5 to test stability/peak solve rate. Running concurrently with compute reallocation (ID 185).\n  - Next: if attn_temp improves, replicate; else finalize guidance for non-FE track (recommend d=5 with default temp, moderate width).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating optimization settings**\n\nI’m looking at defaults to customize the optimizer, but I’m unsure about some details. We need to focus on one variable, which is good. Right now, in the Private Memory Room, we can run two experiments at a time. There’s one running, so we can submit another. I think the new variant with a parameter sweep could yield better results, but I see some clarifications regarding feature engineering; it might be limited by specs.**Refining the plan**\n\nThe station mentioned that general planning architectures are allowed, but not domain-specific heuristics, like Sokoban-specific knowledge. When it comes to \"feature engineering,\" I think it may refer to additional input channels or derived features, but I want to be cautious and avoid any specific feature engineering path. \n\nOur plan is to submit an attention temperature micro-sweep at around 0.8, 1.0, and 1.2 to see if 0.9 improves results. We might also test the value loss coefficient\
  \ or entropy with d=5 to explore further improvements.**Exploring training and modules**\n\nI’m considering proposing a schedule for entropy annealing and customizing the training step to decay the entropy coefficient linearly. However, I need to stick with the default submission format to avoid errors, especially since model-based reinforcement learning and imitation are forbidden. \n\nI also want to ensure concurrency, so we'll run one last attention temperature micro-sweep using base d=5. I should check if I have the module 'networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure' in storage since we used it before, but I need to verify if that file exists in our previous submissions.**Clarifying module usage**\n\nI remembered that we wrote about 'aspp' and 'double_step_dilated_gating,' but not 'single_step_dilated_pure.' However, since evaluation 166 succeeded, that means the module is present and can be reused for the attention temperature. \n\nBut wait, does DilatedSotaNet accept\
  \ the attention temperature parameter? I previously implemented it inline during micro-sweep 182 instead of importing it from storage. For consistency, I think we should define a create_network function that includes the attention temperature parameter to avoid any errors in the code.**Crafting submission commands**\n\nI plan to include the network class inline again to ensure the attn_temp parameter is accessible. I’ll implement a minimal network class within the submission content, which is allowed, and I won’t provide a training step for this. \n\nI’ll set hyperparameters to choose from attn_temp values of [0.8, 1.0, 1.2], using a baseline learning rate of 4e-4, entropy of 0.01, and dilation of 5. For now, we're ready to craft the commands for the submission."
