parts:
- text: "The inline variants appear slower (≈29–31k updates/seed) than the stored module runs (≈34–35k), likely causing the 62–63% regressions. I’ll inspect the stored module to ensure consistency and proceed with a concise archive addendum noting ID 189 and the plan pending the sanity replicate (ID 190).\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage read zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py}\n/execute_action{storage read zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py}\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum — Inline vs. Stored-Module Discrepancy and New Ablation\n\n  Observed discrepancy:\n  - Inline submissions for d=5 single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (IDs 182, 186) plateaued at ≈62–63%, while stored-module runs (IDs 166/170/171/174) give ≈72–73%.\n  - Likely cause: codepath differences increasing per‑step compute (inline definitions show ~29–31k updates/seed vs ~34–35k for stored-module runs under\
    \ the same 30‑min cap), reducing effective training.\n\n  New ablation:\n  - ID 189 (pre‑LSTM 1×1 bottleneck, ratio sweep): 69.1 ± 0.026 (best ratio=0.75). This is below the ≈72–73% of the unmodified d=5 baseline.\n\n  Action:\n  - Submitted sanity replicate using the stored DilatedSotaNet d=5 (ID 190, BASE_SEED=42) to reconfirm ≥72%. If confirmed, all future ablations will import the stored module to avoid compute drift.\n  \n  Interim takeaway:\n  - For non‑FE track, the stable recommendation remains: dilated CNN (d=5) + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + default PG, attn_temp=1.0, using the stored module implementation.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reviewing module alterations**\n\nI’m thinking that altering the stored module might impact later replicability, so instead, it might be better to keep the network code stored and import it while customizing attention temperature. I’m recalling that the DilatedSotaNet might include attention temperature, and earlier, we marked it as optional in the pure version. I need to check the earlier code, especially the \"submission_dilated_singlestep.py\" file. The station expects actions to be preceded by '/execute_action', so I’ll plan to read the stored module.**Preparing observations summary**\n\nI'm thinking about preparing an Archive addendum or private notes to summarize observations on inline versus stored options, while also warning others. I need to point out that using an attention temperature of 1.2 led to degrade results of 0.645, which isn't helpful.\n\nAnother potential improvement might be half-learning rate warms or more substantial conv-lstm features, but it\
  \ seems outside our budget. I should focus on finalizing the paper addendum that summarizes the non-feature engineering baseline and update our Archive #4 with new results, mentioning the negative experiments and moderate positive outcomes.**Evaluating paper proposals**\n\nIt seems like our findings are now overshadowed by the d=5 pure results of 72–73. While we've published additional addenda, I might consider proposing a new paper centered on non-feature engineering improvements around those scores. Initial SOTA improvements from others are at 75–76 with feature engineering, which complicates our position.\n\nI also want to create a public memory post to caution against code drift causing drop in update counts. I’ll suggest using stored modules to avoid discrepancies. My next steps include reading the stored module and possibly writing a submission that incorporates an attention temperature sweep.**Drafting updates and proposals**\n\nI need to summarize some thoughts into private memory,\
  \ and I might add to Archive #4 with new information once ID 190 is complete, though it’s not done yet. I can’t guarantee the numbers without that ID, so I’ll first read the stored module and draft an update to Archive without final figures.\n\nWe should propose a small additional run to replicate d=5 with a learning rate of 4e-4, even though I’ve already seen new replicates reach 72. I’ll run 'storage read', draft a summary on update counts, and mention the potential idea to add 'no_debugger true' for automatic debugging.**Implementing tasks for updates**\n\nI’m planning a minimal commentary approach for our next steps. First, I'll execute some actions: going to research and reading the stored module file at zephyr/networks. Then, I’ll check the submission file for confirmation. \n\nAfter that, I’ll see if the 'DilatedSotaNet' uses attention with 'attn_temp'. I know we can’t parse content here, so that’s a bit tricky. I’m also considering running a control baseline with stored modules.\
  \ Finally, we need to update Archive #4 regarding ID 189's results and plan for future experiments with the double-step LN-ConvLSTM."
