thinking_content: "**Evaluating module structures**\n\nI’m considering consolidating the differences between the \"stored module\" and \"inline\" definitions by rewriting inline definitions to match the stored module’s structure. I want to ensure we use the stored module for replicates, and with new architectures, it might be best to import the baseline and wrap or keep complexity minimal. Since the station rules don’t allow for Sokoban-specific heuristics, that’s good to know. I’m pondering updating the Archive with the latest SOTA, especially since some reports indicate feature-engineered CNNs are outperforming our non-FE track.**Planning next steps**\n\nRight now, evaluations are in progress, and I’m thinking about proposing the next experiment to run once slots open up. Maybe something like a \"d=5 single-step LN-ConvLSTM with bottleneck residual at a 0.66 ratio and SiLU\" could work, perhaps lowering CNN features to maintain throughput. I also want to create a ready-to-run script for\
  \ the d=5 baseline for reproducibility. A quick code check shows that we're consistent across both stored and inline implementations of the attention mechanism. Next, I’ll wait for evaluations 191/192 before recording results, potentially creating an archive addendum.**Assessing next actions**\n\nI can run a \"rank score\" to see the top scores and check for any performance beating the current SOTA. However, I want to keep my responses concise and focus on actionable steps. I don't need to check the running experiments right now since they'll finish soon, but I could set the page size to something manageable. \n\nI can review evaluation ID 189 for more detail, since I have logs from earlier. Looking into a new experiment is tempting; maybe I could suggest a fine-grained bottleneck ratio sweep around 0.75, but I need to consider the complexity and remember that experiments 191 and 192 are still running, preventing me from submitting new ones. So, I’ll write that code for storage for future\
  \ use!**Preparing new network module**\n\nIt seems better to write a new network module and a submission script, but not submit it just yet. The \"submit\" action leads to an evaluation, and with our capacity limits, I want to avoid potential errors. Each agent can only run two simultaneous experiments, but I can still submit—it might just queue up. To play it safe, I'll write to storage instead. \n\nI need to prepare the submission script like \"submission_d5_bottleneck_ratio_fine.py\" or \"d5_bottleneck_residual_silu.py.\" I should also note in the Archive that the stored module replicate 190 yielded a 72.9 score, confirming the baseline. I’ve planned to switch to the stored module for all ablations since we already added an addendum before. Additionally, I can update Public Memory to inform others at a high level about using the stored module to avoid compute drift.**Using collaboration rules carefully**\n\nI need to be cautious about the collaboration rules, which allow high-level\
  \ discussions but not sharing code. I’ll start by noting my plans in private memory. I'll create a submission file named \"submission_d5_bottleneck_residual_sweep_v2.py\" for a bottleneck ratio sweep without submitting it now due to the two-run limit. Additionally, I might consider drafting \"analysis of compute drift\" and \"standardization guidelines\" in private memory.\n\nNext, I should set the \"rank score\" to observe the current best scoreboard across authors, even though it’s not essential. Since the last analysis message indicated that replicate 190 used a dilation of 5, I can propose making the module more compute-efficient while aiming for high performance.\n\nNow, I need to provide brief reasoning in my assistant message, detailing confirmations and next steps. My actions will include:\n\n- Adding a short note to the archive that includes the ID 190 result and states that all subsequent ablations will use the stored module.\n- In private memory, I'll outline the plan for when\
  \ experiments 191 and 192 finish to replicate if the ratio 0.66 with ReLU or SiLU exceeds the baseline.\n- I’ll prepare a new submission script for the fine-grained bottleneck ratio to run next.\n\nFinally, I can write the next planned submission code to storage as \"submission_d5_bottleneck_ratio_fine.py\" without submitting it just yet. Let's implement the content accordingly and summarize our next steps while keeping the formatting simple."
