    \ wide CNN + smaller LSTM)\n\n  Pending replications:\n  - ID 194: bottleneck ratio=0.5, ReLU (BASE_SEED=7) — running\n  - ID 195: bottleneck ratio=0.5, ReLU (BASE_SEED=123) — running\n\n  Note:\n  - Inline-defined variants showed fewer updates/seed (~29–31k) vs stored-module (~34–35k), explaining 62–63% regressions; use stored modules for stable throughput.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Organizing results and submissions**


  I’m considering producing code to store a results.csv file and a plotting script. We may have created this before, but a quick aggregator to read top research results could be helpful. While there''s no direct API to fetch scoreboards, I can compile results in private memory. I can summarize the improved SOTA and note pending replications. The tip suggests keeping messages concise, so I’ll do that. It looks like we have enough tokens, and I’ll prepare to submit once slots are free.**Planning archive revisions and testing**


  I’m thinking about pre-creating an archive revision template using source_private compile. The "Swift Submission from Private Memory" content lets us move items from private to archive using a private capsule ID, which is super helpful. I’ll update Private Memory with a structured results table that references existing IDs. I need to ensure we reduce unverified content. Additionally, I can post a concise note in public memory about the ID 191 improvement. For testing, I want to restrict changes to one variable at a time, focusing on LSTM features.**Considering testing and documentation**


  I''m exploring the idea of testing small attention temperature with a d=5 bottleneck network. Previously, using pure dilation gave us a result of 62.5, which might be slower. I think it''s better to focus on documentation for now and prepare an archive update only after the replications finish. I''ve added a comment about the running replications and ID 191 results. Since we can’t run more than two experiments at the same time, maybe I’ll take a moment to reflect and plan. I''m allowed to read Archive #7, which features a different lineage about ''Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN''.**Finalizing actions and updates**


  I realize we can read Archive #7 to check for domain-specific heuristics, especially since it discusses a ''bottleneck CNN'' similar to ours. They might include feature engineering, which could be valuable to know. Additionally, we can share a high-level result update in ''Public Memory'' regarding ID 191. I need to focus on producing a "final answer" that’s action-oriented with minimal commentary. I’ll keep messages concise, following previous patterns that include text, /execute_action commands, and YAML code.**Planning action steps**


  I’m organizing my tasks: First, I’ll go to the Archive Room to read Capsule 7 for insights that might inspire replicates. Next, I’ll log a concise plan in Private Memory. While experiments are running, I can''t submit, but I can mention that the ''fine sweep'' is prepared. I could also generate a script to export our improved network module for reproducibility. I want to prune older ticks and ensure we only present verifiable claims. Then, I’ll implement the necessary actions to read and write summaries efficiently.'
