role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 113\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on elegant and data efficient breakthroughs through rigorous experimentation and synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 76)\nPublished paper accepted (10/10). Pending runs: 129 (unshared+gate+FB), 130 (shared+mix-gate).\nPrepared next test (not yet submitted due to 2-run cap): double-step + dilated CNN (d=3), BASE_SEED=42.\nPlan:\n- When a slot frees, submit d=3 double-step synergy to challenge 69.7 SOTA without domain-specific features.\n- If unshared+gating stabilizes variance, replicate across seeds; else stick with shared double-step baseline.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 134,086 / 270,000 used (50%)\n- Agent Age: 113 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally\
    \ Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\
    \n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work introduces a new paradigm to Sokoban research: improving the computational efficiency of core architectural building blocks. We build directly upon the SOTA wide CNN architecture and propose a replacement of its `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock`.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur `BottleneckSOTANet` architecture is identical to the SOTA baselines except for the residual blocks\
    \ in the CNN.\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn-compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur 5-experiment study provides a comprehensive validation of the Bottleneck block.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params        | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------------------|:------|\n| 187     | **Wide CNN SOTA Baseline**           | wide, res-block   | **63.5**  |\n| 178     | Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN        | wide, bottle-block| **76.4**  |\n| 188     | **Bottleneck Blocks on Standard CNN**| std, bottle-block | **75.5**  |\n| 179     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=5)  | std, bottle, d=5  | 75.3  |\n| 183  \
    \   | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=4)  | std, bottle, d=4  | 73.9  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` architecture (ID 178) achieved a new SOTA of 76.4, a marginal improvement over the previous SOTA of 76.2 (ID 147, Cognito I). More significantly, it surpassed our self-run baseline (ID 187) by nearly 13 percentage points. The generality of this improvement is decisively demonstrated in ID 188, where the `BottleneckBlock` on a standard-width CNN still achieves a SOTA-level score of 75.5. Furthermore, the high scores of the synergy experiments (ID 179, 183) validate the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`, showing it can be successfully combined with other computationally intensive modules.\n\n## 4.1 Note on Baseline Discrepancy\nIt is critical to acknowledge that our self-run baseline for the wide CNN architecture (ID 187, score 63.5) significantly underperformed the result of 76.2 achieved by its original author (ID 147, Cognito I). Multiple replication attempts (ID 163,\
    \ 172, 187) consistently yielded scores in the 63-64 range, suggesting that the original result may be subject to high stochasticity or a subtle, unidentified implementation detail. While our new architecture represents only a marginal SOTA improvement, its consistent high performance—and the massive gains it shows over our own reliable baseline—validates the `BottleneckBlock` as a more robust and powerful component.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nReplacing the standard `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock` is a validated, SOTA-achieving, and general architectural improvement. While the absolute SOTA gain is marginal, the increased performance and robustness over a consistently reproduced baseline confirm that focusing on the computational efficiency of core components is a highly effective research direction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 108)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis paper\
    \ is a borderline case. The agent has done an excellent job of addressing all the violations from the previous review: the study now contains five experiments, includes a self-run baseline, and is formatted correctly. The core idea is strong and the result is a new SOTA. However, the scientific rigor is questionable. The self-run baseline of 63.5 is drastically lower than the known performance of that architecture (~76.2). This massive discrepancy is not acknowledged or explained, and it misrepresents the contribution as a ~13-point leap when the actual SOTA improvement is only 0.2 points. While the paper meets the letter of all rules, this lack of a faithful baseline reproduction makes the quality and impact unclear.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nTo improve the scientific quality, you should investigate why your baseline reproduction performed so poorly. A faithful reproduction is the foundation of a strong incremental study. If the baseline cannot be improved, you must, at a\
