parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Fine sweep: Bottleneck-dilated d=5 (ratio ∈ {0.60,0.66,0.72,0.75}) × {ReLU, SiLU}\"\ntags: \"ablation, bottleneck, dilation, cnn, convlstm, layernorm\"\nabstract: \"Fine-grained sweep around the best bottleneck configuration for non-FE d=5 single-step LN-ConvLSTM. We scan bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.60, 0.66, 0.72, 0.75} and activation ∈ {ReLU, SiLU} under BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck import DilatedBottleneckSotaNet\n  from ray import tune\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n\
    \          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.60, 0.66, 0.72, 0.75]),\n          'use_silu': tune.choice([False, True]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedBottleneckSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=5,\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          use_silu=bool(hparams['use_silu']),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)\"\ntags: \"sokoban, architecture, dilation, bottleneck, convlstm, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1)\
    \ improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG. We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Motivation and setup\n  - Non-FE track: no engineered channels; default PG (Adam 4e-4, entropy 0.01, vcoef 0.5); batch 64 envs, unroll 20; 30-min/seed cap; 4 seeds averaged.\n  - Architectural baseline: dilated CNN (d=5) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64) with single-head attention pooling.\n  - Change: replace residual blocks with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) to increase compute-efficiency per update.\n\n  2. Main results (mean ± std; all with evaluation IDs)\n  Baseline sanity (stored module, non-FE d=5):\n  - ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n  - ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n  - ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n  - ID 174 (micro-sweep, 42): 73.0\n  - ID 190 (sanity replicate, 42): 72.9\n\n  Bottleneck-dilated residuals (ours):\n  - ID 191 (ratio\
    \ sweep, 42): 73.6 (best ratio=0.5; ReLU)\n  - ID 192 (activation ablation, 42): 73.3 (ReLU > SiLU)\n  Replications of best config (ratio=0.5, ReLU):\n  - ID 194 (BASE_SEED=7): 74.5\n  - ID 195 (BASE_SEED=123): 74.8\n\n  3. Negative/neutral controls\n  - ID 189: pre-LSTM 1×1 bottleneck (reduce input into LSTM) — 69.1 (best ratio=0.75); hurts credit assignment.\n  - ID 182: LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep on d=5 — 62.5; deviating from defaults regresses under cap.\n  - ID 186: inline attention-temp sweep — 62.5; inline path showed slower updates/seed (compute drift).\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - Bottleneck-dilated residuals consistently improve the non-FE d=5 baseline by ~+0.7 to +2.0 points across seed sets, peaking at 74.8 (ID 195).\n  - ReLU edges SiLU in this regime (ID 192).\n  - Implementation stability matters: stored-module runs reach ~34–35k updates/seed; inline variants often run ~29–31k and underperform.\n  - Position vs. broader SOTA: contemporary wide/bottleneck CNN works (e.g.,\
    \ IDs 178=76.4; 196=77.0 by other lineages) set the current ceiling; our non-FE d=5 + bottleneck path narrows the gap while keeping recurrent compute modest.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Network modules: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py (baseline), storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck.py (ours).\n  - Fine sweep script prepared: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_d5_bottleneck_fine.py (to be run when slots free).\n  - Results summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  - A simple, general change — bottleneck-dilated residuals — improves the non-FE d=5 dilated CNN + single-step LN-ConvLSTM from ~72–73 to ~73–75 under standard PG, verified across seeds with full IDs. This provides a stronger, compute-efficient baseline and a reproducible stepping stone toward higher-capacity SOTA variants.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Tick 113:\n  - Replications confirm improvement: bottleneck-dilated d=5 + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (ratio=0.5, ReLU) hits 74.5 (ID 194) and 74.8 (ID 195) vs d=5 baseline ~72–73.\n  - Submitted fine sweep (ratios {0.60,0.66,0.72,0.75} × {ReLU, SiLU}) at BASE_SEED=42.\n  - New archive capsule created with full IDs and reproducibility paths; positioned against broader SOTA (IDs 178=76.4, 196=77.0).\n  - Next: if fine sweep yields ≥75 consistently, attempt minimal recurrent tweaks (e.g., tiny pre-attn MLP) via stored-module path, then finalize consolidated paper update.\n```"
