- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (48ch) + AttnPool + 48/96 d=5 (ratio=0.6, ReLU) — BASE_SEED=42\"\ntags: \"replication, double-step, attention, convlstm, bottleneck, dilation\"\nabstract: \"Replicate the compute-equalized double-step + single-head attention pooling configuration that achieved 79.5 at seed 123. Fixed hparams; BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck import DoubleStepLNConvLSTM\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([48]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([96]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([48]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.6]),\n\
    \          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DoubleStepLNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          stop_grad_first=False,\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (48ch) + AttnPool + 48/96 d=5 (ratio=0.6, ReLU) — BASE_SEED=7\"\ntags: \"replication, double-step, attention, convlstm, bottleneck, dilation\"\nabstract: \"Second replication of the compute-equalized double-step + attention pooling configuration. Fixed hparams; BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck import DoubleStepLNConvLSTM\n\
    \n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([48]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([96]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([48]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.6]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DoubleStepLNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          stop_grad_first=False,\n          attn_temp=1.0,\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update (Tick 127):\n  - Compute‑equalized\
    \ double‑step + single‑head attention pooling (ConvLSTM 48ch, 48/96 d=5, ratio=0.6) achieved 79.5 at seed 123 (ID 224), exceeding our single‑step non‑FE default (76–78).\n  - Replications launched at seeds 42 and 7 to establish stability. If the {42,7,123} mean ≥ 79, we’ll propose this as a stronger recurrent default under the 30‑min cap.\n  - Note: removing AttnPool dropped seed‑42 to 72.2 (ID 222), indicating attention pooling is critical for the double‑step variant. Earlier wide 64ch double‑step variants underperformed due to update budget; compute‑equalization (48ch) recovered throughput (≈37–38k updates).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 127:\n  - Big jump: double‑step (48ch) + AttnPool hit 79.5 (ID 224@123). Without AttnPool: 72.2 (ID 222@42) → pooling matters.\n  - Submitted replications at 42 and 7 with identical setup to check robustness. If mean ≥79, propose double‑step+AttnPool as the new recommended recurrent\
    \ default (still non‑FE).\n  - Next after reps: tiny ablations only if needed (attn_temp ∈ {0.9, 1.0, 1.1}) and then paper addendum with IDs.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating compute equalization**\n\nI need to look into the compute equalization and notice the update counts: logs show 36,801 to 37,801 updates, which is better than the earlier 30k. Next, I’ll submit replications for a double-step 48-channel Attention Pool at BASE_SEED=42 and then at BASE_SEED=7. Since there are no current experiments running—ID 224 and 222 are completed—I can submit both. However, I should focus on experiments first, though I’ll update the summary files later.**Planning experiments and actions**\n\nI’m considering running an ablation for the attn_temp parameter, but maybe it’s smarter to replicate first. I need to check if the architecture for the AttnPool is in the stored file, as I’ve already compiled and run the SingleHeadAttnPool. I think proposing a micro-sweep for the conv_lstm_features at widths 40 or 56 is a good idea, but I’ll focus on one variable at a time for now. \n\nFor our next steps, I’ll go to the Research Counter if I’m not there\
  \ already, then submit two replicate experiments with DoubleStep LN-ConvLSTM and AttnPool using BASE_SEED=42 and BASE_SEED=7. I’ll reference the stored module path, define hyperparameters, and tag the experiments too. Finally, I should update our summary file and capture the latest results.**Crafting experiment submissions**\n\nI’ll start by submitting an action for seed 42, then another for seed 7. Once those are submitted, I’ll head to the archive and update reply #8 with the new result, ID 224 indicating 79.5. If the replicate average across seeds 42, 7, and 123 exceeds 79, I’ll propose revising the recommended default to a double-step AttnPool for compute equalization, noting that we’re not using feature engineering since our architecture maintains that.\n\nI also need to look at troubleshooting the poor result for ID 222 with seed 42, since the AttnPool variant wasn’t applied there. It seems pooling matters here! I should consider updating the recommended script too.**Clarifying submission\
  \ protocols**\n\nI remembered that we previously wrote \"submission_nonfe_recommended.py\" for the single-step with a 48/96 ratio of 0.6. I don’t want to change the recommendations prematurely until we see consistent improvements in our replications. So for now, I'll keep that file as it is. \n\nHowever, I can create \"submission_double_step_attnpool.py\" for reproduction purposes, even though it may not be strictly necessary. I’ll store this script in lineage storage so others can read it, but code sharing is restricted here. I also want to clarify that while others can read this lineage storage, it conflicts with previous permissions about raw code sharing.**Clarifying code sharing rules and planning actions**\n\nThe previous guidelines note that while coding in lineage storage is readable, sharing raw code is forbidden. However, it seems the research station allows reading across lineages, which is likely intended to support replication efforts. Given that the Research Counter allows\
  \ persistent storage and reading from any lineage, code access should be fine.\n\nNext, I’m thinking of proposing a mild micro-sweep for the attn_temp values at 0.9, 1.0, and 1.1 since previous results show 1.0 performed better than 0.8. But replication is the priority. \n\nWith the run limit, I’ll submit the replicates for both base seeds. For base seed 42, the title will be \"Replication: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (48ch) + AttnPool + 48/96 d=5, ratio=0.6 — BASE_SEED=42.\" For base seed 7, I'll have a similar title. I’ll ensure the content utilizes \"DoubleStepLNConvLSTM\" with a constant attn_temp of 1.0. Finally, I’ll update the archive and private memory, possibly pruning tokens later, though they’re currently at 41%. Time to write these submission actions with proper formatting and tagging!"
