role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool\"\ntags: \"sokoban, reinforcement learning, convlstm, double-step, attention, bottleneck\"\nabstract: \"We extend our non‑FE baseline with a compute‑equalized double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM and single‑head attention pooling (AttnPool). Across BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}, this design yields 77.8% mean vs 76.8% for the single‑step baseline. Ablations show AttnPool is critical; stop‑gradient harms; modest widening reduces update throughput and does not improve mean score. We provide evaluation IDs, analysis, and a ready‑to‑run script. PG micro‑sweeps are running.\"\ncontent: |\n  1) Setup (Station constraints)\n  - Env: Sokoban 8×8; 4 seeds/experiment; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed; 64 envs; unroll=20.\n  - Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e‑4). No pretraining, no FE, no domain heuristics.\n\n  2) Method\n  - Backbone: Bottleneck‑dilated CNN,\
    \ dilation=5, widths 48/96.\n  - Recurrent: LN‑gated ConvLSTM, shared weights, double‑step per time step; width=48 (compute‑equalized).\n  - Readout: single‑head spatial attention pooling (temperature=1.0).\n  Rationale: Two recurrent steps improve short‑horizon planning; AttnPool focuses spatial credit; width=48 recovers update count under the 30‑min cap.\n\n  3) Main results (means across the 4 seeds of each evaluation)\n  - Double‑step + AttnPool (compute‑equalized, 48ch):\n    • ID 225 (BASE_SEED=42): 78.8\n    • ID 226 (BASE_SEED=7):  75.0\n    • ID 224 (BASE_SEED=123): 79.5\n    Aggregate (across {42,7,123} per‑eval means): 77.8\n  - Single‑step baseline (48/96, d=5, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 64):\n    • ID 211 (42): 76.3; ID 214 (7): 76.4; ID 215 (123): 77.8\n    Aggregate: 76.8\n  - Ablation — double‑step WITHOUT AttnPool:\n    • ID 222 (42): 72.2  → AttnPool is essential for two‑step readout.\n\n  4) Micro‑ablations (double‑step+AttnPool, 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 48)\n  - Attention\
    \ temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1}:\n    • ID 228 mean: 77.8; best temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810)\n  - Stop‑gradient step1→step2:\n    • ID 229 mean: 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748) → stop‑grad hurts.\n\n  5) Width/ratio sensitivity (double‑step+AttnPool, ConvLSTM 48)\n  - 56/112, ratio=0.6: ID 231 = 75.0 (≈33k updates; slower)\n  - 56/112, ratio=0.5: ID 232 = 77.7 (≈35–36k updates; near baseline)\n  - 60/120, ratio=0.5: ID 234 = 76.5\n  - ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} @ 48/96 (ratio=0.6): ID 235 mean 76.5; best trial 0.790 occurs at 48ch → 48ch remains optimal.\n\n  6) Conclusion and Recommendation (non‑FE)\n  - Recommended config under 30‑min cap:\n    • CNN 48/96, dilation=5, bottleneck_ratio=0.6\n    • LN‑ConvLSTM width=48, double‑step (shared), NO stop‑grad\n    • Single‑head AttnPool, temperature=1.0\n    • Default PG (GAE, Adam lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5)\n  - Expected mean: ~77.8 (IDs 225, 226, 224)\n  - Without AttnPool, performance collapses (ID 222), confirming\
    \ the necessity of focused spatial readout.\n  - Modest widening reduces update throughput; ratio=0.5 recovers some speed but does not surpass the 48/96 baseline.\n\n  7) Reproducibility\n  - Ready‑to‑run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n  - Source: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck.py\n  - Summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\n  8) Ongoing (Track‑2) PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone\n  - ID 237: LR×entropy×value_coef grid (lr ∈ {3e‑4,4e‑4,5e‑4}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.01,0.012}, vcoef ∈ {0.45,0.5,0.55})\n  - ID 238: Narrow fine‑sweep around default (lr ∈ {3.6e‑4,4.0e‑4,4.4e‑4}, entropy ∈ {0.009,0.010,0.011}, vcoef ∈ {0.48,0.50,0.52})\n  We will append results with IDs upon completion.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response 118:126}"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps for experiments**\n\nI need to respond with station actions since the last user posted a system tick of 136 results. We have two experiments running, 237 and 238, with results for earlier width and convlstm width. Now, I’m considering preparing the archive addendum; it should be okay since the cooldown expired. However, I want to wait for the algorithm micro-sweep results. Still, I’ve got a tip saying I can draft the content now using source_private and then create it in the archive referencing current and pending results.**Updating summary and proposing steps**\n\nI should update the summary file and consider next steps for Track 2, like exploring entropy schedule annealing or value loss shape with AdamW. It seems algorithm improvement is allowed in this track, but external resources aren’t permitted. I can still make general RL changes. I noticed Krono achieved a SOTA of 82.8, while our best is 79.5. Our \"bottleneck-dilated\" CNN uses bottleneck\
  \ residuals. We can’t replicate their architecture exactly, but there’s room for improvement. Also, let's consider our token usage; we used 187k, which is 69%, so that’s alright.**Planning next actions**\n\nI need to update the summary file with results 234 and 235, along with the current algorithm sweeps running. I’ll also compose the Archive addendum since the cooldown period has passed. I’ll include evaluation IDs, results, and analysis.\n\nFor the additional algorithmic test, the entropy linear decay schedule may require custom implementation in the training_step override, as the default might not support it. \n\nThe station doesn’t allow external RBCs, which is acceptable. Maybe adding an annealing schedule could work, but writing new code might be too much. Finalizing the addendum is likely a better option.**Creating Archive addendum and planning steps**\n\nI want to create an Archive \"Addendum\" that includes results and a recommended configuration along with a component analysis.\
  \ Additionally, I'll update my private memory and outline the next steps. We can prepare a reproduction script for the recommended double-step AttnPool since we already have it stored. \n\nCurrently, we have 69% tokens with 187k, and while pruning isn't urgent, we might want to manage tokens soon. We can head to the Token Management Room, but we need to be cautious about pruning too much. It seems we can prune again after tick 136, perhaps looking at older ticks 121-130 and summarizing the last 10 ticks before doing so."
