    \ on double-step+AttnPool\"\ntags: \"replication, hyperparam, policy gradient, double-step, attention, bottleneck\"\nabstract: \"Fix the best constant PG hyperparameters from ID 237 (lr=5e-4, entropy=0.012, value_coef=0.5) on the recommended double-step LN-ConvLSTM(48) + AttnPool backbone.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck import DoubleStepLNConvLSTM\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.012]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([48]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([96]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([48]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.6]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'attn_temp': tune.choice([1.0]),\n      }\n\n  def\
    \ create_network(hparams):\n      return DoubleStepLNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          stop_grad_first=False,\n          attn_temp=hparams['attn_temp'],\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Non-FE Dilated CNN + LN-ConvLSTM — Summary (Tick 139)\n\n  Recommended backbone (double-step + AttnPool, 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM=48, d=5):\n  - Replicates: ID 225 (42)=78.8, ID 226 (7)=75.0, ID 224 (123)=79.5 → mean ≈ 77.8\n\n  Track-2 PG sweeps:\n  - ID 237 (broad grid): mean 77.2; best config lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5 (best-seed 0.818)\n  - ID 238 (narrow fine sweep): mean 74.9; best with entropy=0.009\n\n  Wide CNN attempts (double-step+AttnPool, ConvLSTM 48):\n  - 56/112@0.6:\
    \ ID 231=75.0\n  - 56/112@0.5: ID 232=77.7\n  - 60/120@0.5: ID 234=76.5\n  - 64/128 ratio sweep {0.4,0.45,0.5}: ID 242=74.9\n  - 64/128 ratio=0.4, ConvLSTM ∈ {44,48}: ID 243=75.6\n  Conclusion: Modest or wide CNNs reduce update throughput; no gain over 48/96 baseline.\n\n  New submissions (pending):\n  - LR schedule (warmup+cosine) at 48/96 (IDs pending)\n  - Replication of best PG constants from ID 237 (IDs pending)\n\n  Current Recommendation:\n  - Double-step + single-head AttnPool, 48/96 CNN, d=5, bottleneck_ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 48, attn_temp=1.0.\n  - PG: Adam lr=4e-4 (or 5e-4 candidate), entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5.\n  - Repro: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Follow-up (Tick 139): Wide-CNN synthesis without FE underperforms.\n  - 64/128 with ratio ∈ {0.4,0.45,0.5}: ID 242 = 74.9\n  - 64/128 with ratio=0.4 and ConvLSTM {44,48}: ID 243 = 75.6\n  - Prior\
    \ 56/112 results: 77.7 @ ratio=0.5 and 75.0 @ ratio=0.6 (IDs 232,231)\n  Conclusion: widening lowers update throughput and does not beat the compute-equalized 48/96 baseline (~77.8). We have launched two PG variants on the recommended backbone: (1) Adam warmup+cosine LR schedule and (2) a fixed replicate of the best constants from ID 237 (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5). Results will be appended with IDs when complete.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 139:\n  - Wide 64/128 tests: 74.9–75.6 (IDs 242,243) → negative; throughput loss confirmed.\n  - Submitted:\n      • LR schedule (warmup+cosine) on 48/96 double-step+AttnPool.\n      • Replicate best PG constants from ID 237 (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5).\n  - Plan:\n      1) If LR schedule ≥79.5, replicate across seeds and update recommendation.\n      2) If both <77.8, lock current config and explore mild PG schedules (e.g., entropy linear decay) or advantage normalization\
    \ in a minimal training_step edit (still pure PG).\n      3) Keep archive addendum updated with IDs for reproducibility.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering training restrictions**


  I''m noting the restrictions around training parameters. While we can''t change the training per seed, we can customize aspects like the optimizer—we''ll use the default training step. For our schedule, we''re set to implement a custom optimizer, as that''s allowed. The entropy scheduling might hit a snag in the interface since dynamic entropy scheduling may not be supported. If we want to adjust the entropy weight over time, I''d likely need a custom training step. But since past submissions used the default training step, custom changes here could be risky. Plus, we can only stick with the learning rate schedule for now. I''ll consider replicating the best hyperparameters from the micro-sweep to test if we can improve our mean results.**Evaluating trial results**


  I’m trying to make sense of the evaluation results. The best trial has a peak solve rate of 0.818 with hyperparameters at 5e-4, 0.012, and 0.5. The mean across four seeds for this trial might be around 0.772—wait, trial ID 237 shows a mean solve rate of 77.2. I see that "Best trial hyperparameters" refers to the overall best across the four seeds, which can be a bit confusing. It seems I need to confirm whether 0.818 is the mean for all four seeds or just the solve rate for seed 3.**Clarifying trial structure**


  I’m looking closely at the logs to understand the trial structure. It shows returns and final solve rates for each seed. The "Best trial" likely corresponds to the Ray sample index, which uses four seeds. According to the spec, there are four parallel seeds and exactly four hyperparameter choices. Each hyperparameter trial runs "Sokoban RL training" for each seed, in sequence, meaning that for every trial, four seeds are utilized again. This makes things a bit clearer!**Interpreting evaluation logs**


  I’m taking a closer look at the logs from earlier runs. They show that training starts with "Starting Sokoban RL training - Seed {1..4}" for one hyperparameter trial, which could definitely be confusing. The logs for trial 237 indicate solve rates of 0.725, 0.784, 0.818, and 0.762 for the respective seeds. The "Best trial hyperparameters" include a learning rate of 5e-4, entropy of 0.012, and a value loss coefficient of 0.5, with an overall score of 77.2. Since the best seed reached a 0.818 solve rate, replicating those hyperparameters might improve our results over the current baseline of 77.8.**Planning next steps**


  I''m setting up a replicate that uses hyperparameters of 5e-4 and 0.012, expecting it to yield around 77-78, but it may not surpass 79.5. It''s essential to confirm the best hyperparameters across seeds. Since the Ray setup uses four parallel hyperparameter configurations, I’ll set a fixed ''best'' configuration to test. I''ll also submit the LR schedule variant I prepared earlier. Plans include running two submissions for the schedule and replicating the best PG constants. I need to keep track of updates related to wide synergy results, ensuring to summarize recent findings. Finally, I’ll ensure the YAML formatting is correct for our commands as I execute these actions.**Crafting submission content**


  I’m preparing the content for two submissions. For the schedule submission, I''ll title it "LR schedule (warmup+cosine) on double-step+AttnPool (48/96, d=5, ratio=0.6)" and tag it with relevant keywords. The content will include necessary imports from our storage.


  Next, for the replication of the best PG constants, I''ll give it the title "Replication: Best PG constants (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) on double-step+AttnPool" and similarly tag it. I need to ensure that the hyperparameters are fixed, meaning all trials will run identically. I’ll keep disclaimers in mind and remember the YAML formatting requirements. Time to finalize this!'
