parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AdamW+clip + warmup-cosine on double-step+AttnPool (48/96, d=5, ratio=0.6)\"\ntags: \"double-step, attention, optimizer, adamw, schedule, clip\"\nabstract: \"Test AdamW (wd=1e-5) with global grad clip(1.0) and warmup+cosine LR on the recommended double-step LN-ConvLSTM(48) + AttnPool backbone. Goal: check if schedule+wd+clip recovers/improves over 77.8.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from zephyr.submissions.submission_double_step_lr_sched_wd_clip import (\n      _define_hyperparameters as _dh,\n      create_network as _cn,\n      create_optimizer as _co,\n      BASE_SEED as BASE_SEED,\n  )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return _dh()\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return _cn(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return _co(learning_rate)\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Non-FE Dilated CNN + LN-ConvLSTM — Summary (Tick 141)\n\n  Recommended backbone (double-step + AttnPool, 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM=48, d=5):\n  - Replicates: ID 225 (42)=78.8, ID 226 (7)=75.0, ID 224 (123)=79.5 → mean ≈ 77.8\n\n  Track-2 results:\n  - ID 245 LR warmup+cosine (Adam): 70.3 → schedule hurts here.\n  - ID 246 Best constants from grid (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5): 77.8 (matches baseline).\n  - Submitted: AdamW+clip + warmup-cosine (this tick).\n\n  Width/wide CNN (double-step+AttnPool, ConvLSTM 48):\n  - 56/112@0.6: ID 231=75.0; 56/112@0.5: ID 232=77.7\n  - 60/120@0.5: ID 234=76.5\n  - 64/128 ratio sweep: ID 242=74.9; 64/128@0.4 with ConvLSTM {44,48}: ID 243=75.6\n  Conclusion: wider CNN lowers update throughput; no gain over 48/96 compute-equalized baseline.\n\n  Current Recommendation:\n  - Double-step + single-head AttnPool, 48/96 CNN, d=5, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 48, attn_temp=1.0.\n  - PG constants: lr=4e-4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5 (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012\
    \ is a viable alternate; same mean).\n  - Repro: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Track‑2 results update (Tick 141):\n  - LR warmup+cosine (ID 245) yielded 70.3 → harmful under current budget.\n  - Fixed replicate of best constants from the broad grid (ID 246; lr=5e‑4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) achieved 77.8, matching our 48/96 double‑step+AttnPool baseline mean (~77.8).\n  - Conclusion: baseline PG constants remain competitive; minor constant shifts do not exceed 77.8. We have submitted an AdamW+clip+cosine variant to test whether schedule+wd+clip can help; if not, we will lock the recommendation and end the Track‑2 exploration here.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 141:\n  - Results: LR warmup+cosine hurt (ID 245=70.3); fixed best constants reproduced 77.8 (ID 246).\n  - Submitted: AdamW+clip+cosine\
    \ (wd=1e-5) on 48/96 double-step+AttnPool to test if schedule+clip+wd helps where schedule alone failed.\n  - If <77.8, we’ll lock the recommendation (48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM=48, temp=1.0; PG lr=4e-4/ent=0.01/vcoef=0.5) and consider closing Track‑2 with a brief negative-results note.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating a submission strategy**\n\nI need to think about the next steps for continuing my research. The environment is set at station tick 141, and the results show that our LR schedule warmup with cosine didn't perform well, scoring 70.3. However, replication of the best constants gives a 77.8, matching the baseline mean of roughly 77.8, so the recommended config stays. I’m considering submitting the AdamW+clip+schedule script as planned since it's still allowed by task constraints. It seems worth testing if this schedule will improve the results.**Reflecting on algorithm attempts**\n\nI'm considering the earlier results of 88 and 89 from the LN-gated 64-channel single-step, which had lower scores around 59-60 due to a different configuration. There's a chance of underperformance again, but I think trying small weight decay might help. I want to run one more algorithm experiment before freezing things. I can update the archive with IDs 245 and 246 for these results.\
  \ Although the current state-of-the-art is 82.8, our non-feature engineering baseline is at 77.8, so I may need to think about how to adjust moving forward.**Analyzing bottleneck and performance**\n\nI noticed that when we tried widening the network, we lost throughput. The real synergy seems to be with the \"Bottleneck Block,\" and we previously used a \"bottleneck-dilated CNN\" at 48/96 with a 0.6 ratio, which is essentially the same. However, the current state-of-the-art is 82.8, while our baseline is 77.8, even with double-step and AttnPool. It might mean we need design differences or efficiency improvements to boost performance further. They likely have optimizations we don't yet utilize.**Planning next steps for optimization**\n\nThe current state-of-the-art at 82.8 discusses using double-step and wide/dilated configurations, but our results aren’t quite matching. It might be due to other hyperparameters, gating, or attention mechanisms. We have to stick to the constraints without\
  \ feature engineering. I’ve used 151,570 tokens, which is over 56% of the budget, so managing tokens is important, though pruning isn't necessary. \n\nI’ll move forward by submitting the AdamW+clip+schedule variant, updating the archive, and preparing to implement entropy linear decay via a dynamic training_step update.**Planning actionable next steps**\n\nOkay, I think implementing an \"entropy_coef schedule\" that starts at 0.012 and linearly decays to 0.006 might be beneficial. I'm unsure about the exact signature for the `training_step`, but I recall that it’s similar to the original Sokoban task, where we successfully wrote a custom step for PPO. \n\nGiven the time constraints, I'll focus on submitting the AdamW+clip+schedule test. I can also update the archive with IDs 245 and 246 results, while keeping an eye on further experiments and organizing them efficiently."
