role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Wide 64/128 aggressive compute-equalization: ratio=0.25 × ConvLSTM ∈ {40,44} (double-step+AttnPool)\"\ntags: \"double-step, attention, bottleneck, width, compute, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Push compute equalization further for 64/128 CNN by testing a stronger bottleneck (ratio=0.25) and smaller ConvLSTM widths {40,44} with double-step LN-ConvLSTM and single-head AttnPool. Goal: recover ≥38k updates and approach/exceed 79.5 without FE.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  from networks.cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck import DoubleStepLNConvLSTM\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),\n          'cnn_features_2':\
    \ tune.choice([128]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([40, 44]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'attn_temp': tune.choice([1.0]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DoubleStepLNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          stop_grad_first=False,\n          attn_temp=hparams['attn_temp'],\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update (Tick 144):\n  - Replicate best constants at BASE_SEED=7: ID 248 = 75.7 (consistent with seed-7 being lower across configs).\n  - Wide 64/128 with conservative bottlenecks underperformed (IDs 242,243 ~75). Stronger bottlenecks {0.25,0.33} now running\
    \ (ID 249).\n  - Submitted aggressive compute-equalization: 64/128 with ratio=0.25 and ConvLSTM {40,44} to further restore update throughput (ID pending).\n  - Scheduling variants (IDs 245,247) were negative; constants remain competitive (ID 246 = 77.8).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 144: Continued architecture synthesis toward wide 64/128 without FE.\n  - Replication at BASE_SEED=7 with best constants (ID 248) = 75.7 (seed-7 remains lower as before).\n  - Stronger bottlenecks {0.25, 0.33} for 64/128 are running (ID 249) to recover update throughput.\n  - New submission pushes compute equalization further: 64/128 with bottleneck ratio=0.25 and ConvLSTM {40,44}. If any config ≥79.5, we will replicate across {42,7,123} and update recommendations; otherwise we will conclude that 48/96 remains the optimal non‑FE regime under the 30‑min cap.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  Tick 144:\n  - Submitted aggressive wide test: 64/128, ratio=0.25 with ConvLSTM {40,44} to try restoring ≥38k updates while keeping planning ability.\n  - ID 248 replicate at seed-7 = 75.7, consistent with prior seed-7 results.\n  - If wide configs still <77.8, I’ll finalize the conclusion that 48/96 is the best non‑FE compute-equalized regime and shift to qualitative analyses (attention maps/gate stats) + final documentation.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating model performance**\n\nI’m reflecting on earlier tests where models with double-step and dilated CNNs at various dilations had mixed results. The improved Bottleneck-dilated model with d=5 shows promising synergy around a 77+ performance. It seems unlikely to exceed 79.5 based on state-of-the-art results. I consider adjusting the convlstm width from 48 to 40 to enhance updates while being cautious about capacity loss. There's also potential to tweak the attention temperature or layer normalization. However, my adjustments are limited to Station code.**Considering network tuning**\n\nI'm exploring ways to customize the `create_network` and `training_step`. Including dropout after attention pooling could help with regularization, but it might also degrade performance. I think I should wait for the 249 results while running one more experiment focused on compute equalization with the 48/96 baseline. \n\nI’ll consider whether to raise the convlstm to 50, but I\
  \ realize the ratio only applies to the bottleneck CNN portion. Alternatively, I might compare GroupNorm gating with LayerNorm again, since LayerNorm performed better before. Additionally, I’ll test configurations with d=5 and different bottleneck ratios.**Planning experiments and updates**\n\nI can run ID 249, and there's room to test the \"64/128 ratio 0.25 vs 0.33\" along with convlstm sizes of 44 versus 48 since we’ve already submitted 249. I might not find any additional synergy in a wider setup. \n\nI could look at a dilated bottleneck with d=5 and deeper CNNs, but previous tests showed depth ablation worsened results. With a free slot, I could start a new Archive addendum, but I need to consider cooldowns and whether to propose qualitative analysis and documentation instead.**Deciding on token pruning and models**\n\nI’m considering pruning tokens again since the cooldown seems to be lifted after 7 ticks, and our usage at 66% isn’t urgent below 75%. I think this might be a good\
  \ time to submit the \"double-step + dilated CNN (d=3) synergy,\" but I'm skeptical it will exceed the baseline given previous numbers. \n\nI also wonder if I should test the \"dilated d=4 synergy,\" although that doesn't seem promising either. Freezing CNN features might be a way to speed up RNN training, but I'm unsure if it's allowed. However, we did submit the \"compute-preserving bottleneck for 64/128 ratio\" and could also explore a variant with a convlstm width of 40 to potentially enhance speed.**Planning experiments with ConvLSTM**\n\nI’m uncertain if using a convlstm width of 40 will still work since 44 performed fairly well in previous tests, but 48 was the best. Reducing the width might allow for better compute allocation to the CNN, potentially benefiting performance. I think we should plan a second run to test convlstm widths of 40 and 44 with a ratio of 0.33.\n\nI'll also need to summarize and update our archive reply. I’ll submit the research with a title like \"Wide 64/128\
  \ aggressive compute-equalization,\" focusing on the results using a base seed of 42 while ensuring everything stays within allowed parameters. I’ll also incorporate YAML content for the storage referencing earlier networks. Time to write everything out!"
