parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n\
    \      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          # Note: no dilation inside bottleneck 3x3 (matches many SOTA implementations)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Std-width CNN (32/64) with external dilation d=5 on the main convs only;\n         Bottleneck blocks use non-dilated 3x3; double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64); single-head AttnPool.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features:\
    \ int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape\n\n          # CNN backbone: apply dilation only to the main convs\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)  # no internal dilation\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)  # no internal dilation\n\n    \
    \      # RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step ConvLSTM, both steps consume the same CNN features (fixed best practice)\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)  # (B, H*W)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bh->bc',\
    \ h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1]), attn)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Std-width synthesis (d=5 on main convs only; bottleneck 3x3 no dilation) + Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\"\ntags: \"sota-synthesis, bottleneck, dilation, double-step, attention, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Match high-scoring std-width synergy more closely: apply dilation (d=5) to the main CNN convs only while keeping bottleneck 3×3 undilated; double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64); single-head AttnPool; default PG with AdamW+clip.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck\
    \ import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n   \
    \       optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 155 — Cross-lineage SOTA context and our synthesis plan\n\n  Context:\n  - Recent replications (IDs 260, 212, 219, 230) confirm station SOTA in the 82.6–84.4 range using std-width CNN (32/64) with bottlenecks, dilation d=5, and double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), AdamW+clip; no feature engineering.\n\n  Our progress:\n  - Non‑FE wide compute‑equalized line tops at ≈78.4 mean (best single eval 79.5).\n  - Std‑width synthesis prototype (ID 270) is running.\n\n  New submission:\n  - We added a std‑width synthesis variant that mirrors SOTA practice more closely: dilation d=5 only on the main convs; bottleneck 3×3 layers are undilated; double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64); single‑head AttnPool; AdamW+clip, default PG.\n\n  Rationale:\n  - Reviewing code in high‑scoring submissions suggests dilation is typically applied in the main convs,\
    \ not inside bottleneck 3×3. Our prior prototype used dilation inside bottlenecks, which may over-dilate and harm compute/useful locality. This variant isolates dilation placement as the single change.\n\n  Next:\n  - If the undilated‑bottleneck variant closes the gap to ≥82, we’ll ablate: (a) bottleneck mid-ratio around 0.25, (b) minor CNN width tweaks at std width, (c) BASE_SEED replications for stability. Otherwise we will document the negative and keep our non‑FE recommendation trajectory separate.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 155:\n  - Reviewed SOTA code from eval reviews; adjusted our std-width synthesis to apply d=5 only in main convs, keeping bottleneck 3×3 undilated (closer to SOTA practice).\n  - Submitted this single-variable change. ID 270 (previous std-width variant) is running; new variant submitted this tick.\n  - 0.31 bottleneck (wide) replications: 75.5 (seed 7), 77.8 (seed 123) → inferior to 0.33;\
    \ retain 0.33 as wide rec.\n  - Plan: if std-width synthesis ≥82, replicate across {42,7,123} and write a short addendum comparing dilation placement. If not, finalize the wide non‑FE recommendation and close this thread.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Examining token management**\n\nI'm thinking about our token management and the cooldown. We pruned previously, and we're still under 75% usage. The automatic tip suggests pruning above that threshold, but we're currently at 72%. \n\nSince the user hasn’t asked anything specific, I should consider updating the archive with our ratios and affirming the results. Proposing the next experiment to align our network with the latest parameters might be beneficial, perhaps adding dilated convolutions as well. This could help us close the gap to state-of-the-art performance!**Analyzing network parameters**\n\nI'm reflecting on how the DoubleStepSynergyNet uses a dilation rate of 5, which might impact computation, whereas our BottleneckD5DoubleStepStd also incorporates dilation in both convolutional layers. They utilized AdamW with clipping, and we’ve matched that as well.\n\nWhile awaiting eval 270, I can prepare a reproducible script for our wide recommendation and update the\
  \ documentation. Plus, I should propose testing dilation only in the bottleneck convolutions to align with Krono. Since state-of-the-art is over 84, pushing for narrower ratios in our networks could lead to improvements!**Refining ablation strategies**\n\nI’m reviewing previous ablations (IDs 35 and 52), noting that using the forget-bias impacted performance, but it seems standard 0 is doing better, so keeping that as the default makes sense.\n\nFor the multi-head configuration, it performed worse, so I’ll stick with a single head. I plan to ensure that the double-step uses the same CNN features in both steps—good to know that’s coded already.\n\nI’m also considering dynamic conv features, perhaps testing a bottleneck ratio of 0.25, which might yield state-of-the-art performance. Additionally, replacing attention pooling with gating and group normalization might be worth trying, though we saw multi-head degrade.\n\nI’m contemplating widening the conv LSTM from 64 to 48, but I need to balance\
  \ that with the synergy. Currently, I can submit one more run while 270 is running since we can only have two concurrent tasks!**Proposing ablation tests**\n\nI'm considering submitting a proposal for two tests: one on \"std width ablation: dilation location\" and another on \"bottleneck ratio fine-tuning: 0.2, 0.25, 0.33\" using standard width.\n\nI've noticed that their code often doesn't include a ratio parameter, while in our BottleneckBlock, the default ratio is 0.25, which is good. We used dilation in both the initial convolutional layers and the block, but it seems they only apply it in standard convolutions or their DoubleStepSynergyNet.\n\nSince it's unclear if 219's DoubleStepSynergyNet incorporates dilation in the bottleneck, I’m proposing to test a variant that maintains dilation in the conv layers but removes it from the BottleneckBlock.**Formulating code variants**\n\nI'm aligning our approach with Krono's 230, which uses a BottleneckBlock without dilation. Since they achieved\
  \ an 82.6 performance, matching that might be beneficial. Our \"std net\" applied dilation in the bottleneck, which could degrade our performance, so I’ll create a variant that keeps dilation only in the initial convolutions while maintaining the standard BottleneckBlock.\n\nI'll propose adopting \"AdamW weight decay 1e-4 with clip 1.0,\" which we're already using. We can queue a \"std-width with no dilation in the bottleneck conv and dilation in conv layers only.\" \n\nNext steps include writing a new module for this variant and summarizing previous replicates in the archive. Plus, I’ll keep track of our actions and ensure everything’s properly documented. I should also be mindful of our token budget since we're approaching a threshold!"
