  I need to submit "SOTA-level replication attempts for std-width d=5 normal bottleneck" using BASE_SEED 7 and 123, along with the existing module already in storage. It''s great that we have the right code ready to go. I’m also considering testing a mild bottleneck ratio, potentially comparing 0.25 vs 0.33, since the standard typically uses 0.25. I should update the Archive with the new results while keeping our collaboration rules in mind to ensure we''re not sharing code from other lineages.**Implementing research actions**


  I need to take action now, starting with going to research. I’ll submit the first request with the code referencing the module and defining hyperparameters while setting BASE_SEED to 7. After that, I’ll submit another with BASE_SEED set to 123. I might consider committing a variant replicating the "no dilation in bottleneck" if the results are favorable, although request 273 is pending. Then, I''ll update the archive regarding ID 270 and also go to private_memory to document the experiment plan. Finally, I''ll manage tokens and update the private research summary while being mindful of cooldowns.**Managing token pruning actions**


  I realize that the token management pruning action only affects dialogue records, while the summary is stored in private memory. Now, which ticks should I prune? The larger ones are 136 to 145 since they had longer logs, and we already pruned 127 to 135 previously. So, I can move forward with the pruning for 136 to 145. I need to use the command at token_management and format the pruning as separate actions. I’ll draft the two submissions for BASE_SEED 7 first, then for BASE_SEED 123, followed by the archive and private memory update. Lastly, I''ll check token management for pruning 136 to 145, maybe even considering 146 to 151.**Deciding on pruning and updates**


  I’m considering whether to prune some records since they contain major results, but our current token usage is at 200,930, which is 74%. I think that after pruning 10 ticks, our usage will drop significantly, which is a good thing. So, I’ll go ahead and implement that. I’m also contemplating if we should update public memory, but that’s not strictly necessary. Let''s finalize everything and produce the updates!'
