parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate std-width nodil recommended (ratio=0.25, entropy=0.01) — BASE_SEED=7\"\ntags: \"replication, std-width, nodil, double-step, attention, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"Replication of the finalized std-width no-FE baseline: 32/64 CNN with d=5 on main convs only, undilated 3×3 bottlenecks (ratio=0.25), double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), 1-head AttnPool, AdamW+clip, entropy=0.01. BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n\
    \          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replicate std-width nodil recommended (ratio=0.25, entropy=0.01) — BASE_SEED=123\"\ntags: \"replication, std-width, nodil, double-step, attention, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"Second replication of the finalized std-width no-FE\
    \ baseline under identical settings to confirm cross-seed stability. BASE_SEED=123.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n\
    \          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance discrepancies**\n\nI'm looking at cross-seeding results for nodil 0.25 at different seeds. Seed 42 got 86.4 with entropy sweeping, but there was an earlier result of 83.4, which may have stemmed from a different config. ID 273 for seed 42 was at 83.4, whereas ID 291 showed 86.4 using the same setup with AdamW+clip. There seems to be a discrepancy in results, possibly due to subtle architectural changes or code variants, since the configurations varied a bit.**Reviewing optimizer configurations**\n\nI’m checking ID 273, which used standard width in earlier experiments with create_optimizer using AdamW+clip, achieving 83.4. Now, seed 42 has jumped from 83.4 to 86.4 using the same ratio of 0.25, which might hint at training improvements or aggregator differences. The variability across seeds can be substantial. To verify improvements, I plan to submit replicates for entropy=0.01 at seeds 7 and 123, aiming for a mean improvement above 85. Also, I’ll\
  \ consider minor tweaks to possibly surpass Krono II's 86.9.**Evaluating optimization strategies**\n\nI'm sticking with AdamW+clip and a learning rate of 4e-4 but might consider a small increase to 5e-4 with an entropy of 0.012. The sweep indicated 0.01 worked best at seed 42, but it’s not definitive across all seeds. \n\nI could adjust the value loss coefficient slightly or modify the gradient norm. Bootstrapping deeper head networks might help, but I want to keep computation consistent. Also, I’m thinking about gating variants or normalization improvements using LN gating. Adding dropout could be risky. Another approach is mixing double-step outputs from a learned gate. Finally, perhaps attention temperature fine-tuning is worth exploring.**Testing nodil parameters**\n\nFor testing nodil, I want to explore attention temperature around 0.9, 1.0, and 1.1. Previous wide tests showed only a small effect, but I wonder if that holds as we move to a high regime.\n\nI've fixed the convolution\
  \ dilation parameter at 5, though I could consider minor changes to 4 or 6. Krono II found 84.1 at 6 and 80.6 at 4, so 5 seems optimal. Finally, while we’ve settled on a bottleneck ratio of 0.25, I'm curious if proposing a middle ground of 0.225 could work, since cross-seed means at 0.20 are higher but weaker at seeds 7 and 123.**Planning experiment submissions**\n\nI’m considering testing a new bottleneck ratio of 0.225 or 0.22, as we need the code to use fixed floats defined by tune.choice. I want to see if these settings yield a more uniform improvement. The convolution features must remain integer, which might change compute outcomes.\n\nAdditionally, I'm looking at using attention head dimensions or modifying the MLP. We could also consider minor tweaks to layer normalization or gating but aren’t guaranteed improvements. I’m exploring replicating Krono II's results using PPO with a fixed RNN. Despite previous challenges with PPO, I believe repeating experiments and adjusting some\
  \ parameters, like learning rates and value loss coefficients, could be beneficial while keeping an eye on concurrency for running two submissions efficiently.**Preparing submission code**\n\nI need to ensure the 'submit' content contains the necessary code while avoiding duplication. I can import from storage, which simplifies things. I want to import key functions and set BASE_SEED for both submissions to 7 and 123. Since I can’t easily override BASE_SEED through imports, I’ll define it inline instead.\n\nFor both submissions, I'll create inline code that includes network imports, optimizer setups, and fixed parameters for learning rate, entropy, and more. I'll also ensure that my YAML titles are quoted appropriately, particularly because they contain colons. Let’s streamline this message and finalize the submission actions!"
