- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_temp.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n    \
    \  @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBNTemp(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Std-width CNN (32/64) with main-conv dilation d=5; bottleneck 3x3 undilated.\n         Double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64); single-head AttnPool with temperature.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0\n\n \
    \     @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n\n          # CNN backbone: apply dilation only to main convs; bottleneck uses undilated 3x3\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          # RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H,\
    \ W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (both steps read same CNN features)\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling with temperature\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape(B, -1)\n          scale = 1.0 / jnp.clip(self.attn_temp, a_min=1e-6)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits * scale, axis=-1)  # (B, H*W)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bn,bnc->bc', attn, h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1]))\n\
    \n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Std-width nodil bottleneck ratio micro-sweep (0.22, 0.24, 0.26) — BASE_SEED=42\"\ntags: \"std-width, nodil, bottleneck, ablation, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"Fine ablation of the bottleneck mid-channel ratio around the default 0.25 for the std-width nodil config. Tests {0.22, 0.24, 0.26} under PG with AdamW+clip.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n\
    \          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.22, 0.24, 0.26]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit\
    \ 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Std-width nodil attention temperature sweep (0.95, 1.00, 1.05) — BASE_SEED=42\"\ntags: \"std-width, nodil, attention, temperature, ablation, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"Test sensitivity to attention pooling temperature in the std-width nodil config by scaling attention logits with τ ∈ {0.95, 1.00, 1.05}.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_temp import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBNTemp\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n\
    \          'attn_temp': tune.choice([0.95, 1.0, 1.05]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBNTemp(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n          attn_temp=hparams['attn_temp'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating submission settings**\n\nI need to update our \"submission_recommended_std_nodil.py\" file to set the base seed to 42, since we've seen good results with seeds 7 and 123, giving us 84.9 and 87.0. To replicate different sets, I’ll customize the 'BASE_SEED' variable, as the system runs four seeds per run. Now that we've made those changes, we should proceed to submit the PPO reproduction using our code for nodil architecture to verify any improvements across seeds, especially with seed 42.**Implementing PPO training with RNN**\n\nWe’ve used their code before, but now I can implement our own `training_step` with PPO, which is known to handle RNNs. Previously, our attempts with PPO yielded poor results, but with the new state-of-the-art architecture and RNN fixes, it looks more promising. I plan to test PPO in our setup, using `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN`. \n\nI'll implement `ppo_training_step_jax_rnn`, running it for a single epoch versus two, and make choices\
  \ about hyperparameters like clip epsilon and KL coefficient. While considering options like \"PPO+entropy annealing,\" it’s probably best to stick to permissible modifications for better results.**Proposing advantage smoothing tuning**\n\nI’m considering proposing 'Generalized Advantage Smoothing (lambda) tuning' with values like 0.9, 0.95, and 0.99 in our `training_step`, reading lambda from hyperparameters. However, it seems the default `training_step` might fix lambda, so we could override it to embed lambda. Yet, there's uncertainty about whether the default passes hyperparameters correctly. Based on earlier specs, the default might compute advantages using gamma=0.99 and lambda=0.95. Alternatively, I could finalize an archive for the 'Std-width Non-FE baseline recipe' with IDs. But I noticed updates were already made in an addendum.**Reflecting on improvement areas**\n\nI'm noticing that our next big improvement area could be the algorithm. Krono II achieved 86.9 with PPO, while\
  \ our best PG replicates got 87.0, indicating that PPO isn't necessarily better. Testing 'PPO with standard architecture' for seed 42 could help us see if we can surpass 87, potentially reaching 87.5. However, the gains from PPO seem minimal. Alternatively, we could focus on an 'entropy warmup schedule' or 'value loss coefficient tuning.' While we shouldn't share code between lineages directly, we plan to implement these changes ourselves. A micro-sweep of `entropy_coef` values across seeds 7 or 123 might also be an option, even if we already used 42.**Planning concurrent experiments**\n\nThe station allows a maximum of two concurrent experiments, and since we have none running, we can submit two. I'm considering tuning the 'AdamW weight decay value' since weight decay might have a minor impact during time-limited training. Additionally, I could explore options like 'Stop-gradient through the first LSTM step' or mixing gating from earlier experiments. \n\nIt seems best to close the loop\
  \ by finalizing the 'Std-width baseline' archive and adding results 291, 293, and 294. Next, I want to unify the algorithm test to run both PG and PPO under fixed conditions, particularly for base seed 42. For the PPO training step, I need to prepare the data structure for 'batch,' which includes various parameters like observations and advantages. \n\nAlso, it could be valuable to slightly adjust the bottleneck ratio or explore the 'attn_temp' to confirm potential improvements for the standard width nodil. I’ll modify the network code to include 'attn_temp' as a parameter and prepare to run the updated versions of our models.**Implementing updates for experiments**\n\nI need to ensure the name 'BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBNTemp' is implemented, and I can reuse the network file while adjusting 'attn_logits' by multiplying it with '1/attn_temp'. To do this, I'll create a new class and copy earlier cell definitions for the attention pool.\n\nNext steps include submitting two experiments\
  \ while ensuring we don't exceed the concurrent limit. One experiment will focus on a bottleneck ratio micro-sweep, while the other will examine the attention temperature. I also need to properly craft and submit YAML files for each experiment. Lastly, I'll incorporate necessary classes and imports back into the network code."
