parts:
- text: "Great result. I’ll store a reproducible late-time-injection network and launch two fixed-seed replications to verify stability.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Optional, Dict\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n\
    \          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          # Undilated 3x3 inside bottleneck (keep locality/compute)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Std-width CNN (32/64) with dilation d=5 on main convs only;\n      Bottleneck 3x3s are undilated; Double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64);\n      Single-head\
    \ AttnPool; LATE TIME INJECTION: time channel aggregated after pooling.\n      \"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          # Split spatial (first 7 channels) vs time (last channel)\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2))  # (B,1)\n\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n\n          # CNN backbone: dilation on main convs only\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          # RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step ConvLSTM (shared cell, same CNN features each step)\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling over\
    \ spatial tokens\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)  # (B,H,W,1)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)  # (B, H*W)\n          h_flat = h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1])          # (B, H*W, C)\n          z_spat = jnp.einsum('bnc,bn->bc', h_flat, attn)         # (B, C)\n\n          # Late time injection (append t after spatial pooling)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([z_spat, t], axis=-1)               # (B, C+1)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1,\
    \ kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Late time injection after pooling — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=7)\"\ntags: \"architecture, bottleneck, dilation, convlstm, time, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Replicate the 86.6 result with late time injection after pooling: std-width CNN (32/64) with d=5 on main convs only, undilated bottleneck 3×3s, double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), single-head AttnPool, AdamW+clip; BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n  \
    \        'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Late time injection after pooling — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=123)\"\ntags: \"architecture, bottleneck, dilation, convlstm,\
    \ time, policy gradient\"\nabstract: \"Second replicate of late time injection after pooling under identical settings; BASE_SEED=123.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n\
    \          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 180 — Late time injection result and next steps\n  - New result: ID 322 = 86.6 (BASE_SEED=42) with late time injection after pooling on std-width nodil+d=5 main convs, double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), AdamW+clip.\n  - Launched replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {7,123}) with identical config to verify stability.\n  - Plan after reps: controlled ablation comparing early vs late time injection under identical settings; then submit Archive addendum with table (IDs, mean±std across seed sets) and code path recorded at storage/zephyr/networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj.py.\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing for publication**\n\nI’m looking at the “late time injection after pooling” approach we used, which likely helps avoid mixing time into spatial representation too early. We achieved an 86.6 solve rate, which beats the previous best of 84.4 — that’s a good sign!\n\nWe should prepare our paper submission for Archive and address replication across different seed sets beyond 42. Also, running a variant without late-time injection will help demonstrate its effect. Lastly, we need to set up controlled ablation for clarity.**Managing code submissions**\n\nI'm unsure if the code is properly stored. The evaluation for \"Std-width nodil – late time injection after pooling (BASE_SEED=42)\" was from the last turn, but I didn't see the content in the last message. We created the networks and submitted the AdamW+clip code earlier, and it looks like the evaluation ID 322 is tied to \"late time injection after pooling.\"\n\nTo ensure replicability, I should save the code in\
  \ a specific directory or create a module that includes late injection along with a baseline. We need to manage our concurrent evaluation limits as well since we can only submit up to two experiments at the same time.**Queuing experiments for robustness**\n\nI can queue replicates for BASE_SEED=7 and 123 for the new method while also submitting a controlled baseline using \"standard time injection.\" However, I want to make sure to replicate across seeds for the baseline too. \n\nI need to remember that there are fixed constraints, like a maximum of four seeds per submission and that the final score is the mean across those seeds. For the new method, I’ll run two replicates with the chosen BASE_SEED. Ensuring that the code for both architectures is properly saved for reproducibility is essential!**Preparing for the paper**\n\nI’m considering writing a paper for Archive and need to provide evaluation IDs for earlier calibrations. We had replicates across seeds for the nodil baseline, which\
  \ yielded results of 83.4, 83.2, 82.5, 85.6, 84.9, and 87.0.\n\nI noticed that the scores for 293-294 come from the \"std-width nodil recommended\" variant, which achieved 87.0. This suggests that while the new \"late time injection\" improved our score to 86.6, the baseline could also reach similar results. It leads me to wonder if the improvement is entirely due to the late-time injection.**Submitting new experiments**\n\nIt looks like our latest score of 86.6 is pretty close to the earlier highest score of 87.0, which I gathered from the previous nodil baseline evaluations. I noticed some earlier runs show scores from the 84s to mid-85s. Given this, we should submit replicates for the \"late time injection\" method while ensuring it aligns with our baseline architecture. \n\nI need to prepare the updates for Archive, possibly creating a new addendum highlighting the improvements. We can submit two experiments with BASE_SEEDs 7 and 123; I'll handle storing the new network code for the\
  \ late injection method as well. It will be critical to match the hyperparameters we've found successful previously. Let's go ahead and specify everything needed for the submission and ensure our structures are in place!**Finalizing submission process**\n\nI’m getting ready to set BASE_SEEDs 7 and 123 for our experiments. First, I’ll update Private Memory with our plan and possibly the archive after our replicates conclude. I want to make sure to maintain proper YAML formatting since our titles might include special characters.\n\nNext, I’ll create a new module for 'late time injection' and ensure it’s correctly implemented for reproducibility. Then I’ll submit our two experiments while keeping track of code efficiency. Lastly, I’ll reply to the private memory summary and archive to announce our progress and future plans. Let’s execute these actions! \n\n/execute_action{goto research}  \n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/networks/bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj.py}\
  \  \nYAML content code here.  \n\n/execute_action{submit 1}  \nYAML for seed 7, including title etc.  \n\n/execute_action{submit 1}  \nYAML for seed 123.  \n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}  \n/execute_action{reply 2}  \nYAML for the content plan.**Finalizing the implementation**\n\nI need to remain consistent with earlier observations. The input shape is (8, 8, 8), and I have to ensure the axis for channels isn't swapped. I’ll also reset the done mask for the hidden and cell states.\n\nUsing `x_spat = x[..., :7]` will help maintain clarity. It’s also important to keep dilation at a default of 5 for the main convolutions. I’ll import from the existing 'bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck' module, but to keep things simple, I’ll just re-implement instead of subclassing. \n\nOnce I’ve completed the code, I’ll go to research to continue the work."
