    \ - Removing time hurts vs late (e.g., −3.1 at seed 42), so time is beneficial if injected late.\n\n  Next:\n  - Complete policy-only vs value-only ablations; then replicate best of the two across {7,123}.\n  - Update CSV (new_ablations_late_vs_early.csv) and Archive addendum with a consolidated table (IDs, configs, mean±std).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 187 — Status and plan while 338/339 run:\n  - Summary file written at storage/zephyr/results/summary.txt with late/early/no-time results.\n  - Waiting on policy-only (ID 338) and value-only (ID 339). If policy-only ≥ value-only, will replicate policy-only across seeds {7,123}; otherwise do the same for value-only.\n  - After replications, finalize CSV + Archive addendum table and close the time-channel study thread.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning while waiting**\n\nI think we should wait for them to finish while keeping ourselves occupied. I could create an analysis code file to load evaluation logs from the research system, but there isn't an API for that, so we might use review actions to see partial logs instead. Since evaluations not finished can’t be reviewed, I’ll make sure to update the results file and avoid unnecessary noise while checking for changes in ongoing experiments. Keeping concise seems best.**Organizing proposed actions**\n\nI need to monitor research and prepare the queue for time injection with specific seeds. While I can't queue directly, I can write the submission payloads in Private Memory for quick access. Creating code modules for sampling both types might be helpful, and I can pre-compose YAML files in storage for ease of submission. However, since the submit function requires inline content, I’ll focus on string templates in Private Memory for quick copying. Testing a function\
  \ could compute things, but I must avoid interfering with the training process.**Organizing follow-up actions**\n\nI’m considering proposing a follow-up for adding FiLM gating by time, but since it's a new variable, it makes sense to finish the current ablation replicates first. My plan is to check the research status and hold off on further actions until they finish. To stay productive, I'll jot down next steps in Private Memory, including replicating 338 and 339, finalizing the CSV, and updating the Archive with a note. I might also prepare code for both policy-only and value-only replicates to seed 7 when the slot is free.**Planning submissions and actions**\n\nIn this situation, we can’t parameterize directly and need to execute the submission using YAML. I might prepare the code as a storage script called \"policytime_submission.py\" for easier importing later. The training system requires running \"main.py,\" which loads functions from the submission file. It’s clear we can’t import\
  \ from storage directly, but we can import from networks as needed. For now, it seems better to monitor the progress and update the archive afterward while staying productive without idling. I'll check the status and rank IDs, but I need to be cautious of token usage.**Organizing next actions**\n\nAlright, my plan is as follows:\n\nI’ll start by going to research and ranking the score to keep the scoreboard sorted. I want to create a new row in the CSV to reflect the “no_time” result and update Private Memory with a clear plan. \n\nThen, I’ll execute actions for going to research and writing the summary file, which will include late, early, and no time results. I’m also noting future plans to replicate and finalize results after ensuring everything is minimal and straightforward. Keeping track of each action will be essential."
