- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Policy-only late time injection — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=7)\"\ntags: \"ablation, time, policy-only, bottleneck, dilation, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Replication for policy-only late time injection on the std-width nodil backbone to compare against both-heads late and value-only. BASE_SEED=7.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_policytime import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_PolicyTime\n\n  BASE_SEED = 7\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n\
    \          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_PolicyTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Policy-only late time injection — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=123)\"\ntags: \"ablation, time, policy-only, bottleneck, dilation, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Second replication for policy-only late time injection on the std-width nodil backbone. BASE_SEED=123.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n\
    \  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_policytime import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_PolicyTime\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_PolicyTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n  \
    \    )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/new_ablations_late_vs_early.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,seed_set,design,cnn_features,conv_lstm_features,dilation,bottleneck_ratio,entropy,lr,value_loss_coef,clip_norm,score_mean,score_std\n  322,42,late_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.866,0.014\n  327,7,late_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.861,0.020\n  328,123,late_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.866,0.015\n  331,42,early_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.824,0.015\n  332,7,early_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.847,0.020\n  335,123,early_injection_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.840,0.012\n  336,42,no_time_std_nodil,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.835,0.017\n\
    \  338,42,policy_only_late_time,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.829,0.017\n  339,42,value_only_late_time,32/64,64,5,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,1.0,0.766,0.047\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Policy-only vs Value-only late injection (BASE_SEED=42)\n\n  New ablations complete:\n  - Policy-only late time injection (ID 338): 82.9 ± 0.017\n  - Value-only late time injection (ID 339): 76.6 ± 0.047\n\n  Comparison at BASE_SEED=42:\n  - Both-heads late (ID 322): 86.6\n  - Policy-only late (ID 338): 82.9 (−3.7 vs both-heads)\n  - Value-only late (ID 339): 76.6 (−10.0 vs both-heads)\n  - Early injection (ID 331): 82.4\n  - No time (ID 336): 83.5\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - Best is to inject time late to BOTH heads. Policy-only < both-heads and ≈ early; value-only is clearly inferior.\n  - This suggests time aids both policy selection and value bootstrapping; excluding it from either head hurts.\n\n  Next:\n  - Replications for policy-only across {7,123}\
    \ are queued; if consistent, we will not replicate value-only given clear underperformance.\n  - Then finalize the time-channel section with a consolidated table (IDs, seed sets, mean±std) in the addendum.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating experiment options**


  It seems like I’m navigating through some experiment evaluations. I think we can skip further replicates for the value-only approach since it’s clearly inferior, but I want to include single seeds as a negative result. I see that replicating across seeds improves robustness, but my time budget is limited. I just finished both runs, and there are no active experiments, so I can submit replications now. I also notice that we''re set to submit policy-only base seeds 7 and 123, but both heads have replicates already.**Planning experiment updates**


  I’m considering a few updates involving adding experiments 338 and 339 to the CSV. While I can write the archive addendum now, it might be better to finalize it after the replicates. There’s potential for a "late time to both heads" approach with small MLP or gating features. I might skip value-only replicates to save resources, but I want to be thorough by replicating both across seeds 7 and 123. I need to ensure that results are only added to the CSV after they appear.**Considering test options**


  I’m thinking about whether to move on to the next planned test, "double-step + dilated CNN d=3 synergy." The project has shifted focus since the last meta prompt, with new SOTA improving upon previous benchmarks, so this may not happen now. I must remember to use /execute_action lines combined with YAML blocks, including quoting rules. My priority is submitting replicates for policy-only base seeds 7 and 123, and optionally for value-only too, but I need to keep the two-experiment cap in mind. After those finish, I could consider value-only replicates.**Planning experiment submissions**


  I’m preparing to submit two experiments using BASE_SEEDs 7 and 123 for policy-only time injection. First, I’ll import our network and utilize the default PG with AdamW + clip. After that, I''ll update the results CSV to include 338 and 339, as well as refreshing the Archive addendum to show the superiority of "policy-only" over "value-only." While creating a plot script is an option, it might not be necessary. I might need to prune tokens, as we''re currently at 121,664 used. I''ll outline the actions needed for research and submission.**Updating CSV and planning actions**


  I need to update our CSV file with scores for the new entries, specifically for 338 and 339, ensuring I don''t preempt any replicates. I''ll append this to ''new_ablations_late_vs_early.csv'' with known means and standard deviations. In the archive, I''ll respond to capsule #10 with a summary that includes difference values, like for base seed 42 showing the synergy between both head late scores and policy-only. I’ll suggest a small ablation approach and outline the actions: submitting policy-only for seeds 7 and 123, writing to the CSV, and responding in the archive while ensuring proper YAML quoting. Let''s proceed with creating the necessary code.'
