parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"FiLM-style late time modulation (both heads) — std-width nodil (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"architecture, time, FiLM, bottleneck, dilation, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Replace concat-based late time injection with FiLM-style modulation (learned gamma/beta on 256-d preactivations) for both policy and value heads. Backbone unchanged (d=5 main convs, undilated bottlenecks, double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64)). BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj_film import BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTimeFiLM\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n  \
    \        'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([5]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTimeFiLM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/dilation_ablation.csv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id,seed_set,dilation,design,cnn_features,conv_lstm_features,bottleneck_ratio,entropy,lr,value_loss_coef,score_mean,score_std\n\
    \  322,42,5,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.866,0.014\n  327,7,5,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.861,0.020\n  328,123,5,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.866,0.015\n  349,42,6,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.859,0.023\n  353,7,6,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.863,0.018\n  354,123,6,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.880,0.018\n  348,42,3,late_dual_heads,32/64,64,0.25,0.01,4e-4,0.5,0.763,0.054\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/dilation_summary.txt}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Dilation Ablation — Std-width, no FE, late time to both heads, undilated bottlenecks, double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64), AdamW+clip\n\n  Results (mean ± std over 4 seeds per eval):\n  d=5 (baseline):\n    - Seed 42: ID 322 — 86.6 ± 0.014\n    - Seed 7:  ID 327 — 86.1 ± 0.020\n    - Seed 123: ID 328 — 86.6 ± 0.015\n    Overall mean across sets: 86.43\n\n  d=6:\n    - Seed 42: ID 349 — 85.9 ± 0.023\n    - Seed 7:  ID 353 — 86.3\
    \ ± 0.018\n    - Seed 123: ID 354 — 88.0 ± 0.018\n    Overall mean across sets: 86.73 (+0.30 vs d=5)\n\n  d=3:\n    - Seed 42: ID 348 — 76.3 ± 0.054\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - d=6 slightly improves the cross-seed mean (+0.30) but underperforms d=5 on Seed 42 (−0.7). d=3 is clearly poor.\n  - Recommendation: keep d=5 as the default (robust choice), and note d=6 as an optional variant that can yield higher scores on some seed sets (notably Seed 123).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Dilation ablation — replications complete (late dual-head time injection, std-width)\n\n  d=5 (baseline):\n  - 322 (seed 42): 86.6 ± 0.014\n  - 327 (seed 7):  86.1 ± 0.020\n  - 328 (seed 123): 86.6 ± 0.015\n  Cross-seed mean: 86.43\n\n  d=6:\n  - 349 (seed 42): 85.9 ± 0.023\n  - 353 (seed 7):  86.3 ± 0.018\n  - 354 (seed 123): 88.0 ± 0.018\n  Cross-seed mean: 86.73 (+0.30 vs d=5)\n\n  d=3:\n  - 348 (seed 42): 76.3 ± 0.054\n\n  Takeaway:\n  - d=6 yields a modest\
    \ +0.30 uplift in the cross-seed mean, driven by a strong gain on seed 123, but is −0.7 on seed 42. d=3 is strongly inferior.\n  - Default recommendation: d=5 for robustness; d=6 as an optional variant when aiming for peak scores.\n\n  Artifacts updated:\n  - storage/zephyr/results/dilation_ablation.csv\n  - storage/zephyr/results/dilation_summary.txt\n\n  Also re-submitted the FiLM-style late time modulation (both heads) with BASE_SEED=42; once it finishes we will report whether FiLM improves over concat for late injection.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance results**\n\nI need to evaluate the next steps as Zephyr I (Recursive Agent). The new results show that dilation with 6 replicates yields scores of 86.3 and 88.0, surpassing the baseline of 86.6. It looks like the latest scores indicate improvement with d=6. However, earlier I thought d=5 was the best due to performance with single seeds. Overall, d=6 appears to slightly outperform d=5 based on the averages across multiple seeds, even though it underperformed for one seed.**Updating research findings**\n\nThere’s interesting variance here, possibly due to the compute budget. Dilation 6 seems more beneficial for some seeds, prompting me to update the dilation_ablation findings. Overall, d=6 looks comparable or slightly better across seeds, though it's lower for seed 42. I should replicate d=5 across the same seed sets for a fair comparison. Also, I can now submit FiLM since the two-run limit has been cleared. Next, I’ll submit FiLM and update the\
  \ CSV tables with the new results for dilation 6.**Planning next steps**\n\nI'm pleased with our progress! We have a token budget of about 193k, which is around 72%. While pruning is on the table, it isn't necessary just yet. To keep things moving, I’ll focus on a couple of immediate actions: I need to submit the FiLM run again and update the CSV for new_ablations_late_vs_early since that file is related to time injection, not dilation. Maintaining our results is essential too!**Updating files and planning next steps**\n\nI need to update the dilation_ablation.csv to include entries for seeds 7 and 123 for d=6, considering adding baseline entries for d=5 too. However, since the previous CSV only included means, I could create a new comprehensive dilation CSV or keep it simple. \n\nI’ll also update the dilation_summary.txt with results across seeds and their averages. Additionally, I need to revise the Archive addendum (#10) with new details, and I can add a plot script for dilation if\
  \ time allows.\n\nLastly, it’s time to submit FiLM using the network file we prepared earlier. After that, I’ll dive into some research!**Planning actions for updates**\n\nI’m laying out our next actions: First, we’ll go to research and submit the first run with the FiLM config. I’ll reference the existing code snippet for the network module. After that, I’ll update the storage by writing a new dilation CSV and summary.\n\nIn the archive, I’ll summarize the latest results, including the d=6 triad and comparisons with d=5. We’ll create or append to the existing CSV for better organization. It’s important to ensure proper YAML formatting throughout. Lastly, I’ll prepare the necessary execute actions for each command. Let’s get to it!"
