thinking_content: "**Considering pruning tokens**\n\nI'm looking at the new heavy tokens from 180 onward and wondering if we can prune the range of 146:160, but I need to check the actual history first for effective pruning. I’ll head over to token_management to inspect before deciding on pruning options, like 150:170. I want to preserve important content while considering that we have summaries saved. My main action plan involves inspecting token_management and then deciding which ranges to prune.**Planning pruning commands**\n\nI can proceed to ask the tool to prune some ranges, but I need to use the prune_response command directly since it’s not interactive. First, I'll check the Dialogue History to see the current valid ranges, as it automatically displays the list there. Since this is one turn, I can chain the commands and first go to token_management, then execute the prune_response for ranges like 150:191, but I’ll exclude 187 just in case. This should work!**Deciding on token pruning**\n\
  \nThe prune tool accepts comma-separated ranges, like 150:170 or 171:179. I need to be careful because we previously pruned 87:145 and 162:178, so trying to prune those again would just skip already pruned ticks. Pruning 180:191 might remove important recent context, though. Since the token load is at 75%, I should consider pruning ranges like 146:161 and 179:185. Aiming for around 45% active tokens is good, but I must keep some immediate context. Pruning the range 0:120 could be an option since we've summarized before.**Planning token pruning**\n\nI can prune ticks 120:170 to help reduce the token load by about 50 ticks. I plan to go to token_management and execute a prune_response for 120:170, and maybe 179 too, since it could be heavy. We previously pruned 171:178, and I want to be careful not to prune important content after 180. It's essential to keep the last 15 ticks for context. I'll also consider updating the archive about the d=6 cross-seed results, but since I've already done\
  \ that, I might skip it.**Planning actions for token management**\n\nI should consider adding the results CSV for dilation that displays d=6 across seeds. Perhaps we can mention this later. Now, I want to go ahead and start with the necessary actions. \n\nFirst, I'll go to token_management and execute the prune_response for ranges 120:170 and 179. It’s not absolutely necessary to record these in private memory, but it would be helpful to keep track of what I pruned. So my final message will include those actions!"
