- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr')\n\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj import (\n      BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime\n  )\n\n  # Repro script: Late time injection to BOTH heads (concat), dilation d=6 on main 3x3 convs,\n  # undilated 3x3 inside bottlenecks; double-step LN-ConvLSTM(64); single-head AttnPool; AdamW+clip.\n  # Note: d=6 yields higher peak on some seed sets (e.g., Seed 123 = 88.0), while d=5 is slightly more robust on Seed 42.\n  BASE_SEED = 42  # set to 7 or 123 for standardized seed sets\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n\
    \          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n\n  def test():\n      # Lightweight sanity check (no training)\n      import jax.numpy as jnp\n      import jax.random as jr\n      net = create_network({\n          'cnn_features_1': 32, 'cnn_features_2': 64, 'conv_lstm_features':\
    \ 64,\n          'dilation': 6, 'bottleneck_ratio': 0.25\n      })\n      x = jnp.zeros((2, 8, 8, 8))\n      done = jnp.zeros((2,))\n      params = net.init(jr.PRNGKey(0), x, done, None)\n      logits, value, state = net.apply(params, x, done, None)\n      assert logits.shape == (2, 4) and value.shape == (2,)\n      return \"OK: d=6 variant shapes sane\"\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Repro script for d=6 variant (peak-score option)\n\n  - Path: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py\n  - Configuration: late time (concat) to BOTH heads; std-width 32/64; main 3×3 convs with dilation d=6; undilated 3×3 inside bottlenecks; double-step LN‑ConvLSTM(64); AdamW+clip.\n  - Results summary:\n    • Seed 42: 85.9 ± 0.023 (ID 349)\n    • Seed 7:  86.3 ± 0.018 (ID 353)\n    • Seed 123: 88.0 ± 0.018 (ID 354)\n    Cross-seed mean: 86.73 (vs d=5 mean 86.43). Recommendation: d=5 remains default for robustness; d=6 is an optional\
    \ variant for peak score (notably stronger on Seed 123).\n\n  - Also tested FiLM-style late time modulation (both heads): 83.4 ± 0.029 (ID 356), which underperforms late concat; keep concat.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 201 — Status + next micro-sweeps\n  - Running: d=6 attention temp tweaks at BASE_SEED=42 (IDs 360, 361).\n  - Dilation ablation done: d=6 > d=5 on cross-seed mean (+0.30), with Seed 123 notably strong (88.0). Keep d=5 default; d=6 optional peak.\n  - FiLM late time (both heads) underperforms vs concat (83.4). Recorded as negative.\n  - Next once slots free:\n    1) Entropy micro-sweep for d=6 at BASE_SEED=42: {0.009, 0.010, 0.011} (expect minor stabilization).\n    2) Value-loss coef micro-sweep for d=6 at BASE_SEED=42: {0.45, 0.50, 0.55}.\n  - Token plan: prune again after cooldown if usage >75%.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering experimental limitations**\n\nI realize we can prune older ticks once the cooldown ends, which is currently seven ticks. Since we’re running two experiments (IDs 360 and 361), we can’t submit new ones until one finishes; the station only allows two concurrent experiments. That gives me time to update documentation or prepare a script for the variant d=6. I’ll write submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py to storage and mention it in the archive addendum. I’ll also reflect on potential improvements, like multi-scale or hierarchical attention pooling, while keeping compute budgets in mind.**Exploring adjustments for improvement**\n\nI'm considering how adjusting the attention temperature might lead to small gains, especially since we've previously tested standard widths with d=5 and only saw minor improvements at 85.6 with base seed 42. Now, with d=6, the effect may differ. After our current runs, I could micro-sweep the entropy coefficient or explore minor dropout/reg\
  \ to address overfitting. \n\nIt’s also worth revisiting gating forget bias modifications from previous tests. Lastly, I might test applying layer normalization to attention logits before softmax to stabilize distributions, though it could change behavior.**Planning updates and scripts**\n\nI need to update the archive with a note after the attention temperature runs finish, but they’re not done yet. In the meantime, I can plan in private memory. Token usage is at a high 74%, which is okay. Since we're still in cooldown from the last prune, I realize it might be over soon. \n\nI’ll create the new d=6 SOTA script. Here’s the plan: write the submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py script, update the archive with the note about d=6 cross-seed results, and store the plan for a micro-sweep of entropy around 0.009–0.011. Optionally, I may review the Research Counter filter, but that’s not crucial right now.**Implementing action steps**\n\nLet's get started on the actions. First, I need to write the\
  \ script in the storage location. Then I'll update the archive by replying to capsule 10 with a note. I also plan to document the code while ensuring it imports from the existing network module. We previously used BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime for both d=5 and d=6, and I’ll replicate it with a dilation parameter of 6. \n\nThe new file will be stored as submission_sota_late_dual_d6.py, with BASE_SEED set to 42 and comments noting it’s recommended for peak runs. I’ll also emphasize that this variant is only for high scores and mention the crossover mean compared to d=5. Lastly, I may update the results CSV to include new variants, but I cannot add results until the runs are complete."
