  I''m planning to reuse the base lateinj while setting dilation to 6 and keeping the attn_temp default at 1.0 since the earlier class doesn''t have that parameter. To maintain consistency, I’ll import from the B5 module, where attn_temp isn''t included. I’ll also update the archive with attention temp results of 0.95 and 1.05, noting a slight uplift from the baseline. Plus, I''ll ensure YAML quoting for titles that have special characters. Let''s proceed with submitting those experiments!**Organizing titles and code**


  I''m reviewing the title structure which has no colons, only hyphens and parentheses. The abstract includes a colon, but it doesn’t interfere with my notes since it’s just a content block. I’ll craft the code for the entropy micro-sweep by importing the base network and setting dilation to 6. I’ll define hyperparameters using tune.choice for entropy while fixing the others. It''s also important to limit to 4 hyperparameter values. Finally, I’ll make sure to log results in the CSV file for attn temp. Let''s get to assembling!**Planning submission actions**


  I’m getting ready to take some actions: first, I''m heading to the research area. Then, I’ll submit one YAML for the entropy micro-sweep and another for the value_loss micro-sweep. After that, I''ll go to the archive and provide a brief update on the attention temp results along with the plan mentioned. I need to ensure the code''s validity too while writing content to import from the ''storage/zephyr'' path. Lastly, I’ll utilize the network from ''networks.bottleneck_d5_double_step_std_nodil_bottleneck_lateinj''. Let’s get this crafted!'
