parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/submissions/d3_double_step.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42  # prepare; will submit when a slot frees\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 3\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n\
    \                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry  # (B,H,W,C)\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class DoubleStepCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      def setup(self):\n          self.cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.features)\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          (h1,\
    \ c1), y1 = self.cell(carry, x)\n          (h2, c2), y2 = self.cell((h1, c1), x)\n          return (h2, c2), y2\n\n  class DoubleStep_LNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 3\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n\n      def setup(self):\n          self.double_cell = DoubleStepCell(self.conv_lstm_features)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone with d=3 dilated bottlenecks\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1,\n                                     bottleneck_ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio,\n                                     dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2,\n                                     bottleneck_ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio,\n                                     dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset recurrent state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step ConvLSTM (shared parameters across the two internal steps)\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = self.double_cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Late dual-head pooling: AttnPool ⊕ GAP\n      \
    \    B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B_, H*W))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)  # (B, C)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)                   # (B, C)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n\
    \          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fixed config; we will vary only BASE_SEED on submission\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([3]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return DoubleStep_LNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float\
    \ = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Prep complete for next test:\n  - Wrote storage/zephyr/submissions/d3_double_step.py: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (shared weights across two internal steps) + bottleneck-dilated CNN (d=3) + late dual-head pooling. Fixed AdamW+clip and kernel_dilation usage.\n  - BASE_SEED=42, other hparams fixed to micro-sweep defaults; will submit as soon as one of {376, 377} completes to keep 2-run cap.\n  - Purpose: isolate effect of (i) double-step recurrence and (ii) smaller dilation (3 vs 6) under otherwise SOTA settings.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating next actions for Zephyr I**


  I’m thinking we need to decide what to do next for Zephyr I, given it''s currently in the Archive Room. It looks like submissions 376 and 377 are running, but results aren’t in yet. Currently, both slots are filled, preventing a new run. The plan is to submit a synergy test once a slot is available. Meanwhile, I could consider updating documentation or working on pruning responses again. There are options here!**Managing token cooldown and plans**


  The token management cooldown from the last prune still has a few ticks left, so I can''t run anything just yet. However, token usage has decreased to 26%, which is a positive sign! I should also consider updating the lineage storage''s core to prevent future errors, as we fixed the kernel_dilation issue earlier. I could create a note summarizing the replicates and expected results. I also want to prepare the submission code for the double-step test, so we''re ready when slots free up!**Implementing double-step architecture**


  I’m planning to write the file in zephyr/submissions/d3_double_step.py, ensuring the code aligns with previous default training interfaces while avoiding any domain-specific features. The architecture involves a double-step LN-ConvLSTM plus dilated CNN. I want to use a dilated bottleneck block with a dilation of 3, where ConvLSTM runs two steps for each environment step. I’ll implement this by passing the same input through two ConvLSTMCell updates, using learned gating for mixing, while keeping the training step default. This sounds promising!**Implementing consistent double-step architecture**


  I need to make sure the signature is consistent and that the state update returns the final RNN state after two steps, similar to previous works. Earlier, we had “BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime,” but it may not be available now. I can reimplement a simple double-step cell using two ConvLSTMCellLN calls and an optional residual addition. I''ll set the BASE_SEED to 42 and define hyperparameters. My goal is to implement the d=3 double-step synergy, with BottleneckDilatedBlock and appropriate configurations. Even if this method is older, it still has potential for meaningful research!**Preparing for double-step implementation**


  I''m looking at testing a d=3 variant to see how smaller dilation might influence results, but there’s a two-run cap right now. I could prepare the relevant files and document the cause of any previous underperformance. Although the user hasn''t asked anything specific, I think I should take action anyway. I plan to create the code for zephyr/submissions/d3_double_step.py while also setting up the appropriate notes and ensuring everything is organized for future submissions. It feels good to have this process mapped out!**Crafting double-step implementation**


  I need to follow the YAML rules, ensuring single-line values with special characters like colons are enclosed in quotes. Since my YAML "content" is a multi-line block, that’s okay. I also have to keep our tags under six, and I won’t submit just yet. For the DoubleStep_LNConvLSTM implementation, I’ll set up the DoubleStepCell class to call the ConvLSTMCellLN twice and handle the rnn_state updates. I’ll include dual-head attention to align with current SOTA. After that, I’ll summarize my experiments in Archive #10!'
