role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: No time injection — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip, BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"ablation, time, double-step, convlstm, layernorm, dilated\"\nabstract: \"Quantify the effect of late time injection by removing it in the d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM with late dual-head pooling. All else fixed (br=0.28, AdamW+clip). BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3),\
    \ padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\
    \n  class DoubleStepCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      def setup(self):\n          self.cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.features)\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          (h1, c1), _ = self.cell(carry, x)\n          (h2, c2), y2 = self.cell((h1, c1), x)\n          return (h2, c2), y2\n\n  class LateDualHead_DoubleStep_NoTime(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n\n      def setup(self):\n          self.ds = DoubleStepCell(self.conv_lstm_features)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1,\n                           \
    \          bottleneck_ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio,\n                                     dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2,\n                                     bottleneck_ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio,\n                                     dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # Init/reset recurrent state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step LN-ConvLSTM\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = self.ds((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n      \
    \    rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n          # Late dual-head pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP) — NO time concatenation\n          B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B_, H*W))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z);\
    \ val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return LateDualHead_DoubleStep_NoTime(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n\
    \      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating optimization approaches**


  I''m considering testing a double-step with specific parameters. Although earlier micro-sweeps suggest 0.28 is optimal, I wonder if testing training stability with different configurations could yield better results. I noted that AdamW with a weight decay of 1e-4 performed best. There''s also the possibility of testing time injection ablation to isolate improvements. Additionally, storing corrections for bugs in lineage storage sounds like a good plan for future reference.**Planning for optimization experiments**


  I think I should utilize a free slot to run the d=6 replicate with my code to see if I can surpass the 88 mark. Although I already achieved 88.0 with 354, other replicates fell short at around 85.2-85.3 with a fixed double-step late time injection. This could be related to differences in configurations. I''ll need to replicate the micro-sweeps’ setup and document everything as I go along to ensure clarity in my methods.**Planning code reproducibility tests**


  With one free slot, I can queue the d=6 replicate to check the reproducibility of my code against earlier micro-sweep ID 367. While replicates 380 and 381 used d=6 and double-step, they only reached around 85.3, which might be acceptable. I should also test d=3 against d=6, plus consider running d=4 or d=5 to observe trends. There''s mention of "Ablation Study 1: d=4," but it may not apply to my architecture. Pruning tokens once we exceed 75% could be interesting, though we''re currently at 38%.**Planning experiments and organization**


  Since this is the "Research Station," I want to review the results once d=3 finishes. In the meantime, I can queue the next experiment. I’m considering testing the "Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM" with d=6 while adjusting the attention temperature to see if it improves the 86.3 found in previous micro-sweeps. I’ll also think about adding feature LN after pooling for d=6, but I should change only one variable at a time. Additionally, organizing code into a single source of truth would help prevent drift.**Finalizing the ablation submission**


  To avoid future mismatches, I’m proposing to write a module named "sota_master.py" that includes various classes and options for configurations. This will help ensure consistent setups across different seeds. With one slot currently running, I can queue an ablation to remove late time injection from the d=6 architecture, which will help examine its effects. I need to ensure the YAML title follows quoting rules due to the colon. I’ll also limit tags and length appropriately, and then generate the action commands to submit. Let''s proceed quickly!'
