parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Warmup-only LR (2.5%) — no-time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"lr-schedule, warmup-only, ablation, double-step, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Linear 2.5% warmup to base LR, then constant. All else fixed on PG default (no-time, wd=0.0) to isolate schedule effects. BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from double_step_notime_core import create_network as base_create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n\
    \      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return base_create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      total_updates = 39062\n      warmup_steps = max(1, int(0.025 * total_updates))  # 2.5% warmup\n      schedule = optax.join_schedules(\n          schedules=[\n              optax.linear_schedule(init_value=0.0, end_value=learning_rate, transition_steps=warmup_steps),\n              optax.constant_schedule(learning_rate),\n          ],\n          boundaries=[warmup_steps],\n      )\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.0))\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Warmup-only LR (2.5%) — time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)\"\ntags: \"lr-schedule, warmup-only, ablation, double-step, convlstm\"\nabstract: \"Linear 2.5% warmup to base LR, then constant. All else fixed on PG default (time-injected, wd=0.0). BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ sys, optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from double_step_time_core import create_network as base_create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([5e-4]),  # matches seed-42 wd=0.0 baseline\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return base_create_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 5e-4):\n      total_updates = 39062\n      warmup_steps = max(1, int(0.025 * total_updates))  # 2.5% warmup\n      schedule = optax.join_schedules(\n          schedules=[\n              optax.linear_schedule(init_value=0.0,\
    \ end_value=learning_rate, transition_steps=warmup_steps),\n              optax.constant_schedule(learning_rate),\n          ],\n          boundaries=[warmup_steps],\n      )\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n                         optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.0))\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  LR schedule ablation (seed 42, wd=0.0) — negative result:\n  - ID 414 (no-time, 5% warmup + cosine to 0.1×): 65.9 ± 0.024 vs constant 85.7 → severe degradation.\n  - ID 415 (time,   5% warmup + cosine to 0.1×): 74.2 ± 0.026 vs constant 84.9 → strong degradation.\n\n  Interpretation: Under a strict 30-min/seed budget, decaying LR (to 0.1×) cuts late learning; compute-limited regime prefers high, constant LR.\n\n  Follow-up (submitted):\n  - Warmup-only (2.5% linear warmup, then constant) for both variants at seed 42 to test if a tiny burn-in helps without starving later updates.\n\n  If warmup-only\
    \ shows no benefit, we will keep constant LR as part of the finalized PG recipe and record these as significant negative results.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating performance schedule**


  I need to respond thoughtfully here. It turns out the cosine learning rate schedule with warmup has severely impacted performance, dropping from a baseline of 85-86 to 65.9 without time and 74.2 with it. The current setup seems harmful, so I''m considering proposing the next steps to fix this. Perhaps a shorter warmup is needed, or we could explore different shapes for the learning rate schedule, like a linear warmup followed by a constant or a one-cycle policy. It definitely feels like we reduced the learning rate too early!**Planning next steps**


  Looking ahead, I think the best move is to revert to a constant learning rate and document the negative results from the schedule ablation. I can update the Archive addendum with the insight that certain schedules might be harmful under this compute budget. I’ll propose a quick check by using a constant learning rate with a short warmup ramp. I’m considering testing a step learning rate drop for the end but might skip that given our previous results. I’ll also submit two quick follow-up runs to test alternatives since slots are free.**Proposing new schedules**


  I’m proposing a couple of strategies. One is a warmup-only schedule with a 2.5% linear warmup, then a constant learning rate without any weight decay, using a specific seed. Another option is a high-floor cosine schedule with the same warmup percentage but also a floor of 0.7 or 0.8. However, given our recent negative results, it seems prudent to test the warmup-only constant first. I think implementing this with the optax library could work well!**Creating a new learning schedule**


  I realize that I need to create a learning schedule that gradually increases from 0 to the desired learning rate over the warmup steps and then remains constant afterward. The `optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule` can''t do this, so I''ll use `join_schedules` instead, combining a linear schedule with a constant schedule. I''ll be mindful of transition boundaries, ensuring we respect our per-step count. I''ll implement adjustments to the optimizer and update the Archive addendum with the negative results, noting that decaying the learning rate seems harmful given our time-limited compute.**Preparing submission tasks**


  I need to make sure we''re in the Private Memory Room for submission. Our next steps involve going to research, submitting two warmup-only experiments (one for no-time and one for time), and updating the archive with the negative results. We’ll prepare the code tasks and set up a YAML submission, ensuring it includes the right titles and abstracts.


  I’ll make sure the necessary files exist since we’ve referenced `double_step_time_core` and `double_step_notime_core` before. We’ll keep the learning rate at 5e-4 for comparability. I’ve got to ensure everything is structured well in the code, especially for YAML formatting. Let''s get this done!'
