- text: 'Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Private Memory Room.

    Recap: Submitted two A/B replications (IDs 447: head 64 ReLU, and 448: head 128 ReLU) under no-time PG. Earlier micro-sweep (ID 441) succeeded after auto-debug; best was head 128 ReLU. Concurrency limit reached; waiting on results.

    Plan: Review ID 441 logs for details; record concise notes. When 447/448 finish, finalize default head width accordingly and update README.


    # Fetch detailed logs for the micro-sweep result

    /execute_action{goto research}

    /execute_action{review 441}'
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on experiments**\n\nI see there might be some inconsistencies in the scoring because we earlier had a baseline of 62-64, but now some advanced works present scores of 83-88 with PPO synergy while ID 441 is only at 74.3. Maybe this is due to the \"no-time PG\" and the architecture not performing well. While we wait for IDs 447 and 448, I could write documentation or a small analysis script to parse the results CSV. It seems we also have the option to update the README once the results arrive.**Managing experiment limits**\n\nThe system says that each agent can run a maximum of two experiments at once, and since we have IDs 447 and 448 currently running, we shouldn't submit more. We can research and preview or rank, but that's on hold for now. Alternatively, we might create a small addendum in the Archive. Tokens are fine at 23%. I could also summarize the results in private memory and decide on width defaults. Writing unit tests for the LN gate cell or updating\
  \ \"late_dualhead_pg.py\" could be another productive use of time.**Reviewing evaluation logs**\n\nThe research team indicates that we can review evaluation logs using the evaluation ID, which could help us copy the best trial hyperparameters and training curves. Since we already have the debug report, we can run `/execute_action{review 441}` to fetch the code and logs for storage in private memory. I might also consider creating a public memory note, but I need to be careful not to share any raw code or explicit configurations due to collaboration rules. It’s a bit tricky, but we've been careful about it!**Understanding storage permissions**\n\nI've noticed that we've just written the network into storage. While other agents can read the lineage storage, they cannot write to it, which the rules confirm. This raises a question: sharing code through shared storage seems like it would be equivalent to sharing raw code, which is against the rules. It says high-level discussions are allowed,\
  \ but not sharing raw code or explicit configurations. It feels a bit contradictory since agents can read from different lineages, yet they shouldn't share code. This situation is definitely puzzling!**Planning next actions**\n\nHistorically, I’ve written code to my own storage, which is accessible, but I shouldn't actively share it. No immediate ask from the user, so I’ll keep moving on. The plan involves a few steps: \n\nFirst, I’ll go to Research Counter and review ID 441 to confirm training curves; the best trial shows head_hidden=128 and the ReLU activation function. We can’t submit more due to the concurrency limit, so I can note any debug fixes in Private Memory and plan for token pruning later. The next pruning action can happen at tick 259. \n\nNow, I’ll produce the actions needed: executing the research and review actions without any excessive commentary. Let's get started!"
