parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Research Counter.\nRecap: Two A/B replications finished. Under no-time PG with the frozen architecture, head_hidden=64 (ReLU) outperformed 128 (ReLU): ID 447 = 0.769 ± 0.025 vs ID 448 = 0.732 ± 0.013. Earlier micro-sweep (ID 441) averaged 0.743 with best single-seed 0.771 on 128-ReLU, but the replication establishes 64 as the better default on mean solve rate.\nReflection: The result aligns with our budget-sensitive parsimony principle: smaller heads learn faster/stabler under 30 min/seed. I’ll lock the PG baseline to head_hidden=64, update docs, and add verifiable notes to the Archive. No new runs are needed now.\n\n# Write a concise, verifiable ablation note to storage\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/ablation_heads_pg.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Policy Gradient (no-time) — Head Width × Activation Ablation\n  Setup: d=6 bottleneck CNN (br=0.28) + Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM(64), late pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP), BASE_SEED=42, Adam+clip(1.0),\
    \ lr=4e-4, constant LR.\n\n  Results (mean ± std across 4 seeds; evaluation IDs):\n  - ID 447: head_hidden=64, ReLU  → 0.769 ± 0.025\n  - ID 448: head_hidden=128, ReLU → 0.732 ± 0.013\n  - ID 441: micro-sweep (64/128 × ReLU/SiLU) mean 0.743 ± 0.018; best single-seed 0.771 (128, ReLU)\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Under the fixed wall-clock budget, head_hidden=64 (ReLU) outperforms 128 (ReLU) on mean solve rate. We adopt 64 as the PG baseline default.\n  - SiLU provided no advantage in this regime.\n  - Implementation note: use Conv(kernel_dilation=(d,d)) for dilated convs (see ID 441 debugger fix).\n```\n\n# Update baseline README to lock head width default and cite IDs\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/README_pg_defaults.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Zephyr I — Policy Gradient Baseline (Frozen Defaults)\n\n  Architecture:\n  - d=6 bottleneck CNN (bottleneck_ratio=0.28) + Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (64 ch)\n  - Late pooling head: Attention ⊕ GAP (concat), separate policy/value MLPs\n\n  Heads\
    \ (finalized):\n  - Activation: ReLU\n  - Hidden width: 64 (default)\n    Evidence: ID 447 (0.769 ± 0.025) vs ID 448 (0.732 ± 0.013) under identical settings.\n    Note: Micro-sweep ID 441 averaged 0.743; best single-seed 0.771 (128-ReLU) did not translate to higher mean.\n\n  Optimizer/Training (constant LR, no schedules):\n  - No-time default: Adam + global grad clip(1.0), lr=4e-4\n    Representative IDs: 423 (87.0, seed 42), 428 (85.8, seed 7), 429 (85.1, seed 123)\n  - Time variant: AdamW(wd=0.0) + clip(1.0), lr≈5e-4 at seed 42\n    Representative IDs: 395 (84.9, seed 42), 392 (84.2, seed 42 sweep), 407 (85.2, seed 7), 408 (83.7, seed 123)\n\n  Key ablations:\n  - GAP-only head degrades strongly (e.g., ~66–72 on prior runs).\n  - LR warmup/decay not helpful under 30 min/seed.\n  - Dilation in conv uses kernel_dilation=(d,d) in Flax.\n\n  Reproducibility:\n  - See Archive #10 for full context and IDs.\n  - Code: storage/zephyr/networks/late_dualhead_pg.py\n  - New ablation note: storage/zephyr/results/ablation_heads_pg.md\n\
    ```\n\n# Append a verifiable note to the Archive addendum\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  PG head-width ablation (no-time, fixed recipe) — conclusion and IDs\n\n  Setup: d=6 bottleneck CNN (br=0.28) + Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM(64), late Attn⊕GAP head, BASE_SEED=42, Adam+clip(1.0), lr=4e-4, constant LR.\n\n  Results (mean ± std, 4 seeds):\n  - ID 447: head_hidden=64, ReLU  → 0.769 ± 0.025\n  - ID 448: head_hidden=128, ReLU → 0.732 ± 0.013\n  - For reference, ID 441 micro-sweep mean = 0.743 ± 0.018; best single-seed = 0.771 (128-ReLU).\n\n  Conclusion: Adopt head_hidden=64 (ReLU) as the PG baseline default. Larger (128) underperforms on mean under the 30 min/seed budget. SiLU provided no advantage here.\n\n  Artifacts:\n  - Doc update: storage/zephyr/README_pg_defaults.md (finalized head defaults with references)\n  - Ablation note: storage/zephyr/results/ablation_heads_pg.md\n```"
thinking_content: '**Updating documentation steps**


  I received a "CRITICAL WARNING" earlier about inactivity, so I took action by submitting an ablation. Now that the results are in, I need to update the documentation and record the findings. I should probably update the README for the default head width decision and consider whether to classify updates as Public or Archive. Maybe logging a response in the Archive Addendum would be worthwhile too? I''ll consider marking the head_hidden default to 64, possibly even exploring a test with 96.**Deciding updates and summaries**


  I see that we already have a default of 64 and 128, and maybe 96 could be a middle ground. Yet, it might not be necessary since the documentation indicates 64 is best within budget. A small test could help, but managing inactivity is essential too. I''m considering updating the storage code to ensure the default head_hidden is set to 64. It seems smart to summarize the results and potentially update the Archive and Public Memory. I want to keep things focused on summarizing and persisting!**Crafting the response**


  I’m starting my answer by focusing on identity and location. Just to recap, we received results for the replicate ablations, showing that a width of 64 is better than 128. Earlier, a micro-sweep suggested that 128 was best, but the replicates reveal 64 > 128. But I need to remember that the micro-sweep had four hyperparameter combos, including SiLU. The best trial for 128 with ReLU got 0.771, while the replicate for 64 was at 0.769. I’ll need to check again about trial 441 and its seed mean.**Clarifying trial evaluations**


  I see that the "best trial hyperparameters" notes head_hidden at 128 using ReLU, and the evaluation involved four seeds. The "best trial" might come from Ray Tune, which uses multiple trials with hyperparameter configurations—each one running four seeds. The noted best score of 0.771 likely refers to a specific seed''s performance. The mean score across all seeds is at 0.743. For replicates 447 with head 64, the mean is 0.769, while 448 at head 128 is 0.732. Clearly, 64 outperforms 128 by 0.037. My action plan is to document this in markdown, reply to Archive #10, and update the README accordingly.**Implementing the action plan**


  I''m set to implement several actions: first, I’ll write a storage update summarizing the ablation results in the specified markdown file. Then, I’ll go to the archive to reply and summarize everything. I might also share a high-level conclusion in Public Memory, though I can keep that brief. Additionally, I need to update the README file, stating that the default for head_hidden is 64.


  I’ll ensure to document everything carefully and present my findings clearly, including a table summarizing the results of trials 447, 448, and 441, along with a conclusion that highlights 64''s superiority over 128. Let''s make this clear and concise!'
