parts:
- text: 'Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Token Management Room.

    Recap: Pruned recent high-word ticks; token load ~24%. Last experiment ID 441 completed (74.3 mean) after debugger fixed Conv kernel_dilation; stored a clean late_dualhead_pg.py with correct API. Head-width A/B replicates were submitted earlier; I’ll locate their evaluation IDs/status.

    Plan: Query my recent submissions (filter heads/replicate) to confirm replicate IDs and outcomes; then log results to Private Memory.


    # Locate recent head-width replicates

    /execute_action{goto research}

    /execute_action{rank author}

    /execute_action{filter heads}

    /execute_action{page_size 50}

    /execute_action{preview all}'
thinking_content: '**Clarifying evaluation IDs**


  I could update the README or archive with ablation results for the 441 replicates. However, earlier notes mentioned IDs 447, 448, 451, and 452, but I haven''t seen these evaluations created and they may not exist. I should avoid referencing nonexistent IDs. There were also two earlier replicates submitted at tick 252, but I don’t have evaluation IDs for those. The "Running Experiments" at tick 251 only shows 441. Did we actually submit those replicates?**Recording experiment results**


  I''m unsure about the results or evaluation IDs for the replicates, and they might not have been queued due to the two concurrent limit, even though we had capacity. Without confirmation messages, I can''t assume they ran. So, I should record the 441 result in Private Memory and note the debugger fix. It seems best to propose no further experiments right now, but I could update the README with the dilation parameter fix and consider scheduling new replicates later. To avoid inactivity warnings, running a small additional test might be an idea, but we can rest for now.**Updating memory and referencing evaluations**


  I might consider going to Archive or Public Memory to add a reference for evaluation 441, but I should note that its score of 74.3 is lower than the SOTA of 89 with PPO, which relates to the PG baseline. So, the safer action here is to write to Private Memory, documenting the 441 score, fix, and planned replicate status. Then, I could look into our recently submitted replicates, considering that we might not have evaluation IDs. Ranking submissions by author could help find our work first; I could execute that command. Filtering tags like "replicate" may also help in this search.**Planning actions for evaluation**


  I think I should start by ranking authors and then filter for our replicates. So, here''s my plan: first, I’ll provide a recap of identities. Then, I’ll move to the research section and execute the commands: rank authors, filter by "replicate," and maybe set the page size to 50. For the preview, I know "preview all" shows the latest 100 submissions, but I want filtered results to focus only on replicates. I could also review the evaluation logs, though I''m unsure of the evaluation IDs.**Listing submitted evaluations**


  I’m thinking of listing our submitted evaluations sorted by author and maybe asking for a page size of 200 to view more. I’ll scan for recently submitted replicates by title. Since the station will render a table and I don’t have to parse the data myself, I can rely on “running experiments” or “submitted evaluations” to find the ID. I can’t scroll or search by title, but filtering by the "heads" tag should help narrow it down. I’ll implement the actions clearly in the station format while making sure to provide a recap and plan beforehand.'
