tick: 263
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 263\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on elegant and data efficient breakthroughs through rigorous experimentation and synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 241)\nStatus: PG recipe finalized (optimizer choice resolved).\nDefaults:\n  - No-time: Adam + clip(1.0), lr=4e-4.\n  - Time: AdamW(wd=0.0) + clip(1.0), lr≈5e-4 at seed 42.\nLR schedules: negative (constant LR locked).\nNext: Pause new runs; only respond if a compelling low-cost test emerges. Keep tokens <75%; prune if needed.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 66,914 / 270,000 used (25%)\n- Agent Age: 263 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation 458: Insight I: CNNLSTM Baseline (System H-Params Reproduction)**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, reproduction, fixed hyperparameters\nAbstract: This\
    \ submission aims to reproduce the system's RNN baseline score (40.8) for the Sokoban RL task. It uses the `CNNLSTM` architecture with fixed hyperparameters for `entropy_coef` (0.01) and `value_loss_coef` (0.5), matching those specified in `system/defaults.py` and observed in the system baseline's best trial. This serves as a control experiment to establish a reliable baseline before further architectural advancements within the Insight lineage.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 457: Validation of H-Param Sweep on SingleStepWideLSTM**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: validation, architecture, convlstm, parsimony, ppo, hyperparameter\nAbstract: This experiment is a validation run of the best hyperparameter configuration found in my previous sweep (ID 454). The goal is to confirm the high score of 88.6 achieved by a single trial. This run uses learning_rate=5e-4 and entropy_coef=0.01, with the proven optimal vlc=0.5 for this architecture.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 456: Aether\
    \ I: Triple-Step ConvLSTM (vlc=0.5) - Recurrent Depth Ablation**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: recurrent-depth, ablation, architecture, convlstm, ppo, value-loss-coef\nAbstract: This experiment tests a triple-step ConvLSTM variant of Aether I's SOTA architecture with `value_loss_coef=0.5`. This serves as a comparative run against `vlc=0.45` to assess the sensitivity of a deeper recurrent network to value function weighting, as part of the broader 'why' investigation.\nScore: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 455: Aether I: Triple-Step ConvLSTM (vlc=0.45) - Recurrent Depth Ablation**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: recurrent-depth, ablation, architecture, convlstm, ppo, value-loss-coef\nAbstract: This experiment tests a triple-step ConvLSTM variant of Aether I's SOTA architecture with the previously identified optimal `value_loss_coef=0.45`. This investigates the impact of increased recurrent depth on performance and `value_loss_coef` sensitivity, contributing to understanding why optimal `vlc` is architecture-dependent.\n\
    Score: running\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 454: H-Param Sweep on SingleStepWideLSTM: LR and Entropy**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: ablation, architecture, convlstm, parsimony, ppo, hyperparameter\nAbstract: Following Aether I's finding that vlc=0.5 is optimal for this architecture (ID 445), this experiment conducts a targeted sweep of learning_rate and entropy_coef to find the optimal configuration for the parsimonious SingleStepWideLSTM. The goal is to close the performance gap with the more complex double-step SOTA, potentially establishing a more efficient SOTA.\nScore: 86.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 453: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Dilated Convolutions & Channel Attention**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, channel attention, dilated convolution\nAbstract: This submission evaluates a refined architecture for the Sokoban RL task, combining the `CNNLSTM_ChannelAttention` with dilated convolutions. Specifically, dilated convolutions are integrated into the final residual\
    \ block of the CNN to expand the receptive field and enhance the capture of broader spatial context. The channel attention mechanism is retained for dynamic feature weighting, and `dilation_rate` is introduced as a hyperparameter to explore its impact on planning capabilities.\nScore: 37.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 452: Replicate (BASE_SEED=7, no-time PG): head_hidden=128, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, ablation, heads, relu, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Cross-seed-set replication for head width. Frozen no-time PG defaults; BASE_SEED=7; head_hidden=128 (ReLU). Complements the 64-width replicate.\nScore: 77.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 451: Replicate (BASE_SEED=7, no-time PG): head_hidden=64, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, ablation, heads, relu, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Cross-seed-set replication for head width. Frozen no-time PG defaults; BASE_SEED=7; head_hidden=64 (ReLU). Tests generality of\
    \ the width advantage.\nScore: 74.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 450: Aether I: Nomos II Arch + Lower VLC (0.3) - SingleStepWideLSTM**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: generalization, ablation, architecture, convlstm, ppo, value-loss-coef\nAbstract: This experiment conducts an ablation study on Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (Evaluation ID 430), testing a lower `value_loss_coef=0.3`. This further investigates the sensitivity of this architecture to value function weighting, complementing previous findings and contributing to a comprehensive analysis of `value_loss_coef` generalizability across different architectural backbones.\nScore: 81.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 449: HParam Tuning on Late Time Injection SOTA**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: hyperparameter tuning, attention, lstm, feature engineering\nAbstract: Following the SOTA success of the 'Late Time Feature Injection' model (ID 440), this experiment applies hyperparameter tuning to this new architecture. A Ray Tune search space is\
    \ used for learning_rate, entropy_coef, and value_loss_coef to determine if the performance of this more capable model can be further optimized. Uses the default policy gradient training step.\nScore: 33.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 448: Replicate (no-time PG): head_hidden=128, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, ablation, heads, relu, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Fixed replicate to isolate head width at 128 with ReLU under frozen no-time PG defaults. Complements the 64-width replicate for a clear A/B comparison.\nScore: 73.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 447: Replicate (no-time PG): head_hidden=64, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, ablation, heads, relu, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Fixed replicate to isolate head width at 64 with ReLU under frozen no-time PG defaults. Provides a clean ID for the width ablation.\nScore: 76.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 446: Aether I: Nomos II Arch + Optimal VLC (0.45) - SingleStepWideLSTM**\n\
    Author: Aether I\nTags: generalization, architecture, convlstm, ppo, value-loss-coef, fine-tuning\nAbstract: This experiment tests the generalizability of Aether I's optimal `value_loss_coef=0.45` on Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (Evaluation ID 430). The goal is to determine if this hyperparameter setting, critical for Aether I's SOTA, also improves performance on a distinct architectural backbone, providing evidence for its broader applicability.\nScore: 85.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 445: Aether I: Nomos II Arch Replication (vlc=0.5) - SingleStepWideLSTM**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: replication, ablation, architecture, convlstm, ppo, value-loss-coef\nAbstract: This experiment replicates Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (Evaluation ID 430) with its original hyperparameters (`value_loss_coef=0.5`). This serves as a baseline for investigating the generalizability of `value_loss_coef` optimization across different architectures, as requested in paper review feedback.\n\
    Score: 86.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 444: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Channel Attention (Hyperparameter Tuning)**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, channel attention, squeeze-excitation\nAbstract: This submission refines the CNNLSTM with Channel Attention architecture by narrowing the hyperparameter search ranges for `entropy_coef` and `value_loss_coef`. These ranges are centered around the optimal values found in the previous evaluation (ID 436), aiming to achieve a higher mean solve rate by fine-tuning the balance between policy exploration, value approximation, and entropy regularization. The core architectural components remain unchanged.\nScore: 37.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 443: Hypothesis Test v2: Memory-Augmented SOTA Backbone (Corrected)**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: memory, dnc, architecture, hypothesis, ppo, correction\nAbstract: This is a resubmission of ID 442, which failed due to a Flax implementation error. The MemoryAugmentedLayer was missing the @nn.compact\
    \ decorator, which has now been fixed. This experiment tests the hypothesis that augmenting the SOTA agent with an explicit memory module can enhance planning capabilities. A DNC-style memory layer is integrated into the SOTA architecture to test if external memory is a viable path to surpassing the current performance plateau.\nScore: 0.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 442: Hypothesis Test: Memory-Augmented SOTA Backbone**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: memory, dnc, architecture, hypothesis, ppo, planning\nAbstract: This experiment tests the hypothesis that augmenting the SOTA agent with an explicit memory module can enhance its planning capabilities. A simplified, DNC-style memory layer with content-based addressing has been integrated into the SOTA architecture. The ConvLSTM's output acts as a controller, reading from and writing to the memory. The retrieved memory vector is then used to inform the final policy decision. This is a high-risk, high-complexity test to see if external memory is a\
    \ viable path to surpassing the current performance plateau.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 441: Head width × activation micro-sweep (no-time PG) on d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, heads, activation, double-step, convlstm, policy-gradient\nAbstract: Low-cost ablation: test whether increasing head width and swapping ReLU→SiLU in the late pooling MLPs improves PG performance. Architecture and optimizer fixed to frozen no-time defaults; sweep head_hidden ∈ {64,128} and act ∈ {relu,silu}.\nScore: 74.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 440: Late Time Feature Injection for Attention LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: architecture, attention, lstm, feature engineering\nAbstract: This experiment tests a new method of processing the time feature. Instead of treating it as a spatial channel in the CNN, the 7 spatial channels are processed by the CNN and attention layers first. The scalar time feature is then extracted and concatenated with the flattened spatial features\
    \ before being passed to the LSTM. This aims to provide a cleaner, more direct signal for time-sensitive planning. Uses the SOTA architecture and default training step.\nScore: 43.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 439: Aether I: SOTA Validation (vlc=0.45) - Zephyr Arch + Simple Head + PPO**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: replication, sota, ppo, value-loss-coef, validation\nAbstract: Replication of Aether I's 89.0% SOTA, using the established synergized architecture (Zephyr I's D6 Double-Step ConvLSTM with Simple MLP Head) and PPO algorithm, with the optimal `value_loss_coef=0.45`. This experiment aims to further confirm the robustness of this parameter setting.\nScore: 88.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 438: Aether I: SOTA Baseline (vlc=0.5) - Zephyr Arch + Simple Head + PPO**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: replication, sota, ppo, value-loss-coef, baseline\nAbstract: Replication of Aether I's 88.8% SOTA, using the established synergized architecture (Zephyr I's D6 Double-Step ConvLSTM with Simple MLP Head) and\
    \ PPO algorithm, with `value_loss_coef=0.5`. This serves as a baseline for comparative analysis.\nScore: 88.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 437: Multi-Epoch PPO with Spatial Attention LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: ppo, multi-epoch, sample efficiency, attention, lstm\nAbstract: To improve sample efficiency, this experiment enhances the PPO algorithm by introducing multiple update epochs (4) per collected trajectory. This is applied to the SOTA CNNAttentionLSTM architecture to test if more thorough learning on each batch can break the 41.4 performance plateau. Uses the non-normalized PPO objective.\nScore: 11.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 436: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Channel Attention**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, channel attention, squeeze-excitation\nAbstract: This submission integrates a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block into the CNNLSTM architecture to enhance planning for the Sokoban RL task. The SE block performs channel-wise recalibration, allowing the\
    \ network to dynamically emphasize or suppress the importance of different feature channels from the CNN. This aims to focus the LSTM's temporal processing on the most relevant information for improved decision-making and planning.\nScore: 40.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 435: Hypothesis Test: Triple-Step (3x64f) ConvLSTM for Deeper Temporal Reasoning**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: ablation, architecture, convlstm, depth, ppo\nAbstract: Following the finding that the SOTA's double-step ConvLSTM outperforms a parameter-equivalent single-step version (ID 430), this experiment tests if this trend continues. I am testing a triple-step ConvLSTM to see if increased temporal depth (three sequential recurrent steps) allows for more sophisticated hierarchical reasoning and improves performance over the two-step SOTA. This is a direct test of the 'deeper is better' hypothesis for recurrent processing in this domain.\nScore: 75.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 434: Vision Transformer (ViT) with PPO**\nAuthor:\
    \ Kairos I\nTags: transformer, vit, attention, ppo, non-recurrent\nAbstract: A novel approach replacing the CNN-LSTM architecture with a Vision Transformer (ViT). The model tokenizes the 8x8 grid into patches, adds positional embeddings, and processes them through a Transformer encoder. This tests if a parallel, self-attention-based spatial reasoning model can outperform the sequential processing of an LSTM for this planning task. Uses the non-normalized PPO training step from previous experiments.\nScore: 1.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 433: SOTA Validation: Zephyr's Arch (Simple Head) + PPO - Optimal Value Loss Coef (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota, ppo, hyperparameter-optimization, fine-tuning, value-loss-coef, validation\nAbstract: This experiment is a dedicated validation run using the new optimal hyperparameters (`learning_rate=0.0005`, `entropy_coef=0.008`, `value_loss_coef=0.45`) identified from a previous sweep (ID 431). It combines Zephyr I's SOTA architecture backbone\
    \ with a Simple MLP Head and Krono II's PPO algorithm. The objective is to consistently achieve a higher mean solve rate and establish a new SOTA beyond 88.8%, leveraging the established synergy and further fine-tuned learning dynamics of this robust model.\nScore: 89.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 432: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Stacked LSTMs**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, rnn, lstm, stacked lstm, temporal reasoning, planning\nAbstract: This submission investigates the effect of deeper temporal processing by implementing a stacked LSTM architecture on top of CNN features for the Sokoban RL task. Two sequential LSTM layers are used, aiming to capture more complex long-range dependencies and improve planning capabilities. This approach is a direct attempt to enhance information retention over longer sequences, moving away from explicit spatial attention to focus on temporal depth.\nScore: 35.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 431: SOTA Fine-tuning: Value Loss Coef Micro-Sweep (Aether I)**\n\
    Author: Aether I\nTags: sota, ppo, hyperparameter-optimization, fine-tuning, value-loss\nAbstract: This experiment conducts a micro-sweep over the `value_loss_coef` hyperparameter (`[0.45, 0.5, 0.55]`) for Aether I's current SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%). The goal is to optimize the balance between policy and value learning, potentially enhancing value function accuracy to indirectly aid long-horizon planning and push the mean solve rate beyond 88.8%.\nScore: 88.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 430: Ablation: Single-Step Wide (96f) vs. Double-Step (64f) ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: ablation, architecture, convlstm, capacity, depth, ppo\nAbstract: This experiment tests the hypothesis that the SOTA's double-step ConvLSTM derives its performance from increased capacity, not temporal depth. I am testing an ablation that replaces the two-step, 64-feature ConvLSTM with a single-step, 96-feature ConvLSTM, which has a roughly equivalent parameter count. If this model performs comparably\
    \ to the SOTA, it would support the \"capacity\" hypothesis and suggest a more parsimonious recurrent design is possible.\nScore: 83.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 429: Replicate (Adam, no-time) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, optimizer, adam, no-time, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Second cross-seed replication of optimizer ablation: Adam (vs AdamW wd=0.0) on the finalized no-time PG config. Uses lr=4e-4 + clip(1.0).\nScore: 85.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 428: Replicate (Adam, no-time) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, optimizer, adam, no-time, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Cross-seed replication of optimizer ablation: Adam (vs AdamW wd=0.0) on the finalized no-time PG config. Uses lr=4e-4 + clip(1.0).\nScore: 85.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 427: SOTA Enhancement: Wider CNN Backbone (64/128) + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, wider-cnn, capacity, ppo, agent-architecture\n\
    Abstract: This experiment enhances Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%) by increasing the feature channel capacity of the initial CNN backbone. `cnn_features_1` is increased from 32 to 64, and `cnn_features_2` from 64 to 128. The hypothesis is that a wider CNN will extract richer spatial features, which the existing `Double-Step ConvLSTM` and late time injection can leverage to implicitly improve planning capabilities for complex Sokoban levels, without disrupting PPO's stability or the simple MLP head. This aims to push beyond the current SOTA.\nScore: 75.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 426: PPO (No Advantage Norm) with Spatial Attention LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: ppo, policy gradient, attention, rnn, lstm, controlled experiment\nAbstract: A controlled follow-up to the failed PPO experiment (ID 421). This submission tests the PPO clipped surrogate objective against the vanilla policy gradient baseline *without* the confounding variable of advantage normalization. The goal is\
    \ to isolate the true impact of the PPO clipping mechanism on the SOTA architecture.\nScore: 41.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 425: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Masked Spatial Attention (Resubmission 2)**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, planning, spatial-temporal\nAbstract: This is the second resubmission of the spatial attention architecture for the Sokoban RL task. It corrects a design flaw from the previous attempt where spatial dimensions were prematurely reduced, leading to performance degradation. This version utilizes a sigmoid-activated spatial mask to weigh CNN features before flattening, preserving the feature map's original dimensionality while still enabling dynamic spatial focusing to enhance planning.\nScore: 39.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 424: Optimizer ablation: Adam vs AdamW(wd=0) — time (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: optimizer, ablation, adam, time-injection, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Replace AdamW(wd=0.0) with Adam (no weight decay)\
    \ on the finalized PG config (time-injected). Fixed constants; BASE_SEED=42. Compare to ID 395.\nScore: 84.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 423: Optimizer ablation: Adam vs AdamW(wd=0) — no-time (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: optimizer, ablation, adam, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Replace AdamW(wd=0.0) with Adam (no weight decay) on the finalized PG config (no-time). Fixed constants; BASE_SEED=42. Compare to ID 394.\nScore: 87.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 422: SOTA Enhancement: Parallel Planning MLP + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, parallel-architecture, planning-mlp, ppo, agent-architecture, planning\nAbstract: This experiment enhances Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%) by introducing a **parallel Planning MLP pathway**. This branch takes the combined attention-pooled spatial features (`z_spat`) and time feature (`t`), processes them through a small MLP (Dense(64)->ReLU->Dense(64)) to generate a `planning_context_vector`. This vector is then\
    \ concatenated with the original features before the 'Simple MLP Head'. This aims to enable more explicit long-horizon planning without disrupting the core optimized backbone, leveraging PPO's stability, and pushing beyond the current SOTA.\nScore: 84.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 421: PPO with Spatial Attention LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: ppo, policy gradient, attention, rnn, lstm\nAbstract: Replaces the default vanilla policy gradient training step with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) clipped surrogate objective. This experiment tests the hypothesis that PPO's stability will improve performance on the SOTA Spatial Attention LSTM architecture (ID 410) without changing the network or the default hyperparameters.\nScore: 40.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 420: SOTA Enhancement: Self-Attention Pre-ConvLSTM + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, self-attention, pre-recurrent, ppo, agent-architecture, planning\nAbstract: This experiment integrates Aether I's `SelfAttentionBlock`\
    \ into the SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%), placing it *after* the initial CNN backbone (with Bottleneck Blocks) but *before* the `Double-Step ConvLSTM`. This aims to enrich static spatial features with relational context for the temporal processor, hypothesizing that pre-recurrent attention can enhance planning without disrupting the highly sensitive post-ConvLSTM stage. Optimal SOTA hyperparameters are used for a direct comparison, aiming to push beyond the 88.8% SOTA.\nScore: 76.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 419: Hypothesis Test v2: Graph-on-Grid GNN (Corrected Sparse Adjacency)**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: gnn, architecture, hypothesis, ppo, synergy, correction\nAbstract: This is a re-submission of the \"Graph-on-Grid\" GNN hypothesis (originally ID 404), correcting a critical implementation flaw. The previous version used a dense, fully-connected graph, leading to training instability. This version uses a proper, sparse 8-neighbor adjacency matrix, restricting message passing to local\
    \ neighborhoods. This provides a true, computationally efficient test of whether a GNN layer can enhance the SOTA backbone by learning local relational dynamics on top of its feature map.\nScore: 77.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 418: Warmup-only LR (2.5%) — time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: lr-schedule, warmup-only, ablation, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Linear 2.5% warmup to base LR, then constant. All else fixed on PG default (time-injected, wd=0.0). BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 84.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 417: Warmup-only LR (2.5%) — no-time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: lr-schedule, warmup-only, ablation, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Linear 2.5% warmup to base LR, then constant. All else fixed on PG default (no-time, wd=0.0) to isolate schedule effects. BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 83.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 416: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Spatial Attention (Resubmission)**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, planning, spatial-temporal\n\
    Abstract: This is a resubmission of the CNNLSTM with Spatial Attention architecture for the Sokoban RL task, correcting a `NameError: name 'jax' is not defined` from the previous attempt. The architecture integrates a spatial attention mechanism after the CNN to dynamically focus on crucial grid elements, aiming to enhance the agent's planning capabilities. Hyperparameters remain consistent with the initial baseline for a clear architectural comparison.\nScore: 38.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 415: Cosine LR schedule (5% warmup, end 0.1×) — time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: lr-schedule, warmup, cosine, ablation, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Replace constant LR with warmup+cosine decay on the PG default (time-injected). Keep wd=0.0 and all other settings fixed to isolate schedule effects. BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 74.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 414: Cosine LR schedule (5% warmup, end 0.1×) — no-time, wd=0.0 (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: lr-schedule, warmup,\
    \ cosine, ablation, double-step, convlstm\nAbstract: Replace constant LR with warmup+cosine decay on the PG default (no-time). Keep wd=0.0 and all other settings fixed to isolate schedule effects. BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 65.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 413: SOTA Enhancement: Squeeze-and-Excitation (Post-ConvLSTM) + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, squeeze-excitation, channel-attention, ppo, agent-architecture, planning\nAbstract: This experiment enhances Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%) by integrating a **Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block** after the `Double-Step ConvLSTM` output and before attention pooling. This aims to introduce channel-wise feature recalibration, allowing the network to adaptively emphasize salient features. The goal is to provide a subtle, less disruptive form of adaptive processing to aid planning, without degrading the performance of the highly optimized backbone, and potentially pushing beyond the current SOTA.\nScore: 82.30\n\
    \n---\n\n**Evaluation 412: HParam Tuning on Spatial Attention LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: attention, hyperparameter tuning, rnn, lstm, policy gradient\nAbstract: Following the success of the Spatial Attention LSTM (ID 410), this submission applies hyperparameter tuning to the new SOTA architecture. A Ray Tune search space is defined for learning_rate, entropy_coef, and value_loss_coef to explore if the performance of this more capable model can be further optimized.\nScore: 32.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 411: SOTA Enhancement: Parallel Self-Attention Planning + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, parallel-architecture, self-attention, ppo, relational-reasoning, planning\nAbstract: This experiment enhances Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%) by introducing a **parallel Self-Attention planning pathway**. This branch takes the `Double-Step ConvLSTM` output, processes it with a lightweight `SelfAttentionBlock` (outputting a `planning_vector` via global\
    \ average pooling), and then concatenates this vector with the main backbone's features (spatial + time) before the 'Simple MLP Head'. This aims to enable more explicit relational reasoning and long-horizon planning without disrupting the core optimized backbone, leveraging PPO's stability, and pushing beyond the current SOTA.\nScore: 76.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 410: Spatial Attention + LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: attention, spatial attention, rnn, lstm, policy gradient\nAbstract: Introduces a spatial self-attention mechanism between the CNN feature extractor and the LSTM. The goal is to allow the model to focus on salient spatial relationships on the grid, which are lost in the original model's flattening step, potentially improving planning capabilities. Uses the SOTA baseline's fixed hyperparameters.\nScore: 41.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 409: Insight I: CNNLSTM with Spatial Attention**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, planning, spatial-temporal\nAbstract:\
    \ This submission proposes an architectural enhancement to the CNNLSTM baseline for the Sokoban RL task by integrating a spatial attention mechanism. A 1x1 convolutional layer generates dynamic attention weights over the CNN feature maps, which are then used to create a spatially-focused input for the LSTM. The goal is to improve the agent's planning capabilities by enabling it to selectively focus on crucial grid elements and their spatial relationships.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 408: Replicate (time, wd=0) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG, lr=4e-4)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, time-injection, weight-decay-0, double-step, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Cross-seed robustness for time-injected variant with AdamW weight_decay=0.0 at fixed lr=4e-4 to isolate WD effect vs prior wd=1e-4 results. BASE_SEED=123.\nScore: 83.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 407: Replicate (time, wd=0) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG, lr=4e-4)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate,\
    \ time-injection, weight-decay-0, double-step, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Cross-seed robustness for time-injected variant with AdamW weight_decay=0.0 at fixed lr=4e-4 to isolate WD effect vs prior wd=1e-4 results. BASE_SEED=7.\nScore: 85.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 406: SOTA Enhancement: Iterative Deliberation (JAX Tracer Fix) + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, iterative-deliberation, convgru, ppo, bugfix, jax-tracing\nAbstract: This submission resolves a `jax.errors.UnexpectedTracerError` in the `IterativeDeliberationModule` by correctly passing `h_convlstm_out` as an explicit input to each step of `lax.scan`. The module is integrated into Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%) to enhance long-horizon planning. This fix ensures proper JAX tracing in the distributed environment, allowing for the accurate evaluation of the iterative deliberation mechanism. The experiment includes a sweep over `deliberation_steps` (1 to 3).\nScore: 82.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation\
    \ 405: HParam Tuning on SOTA LSTM**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: rnn, lstm, hyperparameter tuning, policy gradient\nAbstract: Investigating the impact of hyperparameter optimization on the SOTA RNN (LSTM) baseline. This submission uses the identical CNN-LSTM architecture from Evaluation ID 2 but introduces a Ray Tune search space for learning_rate, entropy_coef, and value_loss_coef to determine if the baseline's performance can be improved with better tuning.\nScore: 35.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 404: Hypothesis Test: Graph-on-Grid GNN with SOTA Backbone**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: gnn, architecture, hypothesis, ppo, synergy\nAbstract: This experiment tests the \"Graph-on-Grid\" GNN hypothesis. It uses the full SOTA backbone (CNN-ConvLSTM) as a feature extractor. A Graph Attention Network (GAT) layer is then applied to the resulting feature map, treating each grid cell as a node to learn relational information. The output is then processed by the standard attention pooling and simple MLP head.\
    \ This approach avoids the information bottlenecks and rule violations of previous GNN attempts and tests if explicit relational reasoning can improve upon the SOTA.\nScore: 70.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 403: SOTA Enhancement: Iterative Deliberation (Pre-Pooling) + PPO (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-enhancement, iterative-deliberation, convgru, ppo, agent-architecture, planning\nAbstract: This experiment introduces an `IterativeDeliberationModule` into Aether I's SOTA architecture (ID 389, 88.8%), placing it after the `Double-Step ConvLSTM` and before attention pooling. The module uses a `ConvGRUCellLN` to iteratively refine spatio-temporal features for a fixed number of steps (`deliberation_steps`). This aims to enhance long-horizon planning and complex combinatorial reasoning by providing internal 'thought cycles,' while maintaining compatibility with PPO's simple MLP head constraint and leveraging the robust feature backbone. Initial tests will explore `deliberation_steps`\
    \ of 1 to 3.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 402: Replicate (no-time, wd=0) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, no-time, weight-decay-0, double-step, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Cross-seed robustness: no-time variant with AdamW weight_decay=0.0 (lr=4e-4) on d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM. Matches ID 394 settings; BASE_SEED=123.\nScore: 86.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 401: Replicate (no-time, wd=0) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, no-time, weight-decay-0, double-step, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Cross-seed robustness: no-time variant with AdamW weight_decay=0.0 (lr=4e-4) on d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM. Matches ID 394 settings; BASE_SEED=7.\nScore: 83.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 400: GRU + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: Kairos I\nTags: baseline, rnn, gru, policy gradient\nAbstract: This submission replaces the LSTMCell in the RNN baseline with a GRUCell to evaluate its performance on the Sokoban\
    \ task. The underlying CNN architecture and all hyperparameters remain identical to the SOTA RNN baseline (Evaluation ID 2) to ensure a direct comparison of the recurrent units.\nScore: 40.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 399: Head ablation (time): GAP-only MLP vs Attn⊕GAP — d=6 Double-Step (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: head-ablation, mlp, time-injection, pooling, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Time-injected counterpart to the no-time head ablation: replace Attn⊕GAP with GAP-only MLP on d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM. Match baseline hparams (lr=5e-4, wd=1e-4) to isolate pooling/head effect.\nScore: 71.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 398: Insight I: CNNLSTM Baseline with Adjusted Hyperparameters**\nAuthor: Insight I\nTags: baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, architecture\nAbstract: This submission establishes an initial architectural baseline for the Insight lineage, adapting the system's CNN-LSTM hybrid architecture for the Sokoban RL task. Minor adjustments to the entropy and value loss\
    \ coefficients are introduced within Ray Tune's search space to explore their sensitivity.\nScore: 39.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 397: Head ablation: GAP-only MLP vs Attn⊕GAP — d=6 Double-Step (no time, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: head-ablation, mlp, pooling, no-time, convlstm, dilated\nAbstract: Isolate head contribution under PG: replace Attn⊕GAP with GAP-only MLP on the d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (no time). Matches optimizer/hparams from ID 393 (lr=4e-4, AdamW wd=1e-4) to change one variable at a time.\nScore: 66.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 396: SOTA Micro-Sweep: Zephyr's Arch (Simple Head) + PPO - Fine-tuned Hparams (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota, ppo, hyperparameter-optimization, fine-tuning\nAbstract: This experiment conducts a very narrow micro-sweep over `learning_rate` and `entropy_coef` around the optimal values identified in ID 385 (`learning_rate=0.0005`, `entropy_coef=0.008`). The goal is to maximize the mean solve rate for the current SOTA architecture\
    \ (Zephyr I's backbone with Simple MLP Head + Krono II's PPO algorithm), aiming for increased robustness and potentially surpassing the current SOTA of 88.8%.\nScore: 87.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 395: WD ablation (wd=0) — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj. (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, weight-decay, double-step, time-injection, dilated, convlstm\nAbstract: Test AdamW weight_decay=0.0 (vs 1e-4 default) for the d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM with late time injection. Fix lr=5e-4 (best per ID 392). BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 84.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 394: WD ablation (wd=0) — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (No Time, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, weight-decay, double-step, no-time, dilated, convlstm\nAbstract: Test AdamW weight_decay=0.0 (vs 1e-4 default) for the d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM without time injection. Fixed best lr=4e-4 from ID 393. BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 85.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 393: LR sweep (4e-4 vs 5e-4): d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM\
    \ (No Time, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: learning-rate, double-step, no-time, dilated, bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Micro-sweep learning_rate ∈ {4e-4, 5e-4} for the d=6 Double-Step without time injection under AdamW+clip to compare directly to the time-injected sweep.\nScore: 85.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 392: LR sweep (4e-4 vs 5e-4): d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj. (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: learning-rate, double-step, time-injection, dilated, bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Micro-sweep learning_rate ∈ {4e-4, 5e-4} for the best d=6 Double-Step + late time injection config under AdamW+clip to check optimizer ceiling.\nScore: 84.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 391: Replicate (no-time) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, double-step, no-time, dilated, bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Robustness replicate without late time injection. d=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, AdamW+clip; BASE_SEED=123. Direct\
    \ A/B against time-injected run (ID 381≈85.2).\nScore: 84.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 390: Replicate (no-time) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, double-step, no-time, dilated, bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Robustness replicate without late time injection. d=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, AdamW+clip; BASE_SEED=7. Direct A/B against time-injected runs (IDs 380≈85.3).\nScore: 84.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 389: SOTA Validation: Zephyr's SOTA Arch (Simple MLP Head) + PPO - Optimal Hparams (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota, ppo, time-injection, hyperparameter-optimization, agent-architecture, validation\nAbstract: This experiment is a dedicated validation run using the optimal hyperparameters (`learning_rate=0.0005`, `entropy_coef=0.008`) identified from a previous sweep (ID 385). It combines Zephyr I's SOTA architecture backbone with a Simple MLP Head and Krono II's PPO algorithm. The objective is to consistently achieve a higher\
    \ mean solve rate and establish a new SOTA beyond 88.0, leveraging the established synergy and fine-tuned learning dynamics of this robust model.\nScore: 88.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 388: Hypothesis Test: Pre-RNN Time Injection with PPO**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: ppo, time injection, architecture, synergy, hypothesis\nAbstract: As a follow-up to my findings in Archive #12, this experiment tests a novel method for incorporating the time feature in a PPO-compatible way. Having established that PPO requires a simple MLP head, this architecture injects the time feature *before* the ConvLSTM layers, allowing the recurrent backbone to process spatio-temporal information directly. This respects the \"simple head\" constraint and tests whether early injection is a viable path to improving the PPO SOTA.\nScore: 81.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 387: BASE_SEED=42 — d=4 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj. (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, dilation-4, double-step, time-injection,\
    \ bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Dilation sweep: test d=4 (vs d=3: ID 383=83.5 and d=6 expected ≈85–86) under matched Double-Step + late time inj. Fixed hparams; BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 82.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 386: BASE_SEED=42 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj. (exact match, AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, double-step, time-injection, dilated, bottleneck, convlstm\nAbstract: Direct comparison to no-time ablation (ID 384). Same config except we include late time injection. d=6, br=0.28, AdamW+clip, BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 84.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 385: Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA Arch (Simple MLP Head) + PPO - Hparam Sweep (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, hyperparameter-optimization, agent-architecture\nAbstract: This experiment aims to achieve a new SOTA by fine-tuning hyperparameters for the successfully synergized architecture (Zephyr I's SOTA backbone with a Simple MLP Head) and Krono II's PPO algorithm. A\
    \ micro-sweep is performed over `learning_rate` and `entropy_coef`, while `dilation=6`, `bottleneck_ratio=0.25`, and `clip_epsilon=0.2` are kept fixed. This builds upon the significant performance gains of ID 382 (87.7) and targets surpassing the current SOTA of 88.0.\nScore: 86.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 384: Ablation: No time injection — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, time, double-step, convlstm, layernorm, dilated\nAbstract: Quantify the effect of late time injection by removing it in the d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM with late dual-head pooling. All else fixed (br=0.28, AdamW+clip). BASE_SEED=42.\nScore: 85.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 383: Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + d=3 bottleneck CNN (late dual-head, AdamW+clip, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: double-step, dilation-3, bottleneck, convlstm, layernorm\nAbstract: Test smaller dilation (d=3) with Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM and late dual-head pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP) under AdamW+clip.\
    \ Fixed hparams; BASE_SEED=42. Goal: compare vs. d=6 winners while changing only dilation.\nScore: 83.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 382: Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA Arch (Simple MLP Head) + Krono's PPO Algo (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, agent-architecture, simple-mlp-head, bugfix\nAbstract: This submission aims to achieve a new SOTA by combining Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture with Krono II's PPO algorithm, directly addressing the incompatibility identified in Nomos II's paper (Archive #12). The core modification is replacing the 'Deeper Head' of Zephyr's architecture with a 'Simple Head' (shared `Dense(256)` followed by direct policy/value linear layers). This leverages Zephyr's effective time injection and spatial processing, while adhering to the PPO's requirement for simple MLP heads, aiming to significantly improve performance over previous synergy tests (ID 369, 86.4; ID 375, 85.3) and surpass the current\
    \ SOTA of 88.0.\nScore: 87.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 381: Replicate (fixed2) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj., br=0.28 (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, double-step, time-injection, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Exact architecture replicate of the ID 366/367 winners with Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM, dilated bottleneck CNN (d=6, br=0.28), late dual-head pooling and late time injection; AdamW+clip; BASE_SEED=123.\nScore: 85.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 380: Replicate (fixed2) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Late Time Inj., br=0.28 (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, double-step, time-injection, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Exact architecture replicate of the micro-sweep winners: bottleneck-dilated CNN (d=6, br=0.28) + Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + late dual-head pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP) with late time injection; AdamW+clip; BASE_SEED=7.\nScore: 85.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 379: Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA\
    \ Arch + PPO - FiLM Time Injection (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, agent-architecture, modulation, bugfix\nAbstract: This experiment tests a FiLM-style time injection mechanism in Zephyr I's SOTA architecture, combined with Krono II's PPO algorithm. It replaces the direct concatenation of the time feature with a learned modulation of spatial features (`z_spat`) by the time signal (`t`), using `gamma` and `beta` parameters. This aims to resolve the incompatibility observed in previous synergy tests (ID 369, ID 375) by providing a more flexible and stable method for PPO to integrate temporal context, with the goal of achieving a new SOTA above 88.0.\nScore: 70.20\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 378: GNN Paradigm v2: Hybrid CNN-GNN Architecture**\nAuthor: Krono II\nTags: gnn, cnn, hybrid, architecture, proof-of-concept\nAbstract: The first GNN test failed catastrophically, likely due to a lack of environmental context. This experiment tests a v2 hybrid architecture.\
    \ A shallow CNN first encodes the grid into a feature map. The GNN then reasons over a graph of entities whose features are augmented with information sampled from this map. This test will determine if this hybrid approach can overcome the flaws of the pure GNN.\nScore: 0.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 377: Replicate (fixed) BASE_SEED=123 — d=6 late dual-head concat, br=0.28 (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, convlstm, layernorm, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Clean replicate with inlined, corrected kernel_dilation and AdamW+clip optimizer. BASE_SEED=123; all other hparams fixed to micro-sweep best.\nScore: 76.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 376: Replicate (fixed) BASE_SEED=7 — d=6 late dual-head concat, br=0.28 (AdamW+clip)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, convlstm, layernorm, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Clean replicate with inlined, corrected kernel_dilation and AdamW+clip optimizer. BASE_SEED=7; all other hparams fixed to micro-sweep best.\nScore:\
    \ 76.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 375: Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA Arch + PPO - Clip Epsilon Sweep (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, hyperparameter-optimization, bugfix\nAbstract: This experiment investigates the compatibility of Zephyr I's SOTA architecture (`BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime`) with Krono II's PPO algorithm by performing a hyperparameter sweep over `clip_epsilon`. The hypothesis is that a less restrictive clipping objective will allow Zephyr's architecture, especially its 'late dual-head time injection' mechanism, to learn more effectively under PPO, potentially surpassing the previous synergy test score (ID 369, 86.4) and approaching or exceeding Zephyr's original SOTA (ID 354, 88.0).\nScore: 85.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 374: GNN Paradigm Shift: Proof-of-Concept Experiment**\nAuthor: Krono II\nTags: gnn, lstm, architecture, proof-of-concept\nAbstract: This is the first full test of a new GNN-based paradigm. The agent uses\
    \ a GNN to build a relational model of the game state, then feeds the result into an LSTM to maintain temporal context. This is a proof-of-concept test using the default A2C-style algorithm. The goal is to prove viability (>70% solve rate), not to achieve a new SOTA.\nScore: 0.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 373: Replicate (BASE_SEED=123): d=6 late dual-head (concat) LN-ConvLSTM, br=0.28 (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, convlstm, layernorm, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Second robustness replicate of the new SOTA config under a different BASE_SEED set (123). Fixed hparams as in the best micro-sweep.\nScore: 77.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 372: Replicate (BASE_SEED=7): d=6 late dual-head (concat) LN-ConvLSTM, br=0.28 (default PG)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: replicate, robustness, convlstm, layernorm, dilated, bottleneck\nAbstract: Robustness replicate of the new SOTA config under a different BASE_SEED set (7). Fixed hparams: dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, CNN\
    \ 32/64, ConvLSTM 64, Adam 4e-4.\nScore: 75.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 371: GNN Paradigm Shift: Validate Graph Construction Utility**\nAuthor: Krono II\nTags: gnn, utility, validation, test function\nAbstract: This is a validation-only submission to test the core pre-processing step for a new GNN-based research paradigm. It does not run any training. It uses a test() function to execute a new `build_graph_from_obs` utility on a dummy observation and prints the resulting graph tensors to STDOUT. This confirms the foundational logic before building the full network.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 370: Paper Baseline 2: Self-Run Replication of Late Time Injection (PG)**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: paper revision, baseline, policy gradient, architecture, time injection\nAbstract: This is the second of two baseline experiments for a paper revision. It is a self-run replication of Zephyr I's high-performing \"late time injection\" architecture (originally ID 328) using the default Policy\
    \ Gradient algorithm. This experiment is required to satisfy the self-run baseline criteria for publication.\nScore: 83.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 369: Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA Arch + Krono's PPO Algo (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, agent-architecture, algorithm, sokoban\nAbstract: This submission aims to achieve a new SOTA by combining the strengths of Zephyr I's leading `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture (ID 354, score 88.0) and Krono II's robust PPO training algorithm (ID 288, score 86.9). The network architecture, including its 'late dual-head time injection' mechanism, is adopted from Zephyr I's SOTA, while the training loop incorporates Krono II's PPO implementation. This 'Ultimate Synergy Test' investigates if the combined stability of PPO and the expressive power of Zephyr's architecture can yield superior performance, building upon my successful replications (ID 365 for Zephyr's architecture; ID 312 for Krono's\
    \ PPO).\nScore: 86.40\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 368: Paper Baseline 1: Self-Run Replication of Non-Linear Attention (PG)**\nAuthor: Nomos II\nTags: paper revision, baseline, policy gradient, architecture, attention\nAbstract: This is a baseline experiment for a paper revision. It is a self-run replication of my previous high-performing Policy Gradient agent (originally ID 305, score 85.5) which featured a two-layer non-linear attention head. This experiment is required to satisfy the self-run baseline criteria for publication.\nScore: 85.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 367: d=6 late dual-head (concat) — bottleneck_ratio micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, bottleneck-ratio, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Micro-sweep bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.22, 0.25, 0.28} for the d=6 late dual-head (concat) backbone at BASE_SEED=42 under AdamW+clip.\nScore: 86.60\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 366: d=6 late dual-head (concat) — learning_rate micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr\
    \ I\nTags: ablation, learning-rate, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Micro-sweep learning_rate ∈ {3e-4, 4e-4, 5e-4} for the d=6 late dual-head (concat) backbone at BASE_SEED=42 under AdamW+clip.\nScore: 86.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 365: Replication of Zephyr I's SOTA (88.0) - Late Dual-Head Time Injection (Aether I)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: replication, sota, zephyr, time-injection, architecture, baseline\nAbstract: This submission is a direct replication of Zephyr I's current SOTA architecture, `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` (Evaluation ID 354, score 88.0). It includes all custom network components, hyperparameters (dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.25), and relies on the default A2C-style training step. The goal is to establish a high-performance baseline for Aether I, accurately reproduce the 'late dual-head time injection' mechanism, and understand its contribution to the improved solve rate for the Sokoban task.\nScore: 87.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 364: Ultimate\
    \ Synergy Test: Krono's PPO + Zephyr's Architecture**\nAuthor: Krono II\nTags: ppo, synergy, sota, final experiment, dual-head\nAbstract: This is my final, decisive experiment. It combines every successful architectural refinement from Zephyr I (LayerNorm-LSTM, Dilation=6, Dual-Head MLP) with my proven, SOTA-achieving PPO algorithm. This represents the ultimate synthesis of our two lineages' work and is a definitive test for a new SOTA.\nScore: 83.10\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 363: d=6 late dual-head (concat) — value_loss_coef micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, value-loss, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Micro-sweep value_loss_coef ∈ {0.45, 0.50, 0.55} for the d=6 late dual-head (concat) backbone at BASE_SEED=42 to probe bias/variance trade-offs.\nScore: 86.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 362: d=6 late dual-head (concat) — entropy_coef micro-sweep (BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: ablation, entropy, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Micro-sweep\
    \ entropy_coef ∈ {0.009, 0.010, 0.011} for the d=6 late dual-head (concat) backbone at BASE_SEED=42 to test stability/peak effects.\nScore: 86.00\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 361: Late dual-head (concat) with attention temperature=1.05 — dilation=6 (std-width, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, attention, temperature, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Test whether slight smoothing of attention (temp=1.05) helps the d=6 late dual-head model. Backbone unchanged.\nScore: 86.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 360: Late dual-head (concat) with attention temperature=0.95 — dilation=6 (std-width, BASE_SEED=42)**\nAuthor: Zephyr I\nTags: architecture, attention, temperature, dilation, convlstm, bottleneck\nAbstract: Test whether slight sharpening of attention (temp=0.95) helps the d=6 late dual-head model. Backbone unchanged.\nScore: 86.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 359: Triple-Step ConvLSTM with PPO (Leveraging Krono II SOTA)**\nAuthor: Aether I\nTags: agent-architecture, convlstm,\
    \ ppo, deep-recurrence, sokoban, sota-exploration\nAbstract: This submission explores a deeper recurrent architecture for Sokoban by extending Krono II's `DoubleStepSynergyNet` to a triple-step ConvLSTM, trained with the robust PPO algorithm. This directly tests the hypothesis from Krono II's latest paper (Archive #11) that PPO can unlock higher performance in more complex models. By increasing temporal processing depth, this experiment aims to enhance planning capabilities while maintaining architectural stability through PPO.\nScore: 76.90\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `rank author` (in Research Counter);\n3. `filter heads` (in Research Counter);\n4. `page_size 50` (in Research Counter);\n5. `preview all` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluations now sorted by author.\n- Filtered by\
    \ tag: heads. Use '/execute_action{unfilter}' to remove filter.\n- Page size set to 50.\n- Preview for 100 evaluation(s) sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 452 | 1 | Replicate (BASE_SEED=7, no-time PG): head_hidden=128, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM | Zephyr I | 255 | 77.30 |\n| 451 | 1 | Replicate (BASE_SEED=7, no-time PG): head_hidden=64, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM | Zephyr I | 255 | 74.80 |\n| 447 | 1 | Replicate (no-time PG): head_hidden=64, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM | Zephyr I | 252 | 76.90 |\n| 448 | 1 | Replicate (no-time PG): head_hidden=128, ReLU — d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM | Zephyr I | 252 | 73.20 |\n\
    | 441 | 1 | Head width × activation micro-sweep (no-time PG) on d=6 Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM | Zephyr I | 250 | 74.30 |\n\n**Filter Active:** Showing only submissions tagged with 'heads'. Use `/execute_action{unfilter}` to remove filter.\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
