tick: 265
role: model
parts:
- text: "Option 1 — Analytical paper (simple heads + PPO vs deeper heads + PG)\n- Value: Very high. We already have rich, ID-backed evidence across families:\n  - PG: deeper/dual-heads + late time injection work (IDs 360–367, 384–395; 354=88.0).\n  - PPO: simple MLP head wins (389=88.8, 433=89.0; 438/439 validate vlc sensitivity).\n  - GAP-only drops (397=66.0, 399=71.8) quantify the necessity of attention pooling under PG.\n- Cost: Low. Synthesis, figures from existing IDs, no new training.\n- Risk: Low. Claims can be fully ID-anchored.\n- Hypothesis to argue:\n  - PPO’s clipped objective + critic coupling prefers lower head curvature/shallower heads; deeper heads amplify credit variance and destabilize PPO’s multi-batch updates.\n  - PG (single-pass, more on-policy) tolerates/deploys richer heads to extract stronger spatial credit assignment when compute budget is tight.\n- Verdict: GO. This is the highest marginal value item.\n\nOption 2 — test()-only qualitative probes (no training)\n\
    - Targets:\n  - Head/shallow vs deep curvature proxies: gradient norm amplification and spectral norms at init.\n  - Attention pooling selectivity proxy: attention entropy on synthetic batches (distributional proxy).\n  - Gate stats proxy: LN-ConvLSTM gate pre-/post-activation moments at init.\n- Feasibility: High without env if we use synthetic inputs shaped (B,8,8,8). Correlations will be qualitative but still informative when juxtaposed with our ID outcomes.\n- Cost/Risk: Very low compute; fully reproducible; adds mechanistic color to Option 1 without new training.\n- Verdict: GO. Use to support the paper with mechanistic proxies.\n\nOption 3 — PG optimizer micro-ablation (Adam betas/eps)\n- Expected gain: Marginal (<0.3–0.5), easily swamped by seed variance.\n- Cost: Non-trivial (full 4-seed, 30-min/seed evals).\n- Strategic value: Low; unlikely to change conclusions or SOTA.\n- Verdict: NO-GO.\n\nDecision and minimal action plan (parsimonious and verifiable)\n- A1. Draft “Why Simple\
    \ Heads Pair with PPO but Deeper Heads Suit PG” as an Archive addendum:\n  - Sections: Setup; Evidence table (IDs and scores, PG vs PPO, head variants); Mechanistic hypotheses; Proxy measurements; Practical guidance.\n  - All numeric claims tied to IDs (no exceptions).\n- A2. Implement a single test() script (no training) that:\n  - Loads 3–4 architectures we already used (IDs representative: PG deep head, PG GAP-only, PPO simple head).\n  - Computes on synthetic batch (e.g., B=64, N=64 tokens):\n    - Attention entropy (mean, std).\n    - Spectral norms of Dense layers in heads; per-layer gradient norm amplification from a dummy loss.\n    - LN-ConvLSTM gate pre-activation mean/std; fraction near saturation (|tanh|>0.9; σ gates near {0,1}).\n  - Prints a compact JSON summary to STDOUT for reproducibility.\n- A3. Insert the proxy results (raw JSON blobs) into Private Memory, then cite them in the paper with the script path and commit hash (storage path) for verifiability.\n- A4. No new\
    \ training runs; keep compute at zero beyond test().\n\nEnd state: One rigorous, ID-grounded analytical paper plus lightweight mechanistic proxies that explain the observed algorithm–architecture compatibility, all with minimal tokens and no new experiments."
thinking_content: "**Assessing evaluation options**\n\nI'm realizing that each submission triggers four seeds with 30 minutes of training, and it's pretty costly. A micro-sweep could help, but it won't significantly reduce the costs. My likely plan could be a combination of Option 1 and Option 2 to create an analytical paper with proxies. Option 3 seems off the table unless there's a strong theoretical justification. We’ll need to establish our structure over five reflection ticks, refining our analysis each time. In Tick 1, I’ll focus on outlining the evaluation criteria and initial hypothesis along with pros and cons.**Structuring analytical steps**\n\nFor the next ticks, Tick 2 will focus on outlining the structure of my analytical paper and mapping IDs to claims. In Tick 3, I’ll design test probes including instrument code and metrics to examine the expected relationships across variants. I need to consider potential code access issues, as I can only read certain files, yet I can replicate\
  \ proxies from our storage. Tick 4 revolves around writing code for computing proxies for our SOTA and variants, generating Private Memory logs, and updating Archive #10 with additional analytics. Lastly, in Tick 5, I'll finalize my go/no-go decisions on options presented.**Reflecting on evaluation options**\n\nI need to ensure I’m writing reflection content rather than action items. The earlier \"Automatic System Tips\" mentioned including identity and recaps, but for these reflections, that’s not strictly necessary. I’ll keep it concise yet detailed.\n\nNow, for Tick 1, I’m evaluating options: \n\nOption 1 involves creating an analytical paper that could provide high value and utilize existing results at minimal cost, linking it to verifiability through evaluation IDs. It’ll explain why a simple PPO head may perform better due to factors like RNN state interaction and reduced overparameterization.\n\nOption 2 focuses on test proxies, using attention entropy measured across small offline\
  \ episodes, but we haven't saved any recorded observations for that yet.**Exploring synthetic inputs**\n\nI'm considering generating synthetic \"structured\" inputs, like one-hot random placements of boxes. However, I realize that using domain-specific heuristics is prohibited, particularly for Sokoban. Still, proxies for analysis using one-hot grid shapes recognized by the network might not fall under that restriction. \n\nTo keep it simple, I could focus on static snapshot analysis with random noise. But I feel that won’t provide much insight. A better method might be to use test() to log network structural metrics, like gating bias distribution. I'm curious about how forget gate biases behave and LayerNorm gating affects saturation.\n\nI think gradient norms from a random uniform noise batch could help, but the correlation to solve rates remains uncertain. While the test() can't run an environment, maybe I could import the environment generator from storage/system, which includes the\
  \ necessary code.**Planning the import process**\n\nI’m looking into the option of importing Python modules from system storage, and it seems there are likely environment wrappers available. Previously, \"storage/system/main.py\" was used for training, so I can try importing to create a single step. However, the test() environment won't have GPU support, but running on CPU with a few episodes should be manageable. \n\nI’ll try importing 'storage/system/envs/sokoban.py,' but since I’m unsure of the path, I could check \"system/train_single.py\" for the correct import location. A minimal test can sample random observations along with basic statistics, while ensuring criteria for verifiability by publishing code to compute metrics tied to evaluation IDs.\n\nInstead of relying on the environment, I can produce metrics on parameter counts, spectral norms of linear layers, and gradient-to-parameter ratios. These metrics would indicate deeper heads' higher gradient amplification, possibly explaining\
  \ PPO brittleness. \n\nSo, I think I'm ready to summarize my choices for Tick 1, planning to go with Options 1 and 2 while deciding against Option 3."
