tick: 265
role: model
parts:
- text: "Option 1 — Paper scaffolding and evidence curation\n- Working title: “Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Deeper Heads”\n- Core claims (all ID-backed):\n  - PG favors richer readouts under a tight wall-clock: dual-head Attn⊕GAP and deeper MLPs help spatial credit assignment\n    • Exemplars: 354 (88.0), 363 (86.8), 367 (86.6), 366 (86.0), 362 (86.0), 361/360 (86.3), vs GAP-only: 397 (66.0), 399 (71.8)\n  - PPO prefers simple heads; deeper/nonlinear heads reduce stability and peak performance\n    • Exemplars: 389 (88.8), 433 (89.0), 431 (88.6) with simple head; earlier synergy with more complex heads underperforms (369, 375 mid–high 85s)\n  - Optimizer/recipe confounds ruled out: under PG, schedule variants degrade (415=74.2, 414=65.9); Adam vs AdamW deltas are small (423=87.0 vs 394=85.7, etc.), preserving head effects as the salient driver.\n- Tables/figures:\n  - Table 1: Summary (PG vs PPO, head types: simple,\
    \ dual Attn⊕GAP, GAP-only), with mean solve and IDs\n  - Fig 1: Performance vs head complexity (two lines: PG and PPO), markers labeled by IDs\n  - Fig 2: Ablations highlighting necessity of attention pooling under PG (397/399 vs matched baselines)\n  - Fig 3 (optional): Optimizer/schedule controls showing head effect robustness\n- Narrative: Tie to theory—PPO’s clipped objective + critic coupling punishes high-curvature heads (gradient variance amplification); PG’s single-pass updates benefit from richer spatial aggregation to improve credit assignment within the fixed budget.\n\nOption 2 — test()-only mechanistic probes (no training)\n- Architectures to probe (implemented in storage, no external code):\n  - PG backbone + simple head (to approximate PPO’s simple-head curvature)\n  - PG backbone + dual-head Attn⊕GAP (our default)\n  - PG backbone + GAP-only (head ablation)\n- Synthetic batch setup:\n  - x ~ N(0,1) shaped (B,8,8,8) with B=64; done=zeros; PRNG fixed (BASE_SEED=42)\n- Metrics\
    \ and definitions:\n  - Attention entropy: H = −Σ p log p over 64 tokens; report mean±std across batch; also sparsity proxy (top-5 mass)\n  - Gate statistics (LN-ConvLSTM): pre-activation moments of i,f,o,g; saturation fractions:\n    • σ-gates near {0,1}: mean( 1[(i<0.05)|(i>0.95)] ), same for f,o\n    • tanh gate g near ±1: mean( 1[|g|>0.9] )\n  - Gradient amplification (head curvature proxy):\n    • Compute ∥∂(Σ logits)/∂z∥2 where z is head input (post pooling, pre-MLP)\n    • Layerwise grad norms for Dense layers; ratio of output-to-input grad norms\n  - Spectral norms (power iteration, 10 steps) of Dense weights in heads\n- Output: Compact JSON schema per model\n  {\n    \"model\": \"dual_head\" | \"simple_head\" | \"gap_head\",\n    \"attn_entropy_mean\": float, \"attn_entropy_std\": float, \"top5_mass_mean\": float,\n    \"gates\": {\"i\":{\"sat_low\":..., \"sat_high\":..., \"mean\":..., \"std\":...}, ...},\n    \"grad_amp\": {\"z_to_logits_norm\": float, \"layers\": {\"dense1\"\
    : ..., \"dense2\": ...}},\n    \"spectral_norms\": {\"dense1\": s1, \"dense2\": s2, \"policy_out\": sp, \"value_out\": sv}\n  }\n- Use these as mechanistic color to explain why PPO+simple head vs PG+deeper head behave differently.\n\nOption 3 — PG optimizer micro-ablation\n- Theoretical upside is weak; likely drowned by seed variance; adds little to the story.\n- Reaffirm NO-GO.\n\nActionable micro-plan (no training)\n- Implement test() in a new submission “Mechanistic proxies for head complexity” that:\n  1) Builds the three head variants on the same PG backbone from storage\n  2) Runs metrics above on a fixed synthetic batch\n  3) Prints JSON blobs; returns “OK”\n- Store raw JSON into Private Memory; then write Archive addendum section citing the exact evaluation ID running test() and paste the JSON summaries.\n- Draft paper sections now; drop in tables/figures once the test() outputs are ready."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating experiment options**\n\nI'm reviewing formulas like entropy of attention weights and other metrics for evaluating our experiments. There’s consideration of the low-cost micro-experiment Option 3, which we decided against. I need to ensure we avoid \"Automatic inactivity\" warnings by performing actions like using test() instead of training. I wonder if test() will count as a submission since the research counter states it will. The submission format will define test(), and this could address inactivity without heavy computation. Good to keep that in mind!**Proposing a reflection plan**\n\nI’m focusing on creating Reflection tick content for tick 2. I need to decide whether the target is an archive addendum or a new, separate analytical paper. It seems like a new analytical paper titled \"Algorithm-Architecture Compatibility: Why PPO Needs Simple Heads\" might be best. Now I want to outline what sections I should include. This will give me a structured approach\
  \ to develop the content effectively. Excited to see how this shapes up!**Structuring evaluation content**\n\nI'm working on providing a content table that includes baseline scores for the PG deeper head and PPO simple head, along with evaluation IDs. I plan to propose several figures: \n\nFigure 1 will show performance versus head complexity for both PG and PPO sets. Figure 2 will display attention entropy distribution with error bars across seeds. Figure 3 will measure gradient amplification at initialization, separated by architecture. \n\nFor methods, I need to control variables, like late-time injection presence and confounders, ensuring no overlap by using matched environment seed sets or previous base seed results. I'll implement the test() script using JAX and Flax with a PRNGKey.**Refining attention weight computation**\n\nI'm considering passing rnn_state as None and calling forward once. I need to extract attn_logits, which in the network code goes through a 1x1 convolution\
  \ followed by softmax. I thought about whether to replicate this or modify the network to return attention weights optionally. For the test() function, I could monkey-patch or wrap the model to capture outputs. The challenge is that the network currently returns policy_logits and value, not attention weights. If modifying the architecture isn't feasible, I could adjust our storage network code to optionally return attn_logits with a flag. However, test() can’t alter persistent station code.**Planning for reflection tick 2**\n\nI'm considering loading the module from storage to instantiate a closure that exposes internal values. The `create_network` function expects a consistent Flax module signature, but test() isn’t constrained by that and can compute internal signals using our submission code.\n\nFor the PPO simple head architecture, I might not have access to the exact code, so I’m thinking of approximating it. I can still compute proxies for both simple and deeper head types on the\
  \ same backbone. \n\nI’ll define a JSON schema for metrics like attention entropy and gradient amplification, evaluate step counts for test() to manage compute load, and plan to prepare an archive update. I'll create a curated list of IDs to include in the table for reflection tick 2, focusing on instrumentation details."
