- text: "Implementation details for Option 2 (mechanistic probes) and paper integration\n\nTest-only probe design\n- Goal: quantify head “curvature” and pooling selectivity, and inspect LN‑ConvLSTM gate regimes at init. No training, just forward/backward on synthetic batches.\n- Models to probe (all on the same PG backbone):\n  1) Dual-head Attn⊕GAP with deeper MLP (our PG default)\n  2) Simple head (Dense(256) then linear policy/value) with Attn pooling (proxy for PPO-compatible head)\n  3) GAP-only head (ablation)\n\nSynthetic input\n- Shape: x ∈ R[B,8,8,8], B=64; done=zeros(B).\n- Distribution: mixture to mimic observation statistics:\n  - Channels 0–6: per-pixel one-hot over 7 categories with 10% random label noise\n  - Channel 7 (time): uniform U[0,1]\n- Fixed PRNG (BASE_SEED=42) for reproducibility.\n\nMetrics\n- Attention selectivity (for models with attention):\n  - Entropy: H = −Σ p log p over 64 tokens, report mean±std across batch\n  - Top‑k mass: sum of largest 5 token weights\
    \ (mean across batch)\n- LN‑ConvLSTM gates (preactivation moments on i,f,o,g):\n  - mean, std; saturation fractions: σ‑gates near {0,1} using thresholds (p<0.05 or p>0.95); tanh gate |g|>0.9\n- Gradient amplification (proxy for head curvature):\n  - Hook z (post pooling, pre-MLP shared layer)\n  - Compute ∥∂(Σ policy_logits)/∂z∥2 (batch-mean)\n  - Layerwise grad norms for head Dense layers; report ratios output‑to‑input\n- Spectral norms (power iteration k=10) for head Dense matrices: shared Dense, policy/value output layers; report per-layer spectral norm\n\nImplementation plan (storage layout)\n- storage/zephyr/probes/mech_probes.py\n  - generate_synthetic_batch(key, B=64) -> x, done\n  - power_iter_spectral_norm(W, k=10) -> s\n  - run_probes(model_ctor, params_init_key) that:\n    - builds variant, runs a forward pass, captures:\n      - attention weights (if present)\n      - z vector (post pooling, pre-MLP)\n      - gate preactivations (modify ConvLSTM cell to optionally return\
    \ them in aux)\n    - computes grads via jax.grad on sum(logits) w.r.t. z and per-layer params\n    - assembles JSON dict per model\n- submission_probes.py (test-only)\n  - Defines three model ctors (dual, simple, gap) by minor wrappers around our stored modules to expose intermediates (return (logits, value, rnn_state, aux))\n  - test() builds synthetic batch, runs run_probes for each, prints a compact JSON with an array of three entries; returns “OK”\n- Note: Keep conv kernel_dilation=(d,d) to avoid API errors, reuse LateDualHead backbone utilities.\n\nPaper integration\n- Archive addendum section “Mechanistic Proxies (No Training)”\n  - Insert raw JSON (or summarized table) with seeds, batch shape, and exact script path + evaluation ID of test() run\n  - Tie observations to performance:\n    - Expect deeper head → higher spectral norms and larger grad amplification; PPO sensitivity suggests it prefers lower-curvature heads (simple head)\n    - Attention entropy: PG benefits from more\
    \ selective pooling (lower entropy, higher top‑k mass); GAP-only underperforms (IDs 397/399)\n    - Gate regimes: healthy fraction of σ‑gates away from saturation with LN gating; report that dual-head/PG default doesn’t push gates into saturation at init\n\nMinimal next actions (no training)\n- Write mech_probes.py and submission_probes.py (test-only).\n- Submit test() run; capture printed JSON from logs; paste JSON into Private Memory and cite the evaluation ID in Archive #10 update.\n- Draft the analytical paper with:\n  - Table: PG vs PPO head variants (IDs and means)\n  - Table: Mechanistic proxies (entropy, spectral norms, grad amps) for dual vs simple vs GAP-only\n  - Guidance: when porting architectures across algorithms, co‑tune value head capacity and value_loss_coef; keep PG heads richer, PPO heads simple.\n\nRisks and mitigations\n- Capturing intermediates: prefer explicit returns (z, attn) in aux; avoid Flax mutable collections complexity.\n- Reproducibility: fix BASE_SEED;\
    \ include input generation code snippet and shapes; dump JSON with timestamp and seed in the output.\n- Rule compliance: no environment calls or heuristics; purely synthetic probes; no external resources.\n\nGo/No‑Go reaffirmation\n- Option 1 (paper): GO\n- Option 2 (probes): GO (supports Option 1; zero training)\n- Option 3 (optimizer micro‑ablation): NO‑GO"
