- text: "Operationalizing the plan: hypotheses ↔ proxies, acceptance criteria, and implementation guardrails\n\nHypotheses to test with proxies (no training)\n- H1: PPO + simple head succeeds because lower head curvature reduces gradient variance under the clipped objective and critic coupling.\n  - Proxies: smaller spectral norms in head layers; lower z→logits grad amplification; slightly higher attention entropy (less peaky) than deep dual-head.\n  - Expectation: simple head < dual-head on spectral norms and grad amplification; attention entropy modestly higher (but not GAP-high).\n- H2: PG benefits from richer readouts because selective spatial aggregation improves credit assignment under tight wall-clock.\n  - Proxies: dual-head shows lower attention entropy (more selective), higher top-5 mass, but remains within stable gate regimes (few saturated σ-gates; moderate tanh saturation).\n  - Expectation: dual-head has lower entropy and higher top-5 mass than simple head; GAP-only shows high\
    \ entropy and worst performance IDs (397/399), aligning with proxy weakness.\n\nAcceptance criteria for “GO publish”\n- Completeness: Every quantitative claim references an Evaluation ID (existing runs) or a test() Evaluation ID for proxies.\n- Coherence: Proxy differences align with performance ordering across IDs: dual-head PG > simple-head PG > GAP-only PG; PPO simple head > PPO deeper head attempts.\n- Parsimony: No new training runs; only test() and synthesis. Any new hypothesis speculation clearly labeled as such.\n- Reproducibility: Include exact script path(s), BASE_SEED list, and printed JSON blobs in Archive; readers can re-run test() to reproduce.\n\nImplementation guardrails\n- Keep models aligned: Use the same PG backbone for all three heads (dual Attn⊕GAP deep, simple head with Attn, GAP-only). Do not alter backbone or ConvLSTM features.\n- Avoid Flax mutation complexity: return aux dict with intermediates (attn_weights, z, gate preactivations) directly from the call; do\
    \ not rely on capture APIs.\n- Determinism: Fix PRNG (42); optionally also run seeds [7, 123] with B=32 to report mean±std across seeds while staying light.\n- Compute budget: No JIT compilation overhead beyond a single forward/backward per variant; a simple power iteration (k=10) per Dense layer is acceptable on CPU.\n\nPaper scaffolding updates\n- Add a “Mechanistic Proxies (No Training)” subsection with:\n  - A concise table: model variant vs attention entropy/top-5 mass, spectral norms, grad amplification, gate saturation fractions.\n  - A short paragraph connecting proxies to algorithm behavior (PPO clipping + critic coupling vs single-pass PG).\n  - A reproducibility box: script path, evaluation ID, seed(s), batch shape.\n\nMinimal task breakdown and timeline (1–2 ticks)\n- T1: Write storage/zephyr/probes/mech_probes.py and submission_probes.py with test() only; run once; capture JSON (Private Memory).\n- T2: Update Archive #10 with the proxies table, links to JSON snippets, and\
    \ the new test() evaluation ID.\n- T3: Draft the analytical synthesis (Option 1) pulling together ID-backed evidence + proxies; submit as a new Archive capsule or as an addendum update, depending on length.\n\nStop conditions\n- If proxies are inconclusive (e.g., simple vs dual-head show nearly identical spectral norms and grad amplification), still publish the synthesis framed as “negative mechanistic result,” emphasizing that performance patterns remain, but proxies did not clearly discriminate—valuable in itself.\n- Do not proceed to optimizer micro-ablations unless a concrete mechanistic discrepancy suggests they matter (current evidence says no)."
thinking_content: '**Mapping algorithmic behaviors**


  I’m considering what to include when analyzing the connection between proxies and algorithmic behaviors. For instance, using PPO, clipping can effectively reduce gradient magnitude, but deeper heads amplify gradients, potentially causing issues with stability. It seems that value function weighting affects head complexity, and attention plays a role in efficiently selecting key tokens while managing gradients. I’m thinking about proposing conceptual diagrams to illustrate curvature versus objectives and looking into data packaging for log retrieval, although the commit hash isn''t available.**Proposing test structure**


  I’m thinking about incorporating seeds for tests to enhance robustness. We can vary the random seeds, perhaps using 42, 7, and 123, while keeping the test function light. Evaluating three seeds will multiply our time, but it should still work fine. Earlier reflections mentioned using one synthetic batch, but including multiple seeds could show variance better. I’ll outline the paper structure too, covering sections like introduction, methods, results, and limitations, while making sure the test function is not used for heavy computations.**Gathering training traces**


  I’m considering how to gather training-time traces, but I can’t easily load training logs in the test function. Instead, I’ll access relevant evaluation logs through the review ID, but to keep token usage lower, I should summarize known results and reference those evaluation IDs. For the paper, including training-time curves printed in STDOUT would be beneficial, though I need to copy them in. I’m also thinking about updating Archive #10 or creating a new capsule and finalizing the Reflection Tick 4 content around implementation pitfalls and risk management.**Structuring the response**


  I want to keep my answer clear yet comprehensive. The default setting for "Desired oververbosity" is 3, but the reflection encourages a verbose approach. While reflecting, I can be more detailed, but I still need to stay within the token budget. I’ll use bullet points to organize my thoughts:


  - Hypotheses linked to measurable proxies.

  - Acceptance criteria for the paper: all claims connected to IDs, proxies printed, and supportive diagrams.

  - A minimal pseudocode skeleton for the test function.

  - Details on data storage, including storage paths and printing.


  Let’s craft the Tick 4 response!'
