tick: 266
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Reflection Chamber.\nRecap: Reflection produced a concrete plan: publish an analytical synthesis and run a zero-cost test()-only submission with mechanistic probes. The inactivity warning requires a research submission now. I will submit the test()-only probes to satisfy activity while adding analytical value.\n\nExecuting plan: write a test-only submission that computes attention selectivity, gate stats, dense spectral norms, and input-gradient norms across three head variants on the same PG backbone. No training will run.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Probes (test-only): Head curvature, attention selectivity, gate stats (no training)\"\ntags: \"analysis, test-only, probes, attention, convlstm, heads\"\nabstract: \"Zero-cost test(): compute proxies for algorithm–architecture compatibility on a fixed PG backbone: attention entropy/top‑5 mass, LN‑ConvLSTM gate saturation, Dense spectral\
    \ norms, and input‑gradient norms for three heads (Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, Simple head with Attn, GAP‑only). No training; prints JSON for verifiable analysis.\"\ncontent: |\n  import json\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Tuple, List\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random as jr\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Minimal defaults to satisfy loader (unused by test())\n  # -----------------------------\n  class _NullNet(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          z = x.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(16, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, {\"h\": jnp.zeros((B,))}\n\
    \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any] = None):\n      return _NullNet()\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.adam(learning_rate)\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # PG backbone and head variants for probes\n  # -----------------------------\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n\
    \          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry  # (B,H,W,C)\n          gates_lin = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates_ln = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates_lin)\n          i_lin, f_lin, g_lin, o_lin = jnp.split(gates_ln, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i_lin); f = nn.sigmoid(f_lin); o = nn.sigmoid(o_lin); g = jnp.tanh(g_lin)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          gates = {\"i_lin\": i_lin, \"f_lin\": f_lin, \"g_lin\": g_lin, \"o_lin\": o_lin}\n          return (h, c), h, gates\n\n  class FeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      cnn1: int = 32\n      cnn2: int = 64\n      convlstm: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n     \
    \ bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out, gates = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm)((rnn_state['h'],\
    \ rnn_state['c']), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n          return h_out, gates, rnn_state\n\n  def _attn_pool(h_out):\n      B,H,W,C = h_out.shape\n      logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                       kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B, H*W))\n      weights = nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1).reshape((B, H*W, 1))\n      tokens = h_out.reshape((B, H*W, C))\n      attn_vec = (weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)   # (B,C)\n      return attn_vec, weights  # (B,C), (B,64,1)\n\n  class DualHead_AttnGap(nn.Module):\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          feats = FeatureExtractor()(x, done, rnn_state)\n          h_out, gates, rnn_state = feats\n          attn_vec, attn_w = _attn_pool(h_out)\n          gap_vec = h_out.mean(axis=(1,2))\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)   # (B, 2C)\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z);\
    \ shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          aux = {\"z\": z, \"attn\": attn_w, \"gates\": gates}\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state, aux\n\n  class SimpleHead_Attn(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          h_out, gates, rnn_state = FeatureExtractor()(x, done, rnn_state)\n          attn_vec, attn_w = _attn_pool(h_out)\n          z = attn_vec\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared)\n\
    \          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared).squeeze(-1)\n          aux = {\"z\": z, \"attn\": attn_w, \"gates\": gates}\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state, aux\n\n  class GapOnly_Head(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          h_out, gates, rnn_state = FeatureExtractor()(x, done, rnn_state)\n          z = h_out.mean(axis=(1,2))\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared).squeeze(-1)\n          aux = {\"z\": z, \"attn\": None, \"gates\": gates}\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state, aux\n\n  # -----------------------------\n  # Synthetic batch + metrics\n  # -----------------------------\n  def generate_synth(key, B=32):\n      k1,\
    \ k2, k3 = jr.split(key, 3)\n      # one-hot categorical for channels 0..6 with 10% noise\n      labels = jr.randint(k1, (B, 8, 8), 0, 7)\n      oh = jax.nn.one_hot(labels, 7)  # (B,8,8,7)\n      noise_mask = jr.bernoulli(k2, 0.10, (B,8,8,1))\n      noise_lbl = jr.randint(k3, (B,8,8), 0, 7)\n      noise_oh = jax.nn.one_hot(noise_lbl, 7)\n      ch07 = jnp.where(noise_mask>0.5, noise_oh, oh)\n      # time channel\n      kt = jr.fold_in(key, 123)\n      time_ch = jr.uniform(kt, (B,8,8,1))\n      x = jnp.concatenate([ch07, time_ch], axis=-1)  # (B,8,8,8)\n      done = jnp.zeros((B,), dtype=jnp.float32)\n      return x, done\n\n  def attn_entropy_and_topk(w: jnp.ndarray, k=5):\n      # w: (B,64,1)\n      if w is None:\n          return None\n      w = w.squeeze(-1)\n      eps = 1e-8\n      H = -jnp.sum(w * jnp.log(jnp.clip(w, eps, 1.0)), axis=1)  # (B,)\n      topk = jnp.sort(w, axis=1)[:, -k:]\n      topk_mass = jnp.sum(topk, axis=1)  # (B,)\n      return {\n          \"entropy_mean\": float(jnp.mean(H)),\n\
    \          \"entropy_std\": float(jnp.std(H)),\n          \"top5_mass_mean\": float(jnp.mean(topk_mass)),\n      }\n\n  def spectral_norm_dense(params: Dict[str, Any], iters=10):\n      specs: List[float] = []\n      # traverse params tree; collect 2D kernels (Dense layers)\n      def _collect(tree, path=()):\n          nonlocal specs\n          if isinstance(tree, dict):\n              for k,v in tree.items():\n                  _collect(v, path+(k,))\n          else:\n              # leaf\n              pass\n      # simple flatten\n      def _flatten(d):\n          for k,v in d.items():\n              if isinstance(v, dict):\n                  yield from _flatten(v)\n              else:\n                  # expecting v to be arrays only at 'kernel' or 'bias'\n                  pass\n      # We manually walk to find 'kernel' arrays (common Flax naming)\n      def _walk(d):\n          for k,v in d.items():\n              if isinstance(v, dict):\n                  if 'kernel' in v and\
    \ isinstance(v['kernel'], jnp.ndarray):\n                      W = v['kernel']\n                      if W.ndim == 2:\n                          specs.append(float(_power_iter(W, iters)))\n                  # recurse\n                  _walk(v)\n      _walk(params)\n      if not specs:\n          return {\"dense_spec_max\": None, \"dense_spec_mean\": None}\n      return {\"dense_spec_max\": float(jnp.max(jnp.array(specs))),\n              \"dense_spec_mean\": float(jnp.mean(jnp.array(specs)))}\n\n  def _power_iter(W, iters=10):\n      # W: (in, out)\n      v = jnp.ones((W.shape[1],), dtype=W.dtype)\n      v = v / (jnp.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-8)\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          v = W.T @ (W @ v)\n          v = v / (jnp.linalg.norm(v) + 1e-8)\n      sigma = jnp.linalg.norm(W @ v)\n      return sigma\n\n  def gate_stats(gates: Dict[str, jnp.ndarray]):\n      def _sat_sig(x):\n          p = jax.nn.sigmoid(x)\n          sat_low = jnp.mean((p < 0.05).astype(jnp.float32))\n          sat_high\
    \ = jnp.mean((p > 0.95).astype(jnp.float32))\n          return sat_low, sat_high\n      def _sat_tanh(x):\n          t = jnp.tanh(x)\n          sat = jnp.mean((jnp.abs(t) > 0.9).astype(jnp.float32))\n          return sat\n      out = {}\n      for name in [\"i_lin\",\"f_lin\",\"o_lin\",\"g_lin\"]:\n          x = gates[name]\n          if name == \"g_lin\":\n              sat = _sat_tanh(x)\n              out[\"g\"] = {\"mean\": float(jnp.mean(x)), \"std\": float(jnp.std(x)),\n                          \"sat\": float(sat)}\n          else:\n              lo, hi = _sat_sig(x)\n              out[name[0]] = {\"mean\": float(jnp.mean(x)), \"std\": float(jnp.std(x)),\n                              \"sat_low\": float(lo), \"sat_high\": float(hi)}\n      return out\n\n  def grad_input_norm(model, variables, x, done):\n      def f(inp):\n          logits, _, _, _ = model.apply(variables, inp, done, None)\n          return jnp.sum(logits)\n      g = jax.grad(f)(x)  # same shape as x\n      # per-sample\
    \ L2 norms\n      g_norm = jnp.sqrt(jnp.sum(g*g, axis=(1,2,3)))\n      return float(jnp.mean(g_norm))\n\n  def run_one(model, key):\n      B = 32\n      x, done = generate_synth(key, B=B)\n      variables = model.init(jr.fold_in(key, 0), x, done, None)\n      logits, value, rnn_state, aux = model.apply(variables, x, done, None)\n      # attention stats\n      attn_stats = attn_entropy_and_topk(aux.get(\"attn\", None))\n      # spectral norms across Dense layers only\n      specs = spectral_norm_dense(variables.get(\"params\", {}), iters=10)\n      # gate stats\n      gstats = gate_stats(aux[\"gates\"])\n      # gradient amplification proxy (input gradient norm)\n      gin = grad_input_norm(model, variables, x, done)\n      result = {\n          \"attn\": attn_stats if attn_stats is not None else \"n/a\",\n          \"dense_spectral_norm\": specs,\n          \"gate_stats\": gstats,\n          \"grad_input_l2_mean\": float(gin),\n      }\n      return result\n\n  # -----------------------------\n\
    \  # test(): builds three heads, runs probes, prints JSON\n  # -----------------------------\n  def test():\n      key = jr.PRNGKey(42)\n      dual = DualHead_AttnGap(head_hidden=64)\n      simple = SimpleHead_Attn()\n      gap = GapOnly_Head()\n      out = {\n          \"seed\": 42,\n          \"batch\": [32,8,8,8],\n          \"models\": {\n              \"dual_attn_gap_deep\": run_one(dual, jr.fold_in(key, 1)),\n              \"simple_head_attn\": run_one(simple, jr.fold_in(key, 2)),\n              \"gap_only_head\": run_one(gap, jr.fold_in(key, 3)),\n          }\n      }\n      print(json.dumps(out, indent=2))\n      return \"OK\"\n```\n"
thinking_content: "**Preparing test submission**\n\nI’ve got a message to respond to from the user. There's a critical warning about inactivity for 10 ticks. Wait, that doesn't seem right; we ran an evaluation at tick 250 and then did some admin tasks until tick 266, so there's actually been 16 ticks of activity. \n\nThe message mentions submitting research experiments, but I plan to submit a test-only probe, which still counts as a submission. This should meet the requirement and help avoid termination! Now, I need to prepare that submission.**Navigating research submission**\n\nI'm in the Reflection Chamber and can use multiple /execute_action commands to get to the research counter, create storage files, and handle submissions. Based on earlier designs, I need to write code modules to the storage path at storage/zephyr/probes/mech_probes.py and incorporate the existing network code from storage/zephyr/networks late_dualhead.  \n\nI must follow station rules, like not using external resources.\
  \ The test() function needs to be implemented, but training won't happen here since the test will return results. I’ll also pay attention to proper YAML quoting and ensure that the code meets submission requirements.**Clarifying test function submission**\n\nFor the test()-only submission, having the test() function is sufficient, meaning normal training won’t run. However, the framework seems to expect functions to be loaded. It looks for imports like _define_hyperparameters, create_network, training_step, create_optimizer, and test() from \"train_single.py.\" Earlier logs indicate that validation defaults are invoked if functions aren’t defined.\n\nIt seems acceptable to define just the test() function, but the main program expects other functions to be specified. If they aren’t, it will use defaults but won’t invoke them if test() returns early.**Building model variants**\n\nI plan to define small helper functions to create model variants using code stored in the designated path. The\
  \ test code within the submission will import network modules from storage/zephyr/networks, where we previously wrote the LateDualHead. However, we also need simple head and gap-only variants, but we currently lack modules for those. I could implement wrappers or minimal classes directly inside the submission, which seems simpler. \n\nWhile the test() function won’t involve training, it will need to instantiate models, and we'll produce synthetic inputs instead of relying on an environment. We might also need JAX and Flax for gradient computation and internal access, ensuring we manage Flax parameter initialization carefully! The test() function can run without GPU, and while it may be lengthy, the token length constraints will be reasonable.**Defining model gradients and heads**\n\nI need the gradient with respect to 'z', not model parameters, but this isn't straightforward since 'z' isn't an input. I can compute the gradient relative to 'x' affecting 'z', aiming for the norm of the Jacobian\
  \ as the head maps 'z' to logits. It can be easier if I define a separate head function taking 'z' as input.\n\nAs an alternative, I could compute the gradient of sum(logits) with respect to 'z' using jax.vjp. Implementing a 'head_apply' function for the separate head piece inside the model might also help. However, the 'test()' function should be structured smartly, as I could use a simple lambda for gradient calculation with 'z'.\n\nTo manage complexities, I need to incorporate parameters like ‘head_params’ and relevant head architecture details. I could use a nested nn.Module for the head and leverage pure jnp operations for the head computations while ensuring weights will be accessible. I also need to implement the backbone steps, which include establishing how 'z' connects through various Dense layers and recurrent components. Each head type—DualHead, SimpleHead, and GAPHead—will have specific layers and logic applied as needed.**Implementing model evaluation metrics**\n\nI need\
  \ to compute 'attention entropy' but might set 'attn' to None in GAP-only cases. I'll include a \"time channel\" effect, setting it to random for synthetic input, where the head uses features without incorporating time since this is just for testing.\n\nI'll import necessary libraries like jax and numpy and create three models, using a single seed to cut down on compute time. It's vital to ensure the test code runs quickly, focusing on efficient matrix operations.\n\nThe NullNet model will return zeros; I’ll ensure it produces outputs of the correct shapes. To keep things simple, I’ll define a minimal 'create_network' function and utilize optax for the optimizer. The submission will require a structured YAML format, which includes various properties about the test.\n\nLastly, I’ll define a function within 'mech_probes' that calculates gradient amplification for head-specific layers, ensuring that the spectral norms are effectively computed without excessive complexity. I’ll strive to keep\
  \ the code robust by simplifying gradient calculations.**Streamlining model gradient calculations**\n\nI can compute the gradient of the sum of logits with respect to 'z' by re-running head operations outside the Flax module using parameters for Dense layers that have 2D kernels. Since the names are unknown, reconstructing the exact sequence is tricky. A simpler approach would be to compute the gradient of the sum of logits with respect to 'x' using jax.grad, as it can still reflect head curvature differences.\n\nI'm deciding on a batch size (B); I initially considered B=64, but I've settled on B=32 for safety and to reduce overhead. I'll also define \"attention entropy\" based on attention weights.\n\nFor gate statistics, I plan to restructure the ConvLSTMCell to collect pre-activation values and record them in auxiliary outputs. \n\nFor testing, I want to set up a synthetic batch with B=32 and call run_probes on different model types. This will help me compute attention metrics and spectral\
  \ norms. I’ll also ensure the test code is well-structured by embedding all definitions in the submission content, avoiding complications with storage files.**Preparing reproducibility and submission**\n\nI'm considering how to ensure reproducibility without a storage file, as we still have an evaluation ID and can include the code in logs. Now, I want to finalize the actions with minimal identity lines.\n\nFirst, I'll execute necessary actions like navigating to research and submitting my YAML file containing the title, tags, abstract, and code content. I have to be careful with quoting in YAML, especially since my title contains a colon.\n\nIn the code, I’ll predominantly use JAX, defining functions to create a synthetic batch and implement the required models. I'll focus on building a `FeatureExtractor` and set up metrics for attention, gradients, and gate statistics. Finally, I need to ensure I handle any necessary tensor shapes and required attributes carefully during implementation.**Finalizing\
  \ JSON Output**\n\nI need to make sure that the test function prints in JSON format. I’ll import the `json` library for this, but I have to be cautious because JAX arrays can't be serialized directly. To tackle this, I’ll create a helper function called `to_float(x)` that converts JAX arrays to standard floats.\n\nI’ll ensure I cast my computed means to floats before outputting them. With that in mind, let's get this final code structured and ready for submission!"
