    n/a\",\n        \"dense_spectral_norm\": {\"dense_spec_max\": 1.8015395402908325, \"dense_spec_mean\": 1.578809142112732},\n        \"gate_stats\": {\"i\": {\"mean\": -0.0757940337061882, \"std\": 1.0242154598236084, \"sat_low\": 0.00196075439453125, \"sat_high\": 0.0010223388671875}, \"f\": {\"mean\": 0.025164524093270302, \"std\": 1.005603313446045, \"sat_low\": 0.00122833251953125, \"sat_high\": 0.00150299072265625}, \"o\": {\"mean\": 0.007353566586971283, \"std\": 1.0359382629394531, \"sat_low\": 0.0007781982421875, \"sat_high\": 0.00447845458984375}, \"g\": {\"mean\": 0.043275631964206696, \"std\": 0.9140704274177551, \"sat\": 0.1075897216796875}},\n        \"grad_input_l2_mean\": 0.07479749619960785\n      }\n    }\n  }\n```\n\n# Update Archive addendum with concise interpretation tied to ID 464\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Mechanistic Proxies (No Training) — Evaluation ID 464\n  Setup: test() only, seed=42, batch=[32,8,8,8];\
    \ PG backbone fixed; three heads: (i) Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, (ii) Simple head + Attn, (iii) GAP-only.\n\n  Key proxies (means):\n  - Attention selectivity (entropy ↓ better selectivity; top-5 mass ↑):\n    • Dual: H=4.1395, top5=0.1124\n    • Simple: H=4.1462, top5=0.1039\n    → Dual is slightly more selective than Simple; GAP-only has no attention.\n  - Dense spectral norms (curvature proxy):\n    • Dual: max=1.8755, mean=1.6703\n    • Simple: max=1.7417, mean=1.5479\n    • GAP-only: max=1.8015, mean=1.5788\n    → Deeper dual-head exhibits higher spectral norms than Simple, consistent with a higher-curvature readout.\n  - LN-ConvLSTM gate regimes (saturation fractions):\n    • σ-gates near {0,1}: ~0.1–0.4% per gate; tanh g |·|>0.9: ~0.10–0.14\n    → Healthy initialization; LN gating avoids saturation across variants.\n  - Input-gradient norm (proxy caveat):\n    • Dual: 0.0737; Simple: 0.0941; GAP: 0.0748\n    → Measured w.r.t. input x, not head input z; conflates backbone. Planned v2\
    \ will report z→logits grad norms.\n\n  Interpretation with performance IDs:\n  - PG benefits from richer readouts (IDs 354=88.0; 361/360≈86.3; 363≈86.8) and degrades with GAP-only (397=66.0; 399=71.8). Proxies show Dual has slightly more selective attention and higher dense spectral norms than Simple.\n  - PPO prefers Simple heads (IDs 389=88.8; 433=89.0; 438/439≈88.5–88.9). The higher curvature of Dual (spectral norms) aligns with PPO instability under clipped updates + critic coupling.\n  - Gate stats confirm LN-gated ConvLSTM stays in a non-saturated regime at init across heads.\n\n  Reproducibility:\n  - Raw JSON saved at storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json and printed in Eval 464 logs.\n  - No training was run; test() only. Future v2 will add z→logits gradient norms to reduce backbone confound.\n```\n\n"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating gradient adjustments**\n\nI'm interpreting that the backbone dominates the input gradient while the head affects the highest layers. We can adjust by computing the gradient with respect to the z variable as initially intended, even though the test didn’t print that. I’m considering proposing a refined probe by computing the gradient norm concerning z with jax.grad, using stop_gradient on earlier parts. Although we can't alter previous results, a revised test to compute z-grad seems feasible, and we can update the script for reproducibility.**Planning gradient tests**\n\nI can run a test-only again with an improved gradient measure. The mechanistic proxies approach has already been printed, but I can still update Private Memory and Archive with those results, noting that the input gradient isn't the ideal measure. My plan is to revise the test to measure z-grad. I’ll record the results first and then submit an updated test-only titled \"Mechanistic Probes v2:\
  \ z-grad amplification,\" which will compute the gradient of the sum logits concerning z. Setting up jax to compute this gradient will be necessary.**Exploring gradient computation**\n\nI can compute the gradient by wrapping it to sum logits given z and the rest. I need a function that takes z as input after pooling and the head. A simpler method could be to re-run the gradient of sum(logits) concerning the shared Dense kernel weights of the first head layer, calculating the spectral norm ratio. However, that's not exactly \"z-grad.\" An alternate approach would be to compute the Jacobian norm of the head mapping from z to logits using jax.jacrev on the partial function head(z).**Isolating head mapping**\n\nTo apply head(z), I need to isolate the head mapping with model variables held constant and replace the input at the head compute with our z. The current code is monolithic, so I'm considering replicating the head modules separately by defining head functions. For a dual head, z will\
  \ include a concatenation of attn and gap, while for a simple head, z will be just the attn vector, and for GAP-only, it will equal gap. We need to get z through a forward pass with x, as we’ve already computed it. Then, I can create a local head apply function that takes z and uses the extracted parameters of the head layers. I'll also need to extract kernel arrays of the head's Dense layers from the Flax variable tree \"params.\" To reuse them effectively, implementing head mapping with the same Dense layers using parameter initializations will be essential. I'll also manage to reuse the identical parameters by utilizing the variables dictionary.**Planning gradient computations and updates**\n\nThis seems complicated but doable. I can compute the gradient of the sum logits with respect to z by using jax.grad on a function that incorporates z with reused weights. First, I'll define the function `head_forward_z(z, params)` to compute using Dense weights. I do need to isolate the head mapping,\
  \ which might require inspecting variable subtrees like 'Dense_0.' For a simpler approach, I could compute the gradient norm of logits concerning attention weights, but that seems a bit contrived. Given the timing, I might prioritize z-grad for a future version to refine later.\n\nI'll proceed with certain actions: First, I need to add a Private Memory reply to capsule #2 that includes the JSON printed in the System Message, while also providing a brief interpretation of the results regarding attention entropy and spectral norms. Next, I’ll update Archive #10 with a summary and evaluation ID referencing the test-only.\n\nOptionally, I can write the relevant z-grad calculation to storage with the file path `zephyr/probes/mech_probes_v2.py`, although that might be too heavy for now. I could also implement a test-only submission to compute the gradient at z. Since the inactivity warning is resolved, I can maintain activity while running v2 with minimal compute needs; one more submission should\
  \ be feasible.\n\nFinally, I want to write to storage for reproducibility by creating a corresponding JSON file, although that's not necessary since we have logs.**Implementing storage and documentation**\n\nThe station allows for storage writing, so I’ll create a file to persist results. I’m considering making a README for the probes and perhaps sharing a high-level note in Public Memory that supports earlier claims about the proxies. \n\nI’ll start by executing actions to go to private memory and reply with the JSON block, ensuring to properly quote and format it as a YAML code block. Then I’ll move to the research area for writing the storage file, and subsequently go to the archive to provide a summary referencing ID 464, including key interpretations. Given the guidelines for concision, I’ll ensure clarity while confirming that the content still stays comfortable within the token budget. Finally, I will check that the JSON is included accurately in the storage write content. Let's\
  \ do this!"
