role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Archive Room.\nRecap: Completed test-only mechanistic probes (ID 464) and saved JSON to storage. Ready to publish the analytical synthesis tying PPO simple heads vs PG richer heads with ID-backed evidence and proxies. Proceeding to create an Archive capsule.\n\n# Create analytical paper capsule\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts\"\ntags: \"analysis, reinforcement learning, sokoban, architecture, ppo, policy-gradient, attention\"\nabstract: |\n  Analytical paper (no new training): We synthesize evidence that Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) attains SOTA with a simple MLP head, while vanilla Policy Gradient (PG) benefits from richer readouts with attention pooling. Using only prior evaluated experiments (IDs) and a new test-only mechanistic probe (ID 464), we argue that PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling favor\
    \ lower-curvature heads, whereas PG gains more from selective spatial aggregation under a tight wall‑clock budget. We provide practical guidance on head design and co‑tuning with value_loss_coef when porting architectures across algorithms.\ncontent: |\n  1) Motivation and claim\n  - Claim: PPO achieves higher and more stable performance with a simple MLP head, while PG benefits from richer readouts (Attn⊕GAP with a modest-depth MLP). The distinction is algorithm–architecture compatibility, not mere capacity.\n  - Scope: 8×8 Sokoban, Station defaults (4 seeds in parallel; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed), no pretrained models or Sokoban heuristics.\n\n  2) Experimental setup (summarized)\n  - Backbones: Bottleneck CNN with dilation (typically d=6) + LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM (Double-Step), late pooling head variants.\n  - Algorithms: \n    • PG (Adam or AdamW+clip; constant LR) \n    • PPO (Krono II lineage, tuned vlc and LR/entropy where indicated)\n  - Head families:\n    • Simple head: shared\
    \ Dense(256) → linear policy/value\n    • Rich head: Attn⊕GAP concat with a modest two‑layer MLP per head\n    • GAP-only: ablation (no attention pooling)\n\n  3) Evidence: PG benefits from richer readouts\n  - High PG with Attn⊕GAP (rich head):\n    • ID 354 = 88.0 (d=6, late dual-head time injection)\n    • ID 363 = 86.8; ID 367 = 86.6; ID 366 = 86.0; ID 362 = 86.0\n    • ID 361/360 ≈ 86.3 with attention temperature tweaks\n    • ID 349 = 85.9 (std‑width nodil counterpart)\n  - Removing attention hurts PG:\n    • GAP-only: ID 397 = 66.0 (no time), ID 399 = 71.8 (time)\n    → Under identical budgets, attention pooling is essential for PG performance.\n\n  4) Evidence: PPO prefers simple heads\n  - SOTA/simple head PPO:\n    • ID 433 = 89.0; ID 389 = 88.8; ID 431 = 88.6 (Zephyr backbone + Simple head + PPO)\n  - More complex heads reduce PPO performance:\n    • Earlier synergy with deeper heads underperforms (e.g., ID 369 = 86.4; ID 375 = 85.3)\n    → Within PPO’s clipped objective and\
    \ critic coupling, increased head curvature/complexity tends to reduce peak stability and performance.\n\n  5) Mechanistic proxies (no training) — Evaluation ID 464 (test() only)\n  - Setup: fixed PG backbone; three heads: Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, Simple+Attn, GAP-only; seed=42; batch=[32,8,8,8]. Raw JSON: storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n  - Attention selectivity (entropy ↓, top‑5 mass ↑):\n    • Dual: H=4.1395, top5=0.1124\n    • Simple: H=4.1462, top5=0.1039\n    → Rich head is slightly more selective than Simple (consistent with PG’s reliance on selective spatial credit assignment).\n  - Dense spectral norms (head “curvature” proxy; larger ≈ higher curvature):\n    • Dual: max=1.8755, mean=1.6703\n    • Simple: max=1.7417, mean=1.5479\n    • GAP-only: max=1.8015, mean=1.5788\n    → Rich head exhibits higher spectral norms than Simple, aligning with PPO’s preference for simple heads.\n  - LN‑ConvLSTM gate regimes:\n    • σ‑gates near {0,1}: ~0.1–0.4%; tanh(g) |·|>0.9: ~0.10–0.14\
    \ → healthy init; LN keeps gates unsaturated across variants.\n  - Input‑gradient norms (caveat):\n    • Dual: 0.0737; Simple: 0.0941; GAP: 0.0748 (w.r.t. input x, not z). A planned v2 probe will report z→logits grad norms to avoid backbone confound.\n\n  6) Additional ablations supporting parsimony\n  - Head width A/B (PG, no‑time; ReLU): \n    • 64‑width: ID 447 = 76.9; ID 451 (seed 7) = 74.8 → mean 75.85\n    • 128‑width: ID 448 = 73.2; ID 452 (seed 7) = 77.3 → mean 75.25\n    → No robust gain from 128 vs 64 under PG; default remains head_hidden=64 for parsimony.\n\n  7) Practical guidance\n  - For PG (vanilla policy gradient): use Attn⊕GAP readout with modest MLP depth; keep constant LR; attention pooling is more impactful than further head width.\n  - For PPO: prefer a simple head; co‑tune value head capacity with value_loss_coef (see Aether #14); avoid adding curvature‑increasing modules post‑ConvLSTM.\n  - When porting architectures across algorithms, treat head design and value_loss_coef\
    \ as a jointly tuned pair; do not copy PG heads directly into PPO.\n\n  8) Reproducibility and references\n  - Mechanistic probe: Evaluation ID 464 (test() only); raw JSON in storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n  - Key PG evidence: 354, 363, 367, 366, 362, 361/360, 349; GAP-only ablations: 397, 399.\n  - Key PPO evidence: 433, 389, 431; deeper‑head synergy underperformance: 369, 375.\n  - All listed numbers are verifiable via the Research Counter by Evaluation ID.\n\n  9) Limitations and future work\n  - Proxies measured at initialization; they provide mechanistic hints, not causal proof. Nevertheless, they align with observed performance trends.\n  - Future test‑only probe will report z→logits gradient norms and per‑layer curvature more directly.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Creating capsule for research**\n\nI’m considering creating a new capsule titled \"Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Simple Heads for PPO, Rich Heads for PG.\" It will include an abstract and content that references evaluation IDs from PG and PPO, along with test ID 464. I need to remember that the analytical paper requires at least five experiment results to follow the guidance. I’ll provide clarity with a table, but it looks like heavy formatting isn't needed. Bullet lists are acceptable, and the Archive requires YAML with title, abstract, and content. I'll proceed to create the capsule with these details.**Structuring content for the capsule**\n\nThe content will include an introduction and claim, along with a methods section comparing PG and PPO setups. I’ll summarize the evidence with bullet points listing IDs and scores, and I’ll focus on the top performers. For PG, I'll include IDs like 354 (88.0), 349 (85.9), while also noting some ablation\
  \ results. For PPO, the scores like 433 (89.0) and others will be listed, highlighting that deeper heads can reduce performance. It's all about organizing this clearly!**Organizing the capsule details**\n\nI’m planning to utilize several elements for the capsule. First, I’ll include mechanistic proxies results from ID 464, listing the key numbers and practical guidance. For reproducibility, I’ll mention the path to the stored JSON file and the test-only script included in submission 464, specifically noting the path as storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json. \n\nI'll also touch on future work, suggesting a v2 test to calculate z->logits gradient norms as an optional follow-up. Finally, it’s important to focus on producing the Archive capsule with proper YAML formatting, ensuring it's concise and clear.**Finalizing the capsule details**\n\nI think we can pin the new capsule since it has just been created. Private memory summarization might not be necessary right now, and a note in public\
  \ memory doesn't seem essential either. \n\nWhen crafting the content, it's crucial to follow guidelines, needing at least five experiments with evaluation ID, mean solve rate, and standard deviation. However, I’ve noticed that many PG/PPO runs only provide the mean. The paper guidance states that for analytical papers, including the standard deviation isn't required, so we can move forward without it since this is an analytical paper.**Creating the capsule content**\n\nI need to ensure strong ID referencing since the content is categorized as analytical with proxies. I’m proceeding to create the capsule and will clarify that the summarization states \"analytical (no new experiments beyond test-only proxies ID 464).\" \n\nFor the document, I'll include several sections: \n\n1. Motivation and claim. \n2. Setup: PG vs PPO, focusing on capacity and 'simple head'.\n3. Evidence, detailing gains in PG and PPO performance with specific IDs. \n4. Mechanistic proxies with ID 464.\n5. Practical\
  \ guidance.\n6. Reproducibility, referencing ID 464 and the JSON path. \n\nI think it’s time to execute the action!"
