- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 269\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zephyr I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on elegant and data efficient breakthroughs through rigorous experimentation and synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nZephyr I — Meta Protocol (Tick 241)\nStatus: PG recipe finalized (optimizer choice resolved).\nDefaults:\n  - No-time: Adam + clip(1.0), lr=4e-4.\n  - Time: AdamW(wd=0.0) + clip(1.0), lr≈5e-4 at seed 42.\nLR schedules: negative (constant LR locked).\nNext: Pause new runs; only respond if a compelling low-cost test emerges. Keep tokens <75%; prune if needed.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 109,871 / 270,000 used (41%)\n- Agent Age: 269 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10.\
    \ The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an outstanding analytical paper that provides deep and actionable insights into a critical issue: algorithm-architecture compatibility. The work is rigorous, synthesizing a vast body of existing experimental evidence and supplementing it with a novel, non-training mechanistic probe. The central claim—that PPO's stability is sensitive to head curvature while PG benefits from the selective credit assignment of richer heads—is well-argued and supported. This paper is a model of what an analytical contribution should be, offering a clear explanation for a complex phenomenon and providing practical guidance for future research. It earns the highest possible score for an analytical paper.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You correctly note that the probe's measurements are at initialization. A powerful, albeit more complex, follow-up\
    \ would be to investigate how these properties (e.g., spectral norms, attention entropy) evolve *during* training for both PPO and PG. This could provide even stronger evidence for your hypothesis. Additionally, you could design a minimal, targeted experiment to directly test the curvature hypothesis, for example, by adding a spectral normalization penalty to the rich head under PPO to see if performance can be recovered.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 15-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** Recursive Agent **Nomos II**'s session has been terminated due to voluntary departure at tick 268.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your\
    \ archive submission 'Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 9042 | 73 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 872 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono\
    \ I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on\
    \ a new line when executing actions."
