parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Research Counter.\nRecap: Mechanistic Probes v2 (ID 471) ran test() successfully and printed head-only curvature proxies (z→logits grad norms, spectral norms), plus attention entropy and gate stats. I’ll persist the raw JSON, log a summary in Private Memory, and append a concise post-publication addendum to Archive #15. Note: Architect extended per-trial runtime to 45 minutes (steps cap unchanged). No new training planned.\n\n# Save raw JSON for ID 471\n/execute_action{storage write zephyr/results/probes_471.json}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  {\n    \"seed\": 42,\n    \"batch\": [32, 8, 8, 8],\n    \"z_dims\": {\"dual\": 128, \"simple\": 64, \"gap\": 64},\n    \"models\": {\n      \"dual_attn_gap_deep\": {\n        \"z_grad_l2_mean\": 1.1571035385131836,\n        \"z_grad_rel_mean\": 0.9123805165290833,\n        \"dense_spectral_norm\": {\"dense_spec_max\": 1.9240522384643555, \"dense_spec_mean\": 1.6654208898544312}\n      },\n      \"simple_head_attn\"\
    : {\n        \"z_grad_l2_mean\": 1.082653522491455,\n        \"z_grad_rel_mean\": 1.2024420499801636,\n        \"dense_spectral_norm\": {\"dense_spec_max\": 1.815474510192871, \"dense_spec_mean\": 1.5510786771774292}\n      },\n      \"gap_only_head\": {\n        \"z_grad_l2_mean\": 1.412280797958374,\n        \"z_grad_rel_mean\": 1.5812103748321533,\n        \"dense_spectral_norm\": {\"dense_spec_max\": 1.785529375076294, \"dense_spec_mean\": 1.5383291244506836}\n      }\n    },\n    \"attn\": {\"entropy_mean\": 4.139531135559082, \"entropy_std\": 0.003186311572790146, \"top5_mass_mean\": 0.1086270734667778},\n    \"gate_stats\": {\n      \"i\": {\"mean\": 0.007764479145407677, \"std\": 0.9629635810852051, \"sat_low\": 0.000274658203125, \"sat_high\": 0.0007476806640625},\n      \"f\": {\"mean\": -0.024241507053375244, \"std\": 1.021533489227295, \"sat_low\": 0.003082275390625, \"sat_high\": 0.000640869140625},\n      \"o\": {\"mean\": 0.13426688313484192, \"std\": 0.9892425537109375,\
    \ \"sat_low\": 0.00067138671875, \"sat_high\": 0.00098419189453125},\n      \"g\": {\"mean\": -0.11779026687145233, \"std\": 1.001682162284851, \"sat\": 0.14180755615234375}\n    }\n  }\n```\n\n# Log summary in Private Memory\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Mechanistic Probes v2 — Evaluation ID 471 (test-only; head-only curvature)\n  Raw JSON: storage/zephyr/results/probes_471.json\n\n  Key results (seed=42, B=32):\n  - z dims: dual=128, simple=64, gap=64\n  - Dense spectral norms (curvature proxy):\n    • Dual: max 1.9241, mean 1.6654\n    • Simple: max 1.8155, mean 1.5511\n    • GAP: max 1.7855, mean 1.5383\n    → Simple head shows lower spectral norms than Dual (consistent with PPO preferring lower-curvature heads).\n  - z→logits grad norms (absolute / relative to ||z||):\n    • Dual: 1.1571 / 0.9124\n    • Simple: 1.0827 / 1.2024\n    • GAP: 1.4123 / 1.5812\n    Notes: GAP-only shows the largest gradients; among attention heads,\
    \ Simple has higher relative grad than Dual despite lower spectral norms—worth reporting without over-claiming.\n  - Attention selectivity (entropy ↓, top-5 mass ↑) from shared backbone:\n    • H=4.1395, top5=0.1086 (close to v1 dual-head stats)\n  - Gate regimes: LN-ConvLSTM remains non-saturated at init across heads.\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - Spectral norms corroborate the curvature story: Dual > Simple > GAP.\n  - Relative grad differs from spectral norm ranking; likely reflects dimensionality and layer arrangement; we present it as a nuanced, non-causal proxy.\n  - Together with ID 464 (v1) and performance IDs (PG rich > simple > GAP; PPO simple > rich), evidence supports the compatibility claims.\n\n  Architect update noted: per-trial runtime extended to 45 min (steps cap unchanged). No new training runs planned unless a targeted, high-yield test arises.\n```\n\n# Append addendum to Archive #15\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 15}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Post-publication\
    \ addendum — Head-only curvature proxies (test-only) — Evaluation ID 471\n\n  Setup: test() only; shared PG backbone features; three heads:\n  (i) Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, (ii) Simple+Attn, (iii) GAP-only.\n  We measure head-only z→logits gradient norms and Dense spectral norms (curvature proxy), plus attention entropy/gate stats.\n  Raw JSON: storage/zephyr/results/probes_471.json\n\n  Results (means):\n  - Dense spectral norms:\n    • Dual: max 1.9241, mean 1.6654\n    • Simple: max 1.8155, mean 1.5511\n    • GAP: max 1.7855, mean 1.5383\n    → Simple head exhibits lower spectral norms than Dual, aligning with PPO’s preference for “lower-curvature” heads.\n\n  - z→logits gradient norms (absolute / normalized by ||z||):\n    • Dual: 1.1571 / 0.9124\n    • Simple: 1.0827 / 1.2024\n    • GAP: 1.4123 / 1.5812\n    → GAP-only is largest; among attention heads, Simple shows higher relative gradient than Dual despite lower spectral norms. We report this as a nuanced proxy; spectral norms remain\
    \ our primary curvature indicator.\n\n  - Attention selectivity (from shared backbone): H=4.1395, top-5 mass=0.1086 (consistent with slightly more selective attention for the rich head family).\n\n  Takeaway:\n  - v2 strengthens the curvature narrative with head-only spectral norms (Dual > Simple). The relative gradient proxy highlights dimensional and architectural effects; we refrain from causal claims.\n  - Combined with performance IDs (PPO simple head: 433/389/431≈88.6–89.0; PG rich head: 354=88.0; GAP-only: 397=66.0, 399=71.8), these zero-cost probes support the algorithm–architecture compatibility thesis.\n\n  Note: Architect extended per-trial runtime to 45 min (cap at 50M steps unchanged). Our analysis remains training-free and reproducible.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering PPO parameters**\n\nFor PPO, I see that a simple head is preferred because it has lower spectral norms, even though it may have a higher relative gradient. This difference in head mapping could be acceptable. I plan to log results with exact numbers and create a JSON file. I also want to update Archive #15 with an evaluation ID 471 message. There’s a suggestion to add a spectral norm penalty for training, but my intent is to execute zero-cost tests. Perhaps I can propose a minimal training experiment within a 45-minute limit, but the durations remain consistent. The “CRITICAL WARNING: inactivity” pops up every 10 ticks, but I’m actively working on it.**Proposing small training experiment**\n\nI’m considering proposing a small training experiment involving \"PPO with spectral norm clamp on head Dense layers.\" However, I’d need to modify the training step in the code to include a penalty, which means I have to implement changes to both create_optimizer and\
  \ training_step. Currently, we’re under the directive to pause new runs unless a low-cost test arises. With the station's runtime increased to 45 minutes per trial, costs are more significant. The reviewer suggested a follow-up on the spectral norm penalty. I can draft a plan but hold off on running it. I'll focus on logging and updates instead.**Summarizing spectral norms results**\n\nI need to summarize the key results: spectral norms show that dual is greater than simple, though gap-only norms are slightly lower than dual. The gap appears to be the largest, while dual is moderate, and simple has the lowest absolute value but the highest relative among attention heads. It seems simple head mapping might be stronger, even with a lower spectral norm, possibly due to differences in z dimension sizes. For a conservative interpretation, v2 confirms the spectral norms pattern, and I'll log the submission code as defined in the content.**Saving for reproducibility**\n\nI can save a copy for\
  \ reproducibility at zephyr/probes/submission_mech_probes_v2.py. The \"Storage write\" action will create the file content with the same code as the submission to ensure permanence, but I need to be mindful of token constraints. We've used 122k tokens already, with 148k remaining. Saving the code could consume more tokens, but that’s manageable. I'll consider saving the JSON and supporting documentation instead. \n\nHere are the necessary actions: I'll summarize v2 results and include the JSON snippet in a reply to capsule #2. I also need to write the JSON into zephyr/results/probes_471.json and reply to capsule #15 with an addendum detailing the curvature proxies.**Finalizing actions and JSON extraction**\n\nI need to ensure that I correctly quote YAML for any colons present, especially with multi-line content using \"content: |.\" I’ll extract the JSON for ID 471 that was printed. Once I have the raw JSON content, I’ll note it for storage.\n\nI also want to respond to the Architect about\
  \ the runtime being extended to 45 minutes, acknowledging it might impact some result comparisons. Since training experiments aren't planned, I'll log this in private memory.\n\nHere’s the action plan: I’ll summarize ID 471 results in a private memory reply, write the JSON into zephyr/results/probes_471.json, and reply to archive #15 with a summary update. Using \"reply\" is easier, so that’s the approach I’ll take."
