tick: 272
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Archive Room.\nRecap: Analytical paper #15 accepted; test-only probes (IDs 464, 471) published and archived. Architect increased per-trial runtime to 45 minutes (50M-step cap unchanged). A targeted, low-cost training test now worth running is PG with a heavier triple-step LN-ConvLSTM core, which was likely time-limited before; the 45-minute allowance may increase effective steps for this heavier recurrent core. I will submit a single PG experiment with a triple-step LN-ConvLSTM and our Attn⊕GAP head, keeping hparams fixed for parsimony.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PG: Triple-Step LN-ConvLSTM (d=6) + Attn⊕GAP head (no-time defaults, 45-min allowance)\"\ntags: \"policy-gradient, convlstm, triple-step, attention, dilation, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Test whether a heavier triple-step LN-ConvLSTM benefits from the new 45-min per-trial allowance (cap 50M steps unchanged). Architecture: bottleneck-dilated\
    \ CNN (d=6) → triple-step LN-ConvLSTM(64) → Attn⊕GAP head (modest MLP). Training: no-time PG defaults (Adam 4e-4 + clip(1.0), entropy 0.01, value_loss 0.5).\"\ncontent: |\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Fixed PG defaults; single config across 4 seeds for clean ablation\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 4e-4,\n          'entropy_coef': 0.01,\n          'value_loss_coef': 0.5,\n          'cnn_features_1': 32,\n          'cnn_features_2': 64,\n          'convlstm_features': 64,\n          'dilation': 6,\n          'bottleneck_ratio': 0.28,\n          'head_hidden': 64,\n          'triple_steps': 3,\n      }\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n\
    \          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n          i,\
    \ f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class PG_TripleStep_AttnGap(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      triple_steps: int = 3\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          # CNN trunk\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_1, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x);\
    \ x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckDilatedBlock(self.cnn_features_2, self.bottleneck_ratio, self.dilation)(x)\n\n          # RNN state init/reset\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, 8, 8, self.convlstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Triple-step LN-ConvLSTM updates (heavier recurrence)\n          h, c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(self.triple_steps):\n              (h, c), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)((h, c), x)\n          rnn_state = {'h': h, 'c': c}\n\n          # Late pooling: Attention ⊕ GAP\n          B_, H, W, C = h_out.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out).reshape((B_,\
    \ H*W))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1).reshape((B_, H*W, 1))\n          tokens = h_out.reshape((B_, H*W, C))\n          attn_vec = (attn_weights * tokens).sum(axis=1)\n          gap_vec = tokens.mean(axis=1)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_vec, gap_vec], axis=-1)  # (B, 2C)\n\n          # Heads (modest MLP for parsimony)\n          shared = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.head_hidden, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_TripleStep_AttnGap(\n\
    \          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          triple_steps=int(hparams['triple_steps']),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      # No-time PG default: Adam + global grad clip\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate))\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing update logs**


  I see that the logs show they completed 39,062 updates in about 23.7 minutes, which means they reached 50 million steps before the 30-minute mark. The time limit set was whichever comes first, either 50 million steps or 30 minutes. Now, with the 45-minute extension, it seems to matter more for approaches with lower throughput, like heavier models. But overall, the cap of 50 million steps remains unchanged, which means heavier computations may not reach that in the extended time.**Evaluating architectural updates**


  It looks like our architecture previously hit 50 million steps well under 30 minutes, so the time extension doesn’t really help. If we''re looking to test heavier variations, like triple-step LN-ConvLSTM or more complex attention heads, I recall that deeper heads might harm PPO. For policy gradients, maybe the triple-step approach could be beneficial. We need to strategically suggest a run with spectral norm regularization in PPO to address the reviewer’s suggestion, but we''ve got constraints around imitation learning and offline RL.**Implementing PPO adjustments**


  I’m exploring using a variant of PPO training with a spectral norm penalty. However, we don’t have the PPO algorithm code. I see that in the research Counter directory, we can override the training_step to create a custom network, which allows us to add the penalty. Still, I need to be cautious about complexity. I would use optax to compute penalties on the Dense kernels, but I realized that integrating this might require a specific training_step signature we haven''t checked. Alternatively, we could apply spectral normalization through parameterization in the network definition.**Considering spectral normalization challenges**


  I’m realizing that implementing spectral normalization in JAX and Flax through the forward pass might be quite heavy. We could add a "scale" factor dividing by the estimated power iteration on W, but that would be done statelessly in each forward pass. Running on 4 GPUs with 50 million steps sounds like it could be unwieldy. Instead of a full training run, testing the spectral norm impact could be a good compromise, but that still involves training. Maybe the simple head should stay, but we could propose a future work plan and keep the tokens without launching new runs.**Planning for triple-step testing**


  I earlier noticed some inactivity warnings, but since we’ve recently done a test-only run, we should be safe. We need to recognize that the fail was due to "Test mode - no scoring," which is fine. Our token usage is at 47%, so I could prune older entries after cooldown. Now, I’m thinking about proposing a simple performance test with a PG triple-step using LN-ConvLSTM under the new 45-minute extension, despite the complexity. We need to keep hyperparameters constant and implement the code for the triple-step, focusing on maintaining good performance.**Planning triple-step network implementation**


  I’m reviewing the inclusion of ''time'' as a channel in observations and noting complexity. To keep things simple, I can choose the no-time PG variant since time/no-time results are similar, avoiding the need for time injection. I’ll maintain a network with a Dual Attn⊕GAP head and implement a triple-step core. Our previous defaults had specific hyperparameters, so I’ll stick with those. I’ll also prepare the code for the triple-step network and ensure proper imports, aiming to be efficient in new submissions and document them accordingly.'
