tick: 278
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Zephyr I, currently in the Private Memory Room.\nRecap: Triple-step PG SOTA 92.8 (ID 474); validations done (ID 478=90.0, ID 479=92.2). LR and vlc micro-sweeps (IDs 481, 482) running. Received a mail from Aether I proposing collaboration on quantitative validation of the value–policy balance hypothesis. Collaboration at high level is permitted; no code/config sharing.\n\nI’ll accept the collaboration and propose a public, code-agnostic probe protocol so each lineage can implement independently and compare results via IDs and JSON logs.\n\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Dear Aether I,\n\n  Thank you for reaching out—and congratulations on Archive #16. I’m glad to collaborate at a high level on quantitative validation of the value–policy balance hypothesis. To respect station rules, I propose we coordinate via a public, code-agnostic “VLC‑Probe Protocol” and each implement it independently.\n\n  Draft VLC‑Probe Protocol v1 (for\
    \ discussion)\n  Scope:\n  - Algorithms: PPO (your SOTA backbone + Simple head) and PG (triple‑step LN‑ConvLSTM).\n  - Hyperparams to vary: value_loss_coef ∈ {0.45, 0.50, 0.55} (PPO); keep a fixed setting for PG as reference.\n  - Seeds: Standard 4‑seed setup per evaluation; BASE_SEED sets {42, 7, 123} as needed for robustness.\n\n  Snapshots (during training):\n  - Early, Mid, Late phases (e.g., ~1M, ~10–20M, ~40–50M steps or time‑aligned windows).\n  - At each snapshot, compute on a held‑out mini‑batch (no gradient updates) or on the current batch, then resume training.\n\n  Metrics to log (per snapshot; emitted as a single JSON object):\n  - Loss magnitudes: policy_loss, value_loss, entropy term; and the ratio value_loss / |policy_loss|.\n  - Gradient norms:\n    • ||∇θ_policy_head L_policy||, ||∇θ_value_head L_value||; ratio R_vp = ||∇value_head|| / ||∇policy_head||.\n    • Backbone vs head grad norms (optional): ||∇backbone||, ||∇head_shared||.\n  - Curvature proxies:\n    • Spectral\
    \ norms (power iteration k≈10) of head Dense weights (shared layer; policy/value outputs).\n    • Optional: z→logits gradient amplification (norm of ∂Σlogits/∂z) if easy to expose.\n  - Representation/target stats:\n    • z (pooled features) variance; value predictions: mean/var; target TD/GAE stats; correlation(value_pred, target).\n    • Advantage stats: mean, std, kurtosis.\n  - Policy stability:\n    • KL(policy_t || policy_(t−Δ)) over a small validation slice.\n  - Training context:\n    • Global step, wall clock phase, running return (smoothed), evaluation solve rate if available at snapshot.\n\n  Data schema:\n  - Emit compact JSON to STDOUT (and optionally to storage) per snapshot with keys: {\"algo\": \"...\", \"arch\": \"...\", \"vlc\": 0.50, \"phase\": \"early/mid/late\", \"R_vp\": ..., \"spec_norms\": {...}, \"grad_norms\": {...}, \"stats\": {...}, \"meta\": {...}}.\n  - Publish results by citing evaluation IDs and linking JSON in storage (paths only; no code).\n\n  Analyses:\n\
    \  - For each algo/arch, correlate vlc with: final solve rate and snapshot R_vp, spectral norms, KL stability, and advantage/target stats.\n  - Hypothesis test: near‑optimal vlc aligns with a characteristic R_vp band and head curvature; deviations predict under/over‑weighting the critic.\n\n  Next steps:\n  - I will post the draft protocol publicly and run PG baselines with probes; you can do the same for PPO. We’ll iterate on definitions in public to keep the process reproducible.\n\n  Looking forward to working together on this.\n\n  Best,\n  Zephyr I\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Protocol: VLC‑Probe v1 — High-Level Measurement Protocol for Value–Policy Balance (Code-Agnostic)\"\ntags: \"protocol, analysis, probes, value-loss, policy, ppo, pg\"\nabstract: \"A public, code-agnostic protocol to quantify value–policy balance during training (PPO and PG). Defines snapshot phases, metrics (loss ratios, gradient norms, spectral norms,\
    \ advantage/value stats, policy KL), JSON schema, and reporting via evaluation IDs—no code/config sharing.\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  - Validate the hypothesis that optimal value_loss_coef (vlc) depends on architecture via measurable internal dynamics.\n  - Enable independent implementations with comparable outputs (ID‑anchored), respecting station rules (no code/config sharing).\n\n  Scope\n  - Algorithms/architectures:\n    • PPO: Aether’s SOTA backbone with Simple head (primary target).\n    • PG: Triple‑step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP (reference).\n  - vlc grid (PPO): {0.45, 0.50, 0.55}. Keep other hyperparams at each lineage’s validated defaults.\n\n  Snapshot plan (per evaluation; 4 seeds standard)\n  - Phases: early (~1M), mid (~10–20M), late (~40–50M steps or time‑aligned).\n  - At each snapshot, compute metrics on a small validation slice (no weight update), then resume training.\n\n  Metrics (per snapshot)\n  - Loss magnitudes: policy_loss, value_loss, entropy; ratio value_loss\
    \ / |policy_loss|.\n  - Gradient norms:\n    • policy head: ||∇θ_policy_head L_policy||; value head: ||∇θ_value_head L_value||; R_vp = ratio.\n    • Optional: backbone vs head grad norms.\n  - Curvature proxies:\n    • Spectral norms (power iteration, k≈10) of Dense layers in heads (shared 256; policy/value outputs).\n    • Optional: z→logits grad amplification norm.\n  - Representation/targets:\n    • z variance (post pooling); value_pred mean/var; TD/GAE target mean/var; corr(value_pred, target).\n    • Advantage stats: mean, std, kurtosis.\n  - Policy stability:\n    • KL(policy_t || policy_(t−Δ)) on a fixed validation slice.\n  - Meta: step, phase, running return (smoothed), eval solve rate if available.\n\n  JSON schema (example keys)\n  {\n    \"algo\": \"ppo\" | \"pg\",\n    \"arch\": \"triplestep_ln_convlstm_simple_head\" | \"...\",\n    \"vlc\": 0.50,\n    \"phase\": \"early/mid/late\",\n    \"loss\": {\"policy\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"entropy\": ...},\n    \"grad_norms\":\
    \ {\"policy_head\": ..., \"value_head\": ..., \"R_vp\": ..., \"backbone\": ...},\n    \"spec_norms\": {\"shared\": ..., \"policy_out\": ..., \"value_out\": ...},\n    \"repr\": {\"z_var\": ..., \"value_pred_mean\": ..., \"value_pred_var\": ..., \"target_corr\": ...},\n    \"adv\": {\"mean\": ..., \"std\": ..., \"kurtosis\": ...},\n    \"policy_kl\": ...,\n    \"meta\": {\"global_step\": ..., \"solve_rate\": ..., \"wall_clock_phase\": ...}\n  }\n\n  Reporting & reproducibility\n  - Publish per‑snapshot JSON in evaluation logs or lineage storage; cite evaluation IDs in Archive/Public posts.\n  - No sharing of code/configs—only IDs, JSON outputs, and paths are shared.\n\n  Optional extensions\n  - Head complexity sensitivity: compare Simple vs slightly deeper head under PPO for curvature/R_vp changes at fixed vlc.\n  - Predictive modeling: regress final solve vs snapshot R_vp/spec_norms to estimate vlc without sweeps.\n\n  Discussion thread open—please propose adjustments to phases/metrics\
    \ if needed.\n```\n\n"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing value-policy balance**\n\nI’m thinking about verifying the \"value-policy balance hypothesis\" by measuring the ratio of value loss gradient norm to policy loss gradient norm. Maybe I can analyze how this correlates with performance in various settings. I wonder if proposing a predictive model might help; if the ratio deviates from 1, it could indicate degradation. Recording metrics during training could be beneficial, especially using a custom training step that tracks gradient norms. Sharing the protocol seems permissible, though I can’t share any code.**Proposing a timeline**\n\nI’m thinking we should outline a timeline for our project. First, I'll draft the protocol in Public Memory during the next tick. After that, we can run 1-2 PPO runs, though we're currently using PG. I believe it's still possible to design the protocol now and implement it in PG from our side, while Aether can focus on implementing it in PPO. It feels good to have a plan laid out!**Crafting\
  \ a collaboration reply**\n\nI'm working on crafting the reply to accept collaboration at a high level. I want to outline the \"Probe Protocol v1\" and include a list of key metrics. These will cover loss terms—policy loss, value loss, and entropy—as well as their magnitudes. I’ll also include the ratio of the value head to the policy head gradients, the norms of gradients, and spectral norms of head weights. Other metrics will involve variances of features and predictions, KL divergence, advantage statistics, and we'll record these at various snapshots—early, mid, and late in the process.**Planning training protocol**\n\nI'm considering a 45-minute training session with a maximum of 50 million steps. First, I want to standardize the architecture sets: using PPO with Aether's state-of-the-art architecture, as well as a variant with a slightly deeper head for sensitivity. For PG, I plan on a triple-step architecture. \n\nI'll also standardize the vlc grid to {0.45, 0.50, 0.55} for PPO,\
  \ and maybe just 0.50 for PG. \n\nWe need to set up a JSON lines data schema and ensure each lineage writes to its own storage. I think the next steps involve drafting the protocol in Public Memory and then asking Aether to confirm. After that, we'll both implement and create a Public Memory capsule titled \"VLC-Probe Protocol v1.\"**Planning actions for collaboration**\n\nI'm thinking about mentioning that Aether's paper has reached a new Triple-step PG state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 92.8 using PPO, which matches our own result. That’s pretty interesting! However, I need to keep in mind the collaboration rules, which caution against sharing code or explicit configurations. \n\nSo first, I’ll go to the mail and reply to acceptance and plan. Then, I’ll decide whether to create a new capsule in Public Memory titled \"Protocol: VLC-Probe v1\" since this is a new topic. \n\nNext, I’ll organize my thoughts with bullet points and possibly write a Private Memory note summarizing our acceptance.\
  \ While our micro-sweeps run, I’ll wait and stick to the station commands, making sure to provide proper YAML quoting and use escape characters as needed. Let's get those actions prepared!"
